        <p>PLEASANT PA lt AG R ATIIS,</p>
        <p>IT takes about four years to learn to speak, and the other three-dcore-antl-six to learn not to.</p>
        <p>SPEAKING Of magic, there are demagogues who can make a mountain out of a mole-hill, and then make a living out of the mountain. RECEIVED TuFu QUIETUS.-" Ills Henpeck any settled opinious ? " yes. His Wile settles them 1"</p>
        <p>"He says he intends the world shall hear from him some day." Indeed !" "O, yes. He's learning to play the trombone." BELLA (000t1)) : "I've only seen twenty summers. Her Dear Frieod sarea,,tically : Really. Why don't you consult an 00.11,P ? REGGY "Aw—M.•s yoush always in my mind, ountcher know." .Nliss Grace: "Goodness ! that is worse than living in a Hit." _</p>
        <p>TIIE JOYS OF HUNTING —Tile guide: "Now, you sit tight here, don't move, and watch for the deer through that, opening." Amateur Sportsman: " When do you think üb'il come along?" The Guide : "Oa, some time this week." MR. PECS : "I had a funny dream last night. It seemed that I was away in South Africa, where diamonds were lying all a:onod me in neaps." Mrs. Peck; "Did you seem to see any as small as the one in the engageinent ring you gsva me ?" ' - • - - -</p>
        <p>"Gustica is no,; very happy in his choice nf adjectives." "Wty so?" " Miss Gumms fished for a compliment y asking what he thought of her slippers." "Anil whab did he say?" he said they were immense." "Tut," said the cockney who was showing some country friends the sights of London, "is the square that the battle tf Tratalgar was named ax ter." _ _</p>
        <p>THE DEAR CHILD : " MrP. Brown, when did you get back ?'' Mni Brown "These you, dear, I waa not away any wnere. Wnat made you think so?" The Dear Caild : "I heard mamma say that you were at LeGerheade with your husband for over a week." _ _</p>
        <p>CECNIN : "I say, old fellow, lend me £5." Brooks: "I will, when I come back from New York." C:onin : "Didn't know you were going to America." Brooks : "I'm not." _</p>
        <p>MRS. SWEKT (who has her neighbour's children in to spend the day) : "And so your mother is all alone? Dear me, how she must be enjoying the quiet I -I will send my children over there to- morrow ! "</p>
        <p>His BOAST —" D3n't you think Professor Bighead is a weli-mformed man?" asked his friend. "Bah !" said the, Professor of Mnemonics, "I've forgotten more than he ever knew!" _</p>
        <p>EXCITED SPoRT33I.IN TO FARMER, : "Say, did you see a bird tall anywhere about here? I shot at one just now and saw the feathers fly. Farmer: "No, I ain't seen nothin' of it. I guess when the feathers flew the bird flew with 'em.</p>
        <p>QUITE REASONABLE.—" Well, Mollie'S off for holiday but she's Dten very reasonable about it.: " Indeed ?" "Yes. All she asks of her husband is that he send her his salary every week." "CLAUDIA, is the young man industrious to whom you are engaged?" industrious? Why, papa, he intended to propose to me a month ago, but was too busy." HE: "D 3 you know that son The Cows Are In the Corn'?" She: "Yes, I have heard it." "Well, there's a sequel to it. Have you heard that?" "No, I have not. What is it ? ' " The Corn Is In the Cows.'"</p>
        <p>THE man who says what he thinks has the fur of speaking his mind, but that is about all he gets out of this I:fe. It does not go a great way toward filling the stomach or the pocket. MOTH eR.: "Dsar me, you've got your clothes all covered with whitewash." Little Son: "No, mother; it isn't whitewash; 'deed it isn't. It's only paint." "I ALWAYS like to write with a soft lead pencil," remarked tne young man. "Have you ever studied," inquired the cruelly thoughtless girl, the manner in which a person's character will manifest itself in his handwriting ? " HAnDACRE : "You say that Whipple's horse jumped that ditch? That was a wonderful jump." •` Yes ; but it was nothing to the one Whipple made. He landed at least bwenty feet further on than the horse did."</p>
        <p>NO COMPARISON.—" I suppose," said Uncle Jerry Peebles, "the hottest place on earth pi the stokehole of an iron battleship in action." "There is one hotter," remarked Uncle Allen Sparks. "It's the place where a young husband sits when he carves his first turkey for company." "THIS room is very close," remarked the guest to the head waiter; "can I have a little fresh air?" The well-drilled automaton raised his voice to a high-pitch " Oae air I " he yelled; after a pause adding, "and let it ba fresh!" "DON'T you want to leave footprints on the sands of time ? " asked the mentor. "No," answered the young man, who is ambitious, but lazy. "I'd rather leave carriage-ruts." LOVING MOTHER.: "I cannot understand what makes our boy R ibert so foad of pedestrianism." Fond Father; "He gets that from me. Didn't I walk the floor with him for weeks when he was a baby?" "WHAT'S the matter, old man?" "Oh, I've just had a quarrel with my wife." " Well, forget and forgive." "I never can forgive her; you see, I was in the wrong." "Then in that case demand sflanolnav ."</p>
        <p>BRASS BAND CONTEST AT RUNCORN. 'The first annual quartet contest in connection with St. Edward's Prize Band, Runcorn, was held in the Bethesda Lecture Hall on Saturday. The entries,, ten in number, were :—Earlestown Viaduct, Pemberton Old, Widnes St. Mary's, Warrington Boro', Bolton Boro' Ravenhead Silver, Wingates Temperance No. I set, Wingates Temperance No. 2 set, Weston Point Salt Works, and Salford Iron Works. The last-named party failed to turn up and after some very pretty and close playing the judge awarded the prizes as follows :-1, 30s. and four gold centre medals, Wingates Temperance No. 2 set; 3, 205., Wingates Temperance No, I set; 3, 155., Warrington Borough; and 4,10 s., Pemberton Old. The judge was Mr. Joe Jessop, of Manchester. The Hon. Secre- tary, in thanking the players and audience,took occasion to assure them that although their first venture in this line, had been accorded such scant support, it was intended to try again earlier in the contesting season. It is to be regretted that in an essentially musical town like Runcorn a struggling organization like the band in question are not better appreciated, and we sincerely hope that any future effort on their part will meet with the hearty support of all lovers of music.</p>
      </div>
