        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Royal circle, at the first Court of the season, was particularly small, and the number of the debutantes almost reached vanishing point. The Royal bride, Princess Alexander of Teck, contrary to expectation did not attend the Court, neither did her cousin the Princess Patricia of Connaught. In her vivacious laughter-loving way, Princess Patricia much resemble s Princess Charles of Denmark who in her debutante days was the life of all State functions. Although mourning for the late Duke of Cambridge was not commanded at the Court, colours were little worn, the Royalties and their suites being in sable attire. In the matter of jewels, Lady Ludlow was again the most envied woman there, her emeralds and diamonds—a gift from her son Lord Howard de Walden—being magnificent in the extreme. Two Noble Women.—Next to Queen Victoria,the two most widely respected women in the last reign were the Baroness Burdett-Coutts, and Miss Florence Night- ingale, both of whom are still happily living. The Baroness only recently celebrated her ninetieth birth- day, and is in such good health that she was present a few weeks previously at one of the London hospitals. For her unexampled philanthropic activities, she re- ceived signal honours from her country, and her Sovereign, being made a peeress in 1871, and the next year was presented with the freedom of the City of London. Miss Florence Nightingale was never made a ' peeress, but the memory of the noble work she initiated in the Crimea nearly fifty years ago, remains as green as ever. She is now eighty-three years of age, and though seven years younger is not in such good health as the Baroness Burdett-Coutts, who still takes an active interest in her many good works and benefactions. After Six Years in India.—Lady Curzon will spend a good part of the summer at Weimer Castle with her children. It is six years since her husband went to India, nearly the whole of which time Lady Curzon has spent in the trying climate of that country. She there- fore kopes to benefit from the bracing air of the East Coast, and few more delightful seaside residences could be imagined than Weimer Castle on the Kentish Coast between Deal and Dover. Lord Curzon is shortly returning home' and his appointment to the Lord Wardenship of the Cinque Ports suggests that he will not go back to India. Lord and Lady Curzon have now three daughters,the two elder ones being eight and five, wl.o bear the pretty names of Irene, aneCynthia, and in, all probability the Queen will stand as godmother to their little baby girl. Women and Politics.—Australia is about to entrust its executive Government to the Labour Party, and the older nations will watch the experiment with interest. It will put to the test many political and economic theories, but one of the chief factors inthis situation is that it was the votes of the newly enfranchised working women of Australia which chiefly contributed to the success of the Labour Party at the recent Common- wealth General Election. It is thought that if women had the Parliamentary vote in this country, the bulk of their influence would be thro.vn on the Conservative side, but in Australia, the opposite result is the case. The working class women have organised themselves into political bodies, whilst those of the higher social grades have abstained. Nearly all the old-established families in Australia are opposed to a proletariat govern- ment, but whatever such politics may mean to the Commonwealth, it is largely the women voters who have given to them this striking ascendancy. Shorter Church Services.—" How shall we spend our Sundays" is a question which the Archbishop of Canter- bury recently said each must decide for oneself. As a nation we seem to be giving up Sunday rest for Sunday gaiety, and in the matter of Sabbath observance, we appear to have gone from one extreme to the other. Formerly it was a day of idleness and gloom, now it is a day of social entertainment and holiday making. One cause which has undoubtedly contributed to this result is the undue length of the long church or chapel services. There are still many who prefer the old- fashioned long service. but the great majority of people have revolted in favour of spending a greater part of the day out of doors. One of the best inducements for the rational Sunday is a brief and bright service which , would attract many who shun the undue long enforced attendance. The Usual Fate of Dress Reformers.—The trousseau of the Hon. Angelaplaxwell, now Mrs. Eric Drummond, like that of her sister the Duchess of Norfolk. was very complete and beautiful. One wonders in reading of all the costly and elaborate gowns provided for the daughters of Lord and Lady Herries, whether they remember that 1 their grandmother once started a crusade to encourage . simplicity of dress for women. Yet that was only thirty years ago when Marcia Lady Herries, went in all seriousness to Rome to obtain the Pope's blessing on her mission. Se did not anticipate any early success, , as, though she obtained the blessing, she thought it I would be some time before her cause would triumph, as jit surely would in the second or third generation. The I weddings at Everingham suggesthowever that the cause, • ; as yet, has made little progress even under the parental I roof, and once again it is shown how hopeless it is to ' try and eradicate the feminine love of finery. A Comfortable Mode.--The tea gown has long been permissible for dinner wear, but it is now promoted one I step higher, and is allowed to appear at the theatre. This garment is built- to show the figure of the wearer, and is as perfectly fitted as the most elaborate ball gown. But it possesses many advantages. In the first instance it is much easier to get into than a bodice, skirt and waistband, again it invariably' fastens up the front or at the side, in a simple manner, and yet again 'there is a delicious feeling of comfort about a tea Igown, never to be found in the most middle aged, roomy dress. The tendency to include two or more fabrics, and colours in the creation of the tea gown, is still in evidence, many delightful garments of this description being carried out in voile of some delicate shade over silk of a different hue. For instance, cloud grey voile over rose pink silk, or pale blue voile over daffodil silk, give the most exquisite results. But when chiffon is the fabric chosen, many layers—all different colours— are built up, which give lovely transitory effects of shading with the movements of the wearer. All silks used for lining are of the soft non-rustling persuasion, a gentle unobstrusive swish swish being all that is heard when women of high breeding move. Matching the Colour of the Eyes.—Whenever it is possible to repeat in the dress and headgear, the precise colour of the eyes, this should be done. Of course numbers of women possess eyes of an indefinite shade, that could not, with advantage be copied in the toilette, but all decided shades of blue, brown and grey, should be matched, and find someprominent place in the costume. The effect this little scheme invariably has is little short of wonderful, even the plainest girl being i rendered interesting thereby. ' Fashions in Veils.—The new, huge veils which take , 1 the form of a cape, and fall from the brim of a hat or ' toque in abundant folds, almost to the waist. are never likely to become "popular," but on a tall, slender, graceful girl, they are old-world looking and elegant to a degree. For women less endowed by nature, and who move in circles where an accessory of this description would prove conspicuous, there are however the More medium veils, with the hem prettily finished with a pattern in chenille. When drawn up at the back, this border drapes the neck, and affords a soft becoming finish.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>r i OCEA FOR BRONCHITIS USE . EMPROCATION</head>
        <p>r i OCEA FOR BRONCHITIS USE . EMPROCATION</p>
        <p>Cr7l/flght•</p>
        <p>"IT TOUCHES THE SPOT."</p>
        <p>NG FLOUR The Examiner Saturday, April 3071904.</p>
        <p>end the birds," and Val Torr laughed heartily. "Well, good evening again, Woods." "I don't care overmuch for the new squire," old Woods said to himself, as he made his way to the Torr Arms. "He'll never be liked as the old squire was. Well, 1 aon't suppose we-it see mucn of him."</p>
        <p>Val Tarr watched the old man turn into the village inn; then, with a laugh, he hastened through the park and across the fields to Stocklands Farm—the home of Richard Woods.</p>
        <p>He would not go straight to the house and ask for Mattie ; he would walk round about and into the orchard—in all probability she was out of doors this tine evening. The yard was deserted; all work was over and done for that day. His footsteps roused the dogs and set them barking; still, not a soul came out of the house or from any of the out-buildings to inquire who was the intruder. It was fortunate for him that no one was about. He did not wish to fill the mouths of old Woods's servants, nor did he care to rome the anger of old Woods hitn- self. He liked to amuse himself with handsome Mettle; neverthelesF, she was not worth any unpleasantness with a good tenant. He was a good tenant. Dobson, the steward, had told him thot there was not another farm on the estate in such splendid condition as Stot klands. Yes, he must be.careful, he told himself, she opened the gate of the orchard. Mattie was ertain!y not worth a village scandal or the loss of a ood tenant. There was a bower, all honepockle and roses, at the end of the orchard. She might be there. Yes, his conjecture was right. He saw the white skirts of someone through the foliage. They must belong to her. He made no sound 2s he walked over the grass. He would surprise her. Would she be delighted to see him there ? Of course she would, though she would not say it, and might not shew it. She was in love with bim already. Thus he thought, and smiled. Ile stood at tho entreu OT of the bower. _ _ _</p>
        <p>MaLiie sprang to her feet, and the book she had been pert:sing fell to the ground. "This is an intrusion, sir! What do you mean by stealing ot tue in this manner?" Was this Mattie, the dairymaid--this befrilleci and belaced young lady, with 'trailing skirts and fashionably-dressed' hair ? He stood silent and regarded her with astonishment. Then, suddenly changing her tone, she made a curtsey and said: "Oh, sir, excuse my speaking so rough, but I'm so afraid someone'll see you here; and grandfather'd be awful angry with me. Do go away, sir, please." "My dear Mattie, now I know you," Val Torr rejoined, taking her hands in his. How soft they were fora dairymaid's! She drew them from him. "For a moment I thought I had stumbled across one of my own women—a lady of fashion. What a relief it is to find that all this white finery clothes my unsophisticated Mattie." "This is my ffunday frock, sir, and grandfather likes to see me in it; that's how .0 came to put it on. Oh, don't I wish I was a lady with plenty of money and lots of fine dresses! But, there! grandfather says I mustn't think about such things. I come of hard-working folk, and fine frocks and ciainty food wouldn't suit me." "Now, that is just what I have come here to talk to you about, Mattie mine!" "Oh, don't, sir! John Hazel might get to hear that you called me that, and—and—" "And who is John Hazel ?" Torr inquired. "He's grandfather's cowman, sir," she replied with a smile.</p>
        <p>"Have you promised to marry the fellow?" "Not yet, sil. But he—he—l—l--" "Bother Hazel and all cowmen! Now listen to me."</p>
        <p>"I can't, sir! I'm so afraid that grandfather'll catch you here. Or perhaps John--" He put a hand over her mouth. She grew red and very angry. ~"Well, don't mention that brute's name. You don't belong to him, and never will. Come out of this and have a stroll in the lane. Do, there's a dear girl." After a little persuasi( n she accompanied him to the lane—Honeypot-lane. They seated themselves on a low stile.</p>
        <p>"How would you like to change your life—your way of life? You are much too charming to be buried for ever in the country like this." While he spoke he endeavoured to slip his arm around her waist. She would not permit such a familiarity, however. "Don't do that, sir. I never let any- one do that," she said, rather sharply. "I always say a woman shouldn't let a man touch her or kiss her unless he's a-courting her to marry her." "My dear 'Mattie, you are too severe. Surely you would make an exception—an exception in my case ? " said Val Torr.</p>
        <p>"Why in your case, sir? You're nothing to me —you aren't a-courting me to marry me." He laughed uneasily.. "I am your greatest admirer, Mattie. But listen to me. You are far too charming to be buried alive in Stocklands. Farm. You must come to London—the world of pleasure, beauty, and fashion. You must come and stay with some friends of mine. They would be delighted with you, just fresh from the country, like the flowers, the butter, and the cream."</p>
        <p>"I like London," she replied. "It's a big place, isn't it? I've been there."</p>
        <p>"Well, come again, this time to stay. You must. be thoroughly tired of this life—cows and sheep and pigs and poultry, and, worst of all, John Hazel. Give them all up, and come with me. It's a gentleman husband that you must have, not a clod." "I'm not in a hurry to get married, sir—oh, no." "Nor am I, Mattie. I will tell you the truth. You are a sensible girl, and will understand. I have spent rather too much money on my pleasures up to the present, and I cannot marry any woman without money—without a good big fortune. You are very handsome, Mattie, but, unfortunately, you have no fortune." "Yes, sir, I have," she replied, quickly. "What is it ?"_ _ _ . .</p>
        <p>, She laughed. "My face is my fortune, sir." He joined her in the laugh. "And quite enough, too, for a rich man. Come to London, and I'll find a husband for you when you want to marry. I cannot marry you myself, but promise you truly to find some other gentleman to do so." "You're too kind, sir; I shouldn't like to trouble you so much. Oh, no, nobody asked you to marry me, sir, and I can find a gentleman husband for myself when I want one. Thank you all the same, sir. As I think now, John Hazel's more to my fancy than you are." "Now you are angry, my beautiful Mettle. 9h, well, never mind. I shall wait patiently, for sooner or later you will accept my offer to take you to some friends of mine in London. Once you were in town everybody would be talking of your beauty, and you would .be made much of, and could have as many fine frocks as you wished. And more than that, you--" "Oh, I say! Somebpdy's a-coming up the lane. I do believe it's John Hazel. Go, sir—do go; get over the stile and cut across this field to the park." He did not require to be told twice; he was off and away in an instant. When Martha dropped the book in her bower at the end of the orchard, Torr picked it up, but he did not return it to her; he absently kept it in his hand, and now, when he was half-way through the park, he glanced at its title. His astonishment was unspeakable when he read: "Stones of Venice." Mattie reading Ruskin! No, no; she couldn't understand a word of him. The book must belong to someone else, he thought—someone had left it in the farmhouse. Some educated person might have put up there in the summer of the previous year. He turned to the fly-leaf, and read the superscription : "Dorothy Berkley." Berkley— Berkley. 'Why, that was the name of the banker to whom he purposed to let the Hall in the autumn. Perhaps some of the family had been staying at Stocklands and had taken a fancy to the Hall at the time. He would question Mettle to-morrow. At the same hour on the following morning Val Torr was in thelane leading to Stocklands Farm. Martha Woods soon made her appearance. . "You are as sweet and as fresh as the morning, my pretty maid," he cried, on seeing her. She bobbed a curtsey as usual. "Good-morning, sir! " she said. "I can't stay a-talking to you like yesterday. I was all behind, and it threw me out all the day. Gentlemen shouldn't want to talk along of poor country girls. The fine things gentlemen says to them they too often take to be real true, and it does them no end of harm. Now, you go on about your business, sir, and leave me to honest John Hazel." "My dear Mattie. I wanted to befriend you," Torr returned, growing rather uncomfortable under her words. "You are too good for your position ; your life is a waste. I thought to raise you to a higher position, and in return you speak words that to me sound' ungrateful—very ungrateful." "Why should you want to raise me, sir? I'm nothing to you. I'll tell you straight, sir. I don't believe in you. You're not talking for my good. Now, you go on away, sir. I'm going a-milking." "Oh, before you go, perhar you will be so good as to tell me to whom this book belongs." The girl took it from him. "it is mine," she replied.</p>
        <p>"Yours now," he said, "but formerly Dorothy Berkley's—eh ?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Yes, yes," she answered, quickly; "formerly Dorothy Berkley's, but mine now." She grew crimson, and, without another word, hurried away to her cows, two or three fields .off. _</p>
        <p>"And that is all I have got for my pains," Torr miltt.red. lonVinm after her. "She is not Quite SO simple as I thought. These rustics are very aeceiv- ing. After all, give me a city-bred girl; she knows what you mean, and you know what she means. Still, she is a gloriously handsome creature."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>BAWAW McDOUGALL'S Patellt SELF—RAIS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The shop windows at this season of the year are more than usually attractive in the display of fascinating goods of all sorts, for as year succeeds year the manufacture of lovely materials seems to advance steadily along the path to perfection. We have been favoured with a very early glimpse of summer fashions which have sprung into life in a night, so to speak, and have blossomed in the welcome burst of fine weather and brilliant sunshine which has recently rejoiced all hearts. There is every promise of a still greater increase of beautiful garments of all sorts and bewilder- ingly beautiful hats and toques which Will lead many of us to yield to their fascinations and wander into the pleasant paths of extrava- gance, which point to the uncomfortable quag- mire of debt. For early summer wear there are some truly charming light cloths exceed- ingly soft and lending themselves to artistic drapery. A new shade of these, known as zinc, is blue-white anti contends in fashionable</p>
        <p>UNTRY WEAR. esteem with pale green and a faint apricot. Gowns are trimmed with lace and taffeta and when for afternoon wear, are made rather elaborately, and still sweep the ground for a few inches with their flowing folds. Lace is the trimming par excellence. _</p>
        <p>For morning wear the skirt must clear the ground all round although only a fleeting glimpse of the feet is permitted, but for wear in the country a more abbreviated style is de rigueur, such as is depicted in my sketch. The well-hung skirt in this instance is long enough to come halfway down the ankles but no further, and is bound with leather to give it greater durability. The smart little coat can be worn open in front or buttoned up, as desired. P.: is kept neatly into the vest by a leather belt which passes through an opening at either side and fastens underneath the front of the coat, which is allowed to fall loose. Strappings of leather give a finish to the simple coat sleeves and a leather band, finished by a small buckle, surrounds the crown of the neat felt hat, whose only trim- ming it forms, together with a natural- coloured quill. A neat cravat tied in a bow at the throat, and a fashionable veil drawn loosely over the brim, complete a simple, but particularly smart and chic toilette. There are many who annually invest in a dress of this style, and all those will find this is an ex- cellent costume to go by, as it will look well</p>
        <p>ia. e-- i 4A, I , Z)gtA . 1 t ' I , , k / / /7; MO' j I - \ qS'___----, • i/ t . ' Y,I/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PRETTY BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>either in serge or tweed or in any colour, although I should advise brown or navy blue serge,or a nice mixed heather tweed in prefer- ence to any other. So much depends on whether the dress is to be used as an everyday costume or only occasionally. There are some very pretty blouse materials this year and the patterns which appear are some of the most dainty and remarkably artistic. Linen and tussore silk will be much in evidence for morning wear, while for the afternoon,white silk made up in a pretty style, such as that set forth in my second sketch, will prove not only very smart but very useful as it looks well with any sort of skirt. In this instance the silk is trimmed with. bands of lace and is arranged in the front in numerous tiny tucks. Any girl who possesses a good blouse pattern ought to be able to keep herself well dressed without much expense as blouse materials are quite inexpensive, and one can secure a ready-made coat and skirt together with an extra skirt or two of a more dressy p, description for house wear for very little ex- ense and these, together with a smart straw toque or French sailor hat for morning wear, and a more elaborately trimmed hat for the afternoon, will provide plenty of variety and can always be added to from time to time and varied to sui later requirements.</p>
        <p>MODISH TOQUE. The millinery models are particularly pretty just now and 1 hardly fancy that there will be much improvement on those offered for our present inspection. The charming toque illustrated, is made of fanciful straw trimmed at the back of the brim, which is turned up sharply with masses ox violets and a small aigrettel The brim does not projcct at all at the back of the head, but follows the outlines of the coiffure, while it shades the face in front. There are. two distinct styles in headgear this season and it behoves us to consider seriously before selecting one or the other as they are suited to very different types of face. in the one case, the hat has a wide brim, which is turned back abruptly in . front from the face which it frames, while in the other it is the back of the hat which is turned up while the brim is somewhat depressed and projects over the face, which it leaves in shadow. The French sailor hat may also be included in this category, as the bandeau at the back gives it a tilt which is not only effective,but has the same effect as in the variety previously described. Both are pretty and both becoming, but each has its own style of features to set oft 0 advantage. COQUETTE.</p>
        <p>• c, c 1, 4 tor/ • „ 1\ )11 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>MY PRETTY MAID.</head>
        <p>MY PRETTY MAID.</p>
        <p>Bit THE COUNTESS SULMALLA, ikuthor of "Under the Sword," "Monica's Story.' "The Birth-Mark," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>What -t.rought him out here this morning? II was not a lover of Nature; in fact, he observed the changes of the seasons only in connection with hi, out-door amusements. The hard frosts of winter were very well when be wished to skate ; a thaw with intermittent rainfall was preferable when he felt disposed to follow the hounds; the heat and gorgeousness of summer were just right for a cruise on Lady Rose's yacht; and the cool breezes and mists of autumn were delightful when he made one of a party of guns up in the North. During the spring, when in town, it was all one to him; he had his cab, and he never accepted an invitation to a garden-party or to a river picnic. Just now his many acquaintanc-s wondered what it was that could keep him so long away from the world down there in Essex. Ile was not enter- taining any of his friends, and he hated solitude. What could it be ? they asked one another. What had really called Valentine Torr out of town, before the season was over, had been business of importance with his steward in connection with the letting of Weald Hall—a property which had lately fallen to him. It was a fine old place, Weald Hall, replete with historical and romantic stories. The Belvedere standing some little distance from the Hall and connected with it by an underground passage—was u favourite Sunday after- noon resort of the local rustics. Here they would sit, on the grass-grown mound which was crowned by the old tower, and repeat with much colouring what they had heard about the place. Sometimes Some of them would think the truth, even when coloured, not sufficiently horrible to make the hair rise and the blood run cold; therefore they would invent matter to please their own tastes. Clumsy, rambling, impossible though these improvised stories were, they gave infinite satisfaction to the ignorant and credulous.</p>
        <p>Weald Hall and the surrounding lands were mortgaged, for Valentine Torr was in low water; he had gone the pace for years. When Mr. Berkley, a Lombard-street banker, asked to become his tenant, he readily accepted him as such. Not until the autumn, however, would Mr. Berkley take possession of the Hall. Yes, business had called Val Torr here, but what had kept him here was another thing. It was morning, and early, very early for him. Grass, leaf and flower were yet dew-bespangled, and the lark was singing the first song of the opening day. It was not for these things that Val Torr sauntered under the oaks of his park. His head was turned in one direction, his eyes rested on one spot. He was growing impatient. He looked at his watch. He took the half-smoked cigar from his mouth and dashed it to the ground. He swore. He quickened his pace and was soon out of the park. "Well, 'if Mahomet won't come to7the mountain, the mountain must go to Mahomet, ' " be muttered. Across several fields he strode, and over one or two stiles he bounded, until he reached a narrow, high- hedged lane—Honey-pot-lane the rustics told him it was called.o Not far distant was a farmhouse— Stocklands. It was to this house he was making his way. He had not proceeded many yards up the lane when a young woman came in view. ,She was crossing ' from one field to another, and carried a milking pail, also a milking stool. She was hand-. some fad, with a well-developed form, and her years numbered twenty-three.</p>
        <p>"Ah ! Where are you going, my pretty maid ? " he exclaimed, as he neared her. She started. She had not observed him. Her cheeks grew a shade redder than they had been before he spoke. She smiled and dropped him a respectful curtsey. "How unkind you are growing," he continued. "I waited in the park for you a tremendous time. I expected to see you near the Belvedere. How was it that you did not meet me there ?"</p>
        <p>:"011, sir, I couldn't do that. 7. can't help meeting you here now, like this, because I'm on my way to my work. I'm going a-milking, sir, so grandfather can't be angry along of me anyway; but I couldn't go right away in the •Inorning and waste my time a-talking along of fine gentlemen when twenty-three cows is got to be milked." "But, Martha, or Mattie, as your grandfather calls you, you are much too charming to spend all your time in dairy-work; in fact, my dear girl, you are superior to your position. It is a shame that you, such a fine, handsome girl as you are, should have to work at all." : "Oh, don't talk such nonsense to me, sir. I come of hard-working folk, and what's good enough for them's good enough for me. Grandad always:says that, and he never says anything that isn't wise and true."</p>
        <p>"Hang your grandfather, Mattie t I don't care what he says. I repeat you are too handsome—too good in every way for such work as this." The young woman's laugh rang out on the char morning air. I Your laugh is very musical, do you know? Val Torr said.</p>
        <p>"I don't know what you mean, sir," she replied, and laughed again. "Grandfather's plain-spoken; he don't put his thoughts into fine words, tad I'm just like him. If words isn't plain, I can't make them out."</p>
        <p>"You are really very refreshing, quite in keeping with your meadows and your cows and your butter and cream; and when you approach there is a scent of new-mown hay. You delight me, Mattie." "Your talk's too fine for me, sir; I can't make out quite what you mean. I'm only a dairy-maid, and don't understand anything that ain't plain. I s'ppose it's something very nice, but, I tell you, sir, it's all gibberish to me. Sometimes parson talks that fine in church on Sundays that grand- father can't make him out at all, It's all very well for fine ladies and gentlemen, they know every- thing, but for working-folk, like me and grand- father, talk must be plain." He smiled at her, and caressed his tawny moustache. "How old are you,. Nettie?" he asked, suddenly. "Good gracious, sir!" she exclaimed. "Old enough to know better than to be wasting my time here a-talking to a fine gentleman when twenty- three cows is got to be milked." "You are not wasting your time, Mettle. Put down your stool, my wild briar rose, and listen to me." "I shall be all behind, sir, and grandfather— he's a terror when he's put out. I can't listen to you any more." She hurried away. He strode after her.</p>
        <p>"Mattie, I have something very serious to say to you," he continued. "Will you meet me this evening after you have finished with your cows and your pigs and all your other abominations? I will speak as plainly as I can. I will make my mean- ing clear—you shall understand every word. Say you will meet inc—do--do, dear girl." "I can't promise such a thing, sir. Grand- father'd be awful cross, and he's a terror when he's</p>
        <p>put out." "Don't talk to me of your grandfather," Val Torr rejoined, impatiently. "Do you know, Mks Mattie, that I could refuse to renew the lea.o of the farm he holds, and I would, too, if he gave me any of his impudence." "Oh, sir, poor grandfather isn't impudent; he's a dear, good old man, but he wouldn't like me to meet you or any other gentleman shyly. lie doesn't think such ways right, ,for he knows that gentlemen only amuse themselves with young women like me."</p>
        <p>"You must not believe all that your grandfather says ; old people are fearful and suspicious. I assure you, my dear Mattie, I admire and like you too much to amuse myself at your expem.e." She shook her head. "Good-bye, sir," she cried, and hurried away from him.</p>
        <p>"Good-evening, Woods. Pleasant just now, after the heat of the day." "Your servant, sir," replied the old fanner, touching his bat. "Yes, i's pleasant now ; hut the best time of a summer's day is the early morn- ing—sunrise. Ah, I suppose that's what you've nevi, seen, sir." "It's cool, Wcods—the early morning is cool, nothing more." "It's beautiful, sir, the most beautiful time of all the day." • "Perhaps so, perhaps Do; but it's not in my way, you know." "No, I suppose not, sir. You gentlemen generally turn the night into day, so I've heard: when we're rising, you're going to your beds. But it isn't natural, squire. All the birds and the beasts teach us a lesson; they go to their rest about sundown." - _</p>
        <p>"Very good ; very good, indeed. All the same, 1 don't think I shall take a lesson from the beasts</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>It was autumn, and Val Torr was still in town. Several invitations to country houses lay on the table before him, but. without any hesitation he decided to accept that which came from Mr. Berkley, his tenant at Weald. He had met the banker on several occasions. He was a genial man, and Torr hoped that his daughter would prove equally kind. He would go in for the girl. He came of a good old county family, while the Berkleys were new people. who would deem it an honour, well worth paying for, to be allied to the head of the Torr family. It would seem strange to him to be a guest in his own halls ; nevertheless, he would not appear to feel his position. He would do his utmost to please old Berkley. With the girl he would make his way rapidly; he had no fear of failure in that quarter—he was always successful with women.</p>
        <p>Torr smiled as he penned a gracious acceptance of the invitation to Weald.</p>
        <p>He arrived at sundown. The dressing-bell was ringing as he alighted from the phaeton, and only a few gentlemen remained chatting in the ball. His host was not one of them, so he immediately went to his room to prepare for the great meal of the day. "Anyone we know staying here, Pike?" he inquired of his valet. "Colonel and Mrs. Murdock, sir, and Lady Hartwell, and Miss Goulding, sir, and-"</p>
        <p>"Miss Goulding!" "Yes, sir, and her mother." :1" Hideous old hag!" Pi "But the honoured guest, sir, is a South African millionaire—a'Mr.. Hazel--quite a young gentle- man, too, sir." "Hazel—Hazel! He is of no family; but those vulgar millionaires never are."</p>
        <p>. "-No, Sir." E "Any more ?" "Yes, sir;. the house-party is a very large one. Valentine Torr was the first to appear in the drawing-room. Presently, however, he heard some persons enter the, conservatory from the gardens. Ile took up a book, but did not peruse a line of it, for the voices in the conservatory attracted his attention. Surely he knew - that laugh, so clear and gay. But it could not be. No, no; it was only a resemblance. His host, after ten minutes or so, welcomed him heartily, and Miss Goulding did not conceal her delight at the discovery that he was one of the house-party. Just before the butler announced dinner, Miss Berkley joined the guests. Yes it was she. He had not been mistaken. But how was this ? He had not had such a disagreeable surprise in his life before. "Come, Torr, let me present you to my. little girl," said his host. "This is Mr. Torr, my dear."</p>
        <p>He bowed low before her. "My daughter, Dorothy—Miss Berkley." She inclined her head, then looked him straight in the face, without the slightest sign of recog- nition. He would take the cue from her. If that was what she wished, well, it should be so. After dinner, he did not make his way to her side, but during the evening she crossed the drawing-room and seated herself on an ottoman beside him. "This is' a nice old place of yours, Mr. Torr," she began. "I can't bear modern houses. My father rents this Hall from you expressly for me. He is very indulgent, don't you think? I admire ,this part of the country so much." "Yes, it is rather pretty," he answered, some- what awkwardly. "But I shouldn't care to spend much of my time here. Did you know my uncle?" he asked her. "The late squire, I mean ?" "Oh, yes; a dear old gentleman. I have visited here during his lifetitne. He was so good, and so much beloved by his tenantry. My grand- father held a farm under him for thirty years ; he is under you now. He holds Stocklands Farm."</p>
        <p>"Oh, indeed." The mist began to UR. His mental vision grew clearer. She Aras the granddaughter of old Richard Woods, after all! How beautiful she was He had thought her handsome when she had played the part of the dairymaid, but how much hand- somer she was now, with her elegant apparel and her magnificent surroundings. He asked her if she had enjoyed the past season in town. "I did not go to town this last season," she replied, regarding him over the top of her fan. "I spent the spring and the summer here—at Stock- lands, I mean—with my grandfather. I helped in the dairy and had a delightfully healthful time. I played dairymaid to perfection, she added, with a laugh. "I used to go a-milking, sir—twenty-three COWS, early in the morning. Oh, yes, I was a great success. I deceived one gentleman so well— completely—that he actually did his very best to deceive me—poor Mattie, the artless dairymaid, who was supposed to know nothing of the wicked- ness of town-bred gentlemen. It was so amusing." She laughed again. "And then, what a rare occurrence, to see an exquisite town-bred gentle- man of one of the very smartest sets in his true colours. I may never have that opportunity again." He was beginning to feel very uncomfortable. What a terrible blunder he had made! However, he would put a bold face on the matter, and endeavour to remove the unfavourable impression which he feared he had made on her. "You are an excellent actress, Miss Berkley. Your Mattie, the dairymaid, was true to life; but you did not deceive me; I—l " "Don't say that you knew I was Miss Berkley. That would be untrue. You just thought I was poor Mattie Woods, the ignorant, credulous, weak dairymaid, who could be led, without much trouble, to her destruction, as you had so fully planned." "No, indeed, Miss Berkley, you are quite wrong," Torr replied, with a forced laugh. "No doubt I shall find it difficult to convince you." "You could never convince me, Mr. Torr; but let us say no more about it; it was good fun for me, and I pardon you. By the bye, permit me to make you and Mr. John Hazel known to each other. You must have beard of him: he is a multi-millionaire, highly entertaining—he has seen so much of the world, although he is young, but he sees everybody and everything in a true light. lam sure you will like him." ti "Hazel—John Hazel !" said Val Torr, knitting his brows. "When playing dairymaid, you spoke once or twice of a John Hazel, your grandfather's cowman,, I think you said he was. Are they one and the same? Did he also go in for private theatricals ? " Dorothy Berkley laughed. "No, oh, no! John Hazel, my grandfather's cowman, and John Hazel, the millionaire, are not one and the same: they are uncle and nephew. The uncle has just left my grandfather's service, and is living on a comfort- able allowance from the nephew. The nephew is fatherless, has been so for many years; he came of age before he went to Oxford; his University days are now over; and, being without brothers or sisters, he is sole master oethe ninny millions his father made in Soul It Africa. Isn't that nice ?" "The only thing he has to do now is to look for a lady who will become his wife and share his millions," said Torr. "He has done so, and been successful," Dorothy Berkley returned. "Is the lady not fortunate ?" "if money is her heart's desire—well, yes, I suppose she would be called so; otherwise, I should say she is unfortunate." "Unfortunate!" exclaimed Dorothy. "In what way, Mr. Torr ? " "To be allied to a lout—a clod—must be a horrible experience," he returned. "And, with all his millions, John Hazel is close to the soil. Now, is it not so? His uncle was a cowman, and his father, no doubt, followed a similar calling before he made his millions." John Hazel, junior, is a perfect gentleman, Mr. Torr; but you shall judge for yourself." She beckoned across the room, and was immediately joined by the young South African millionaire. She introduced him to Valentine Torr, then left them. At the expiration of his visit—three weeks—the impoverished owner of Weald Hall offered marriage to Miss Berkley. She thanked him for the great honour he paid her, and informed him that she was already a promised bride. Being so near the soil herself—her grandfather being a working farmer— she thought she could not make a more suitable alliance than that which John Hazel, the multi- millionaire, proposed to her. Mr. Valentine Torr was not seen at Weald again during the tenancy of Mr. Berkley and his charming daughter, Dorothy.</p>
        <p>[THE END. 1</p>
