      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE ASSOCIATION CUP FINAL.</p>
        <p>Viewed merely as a spectacular wonder, the -scene at the Crystal Palace at the final of the English Football Cup on Saturday last -was a remarkable one, and the fact that the two contesting clubs were Lancashire organ- isations in no way deterred thousands of enthusiasts from all parts of the country travelling to the Metropolis to witness this • always popular match. The official number of visitors watching the game is put down at 61,374, which is a trifle disappointing 'compared with the attendances of previous years, and when it is remembered that the two clubs competing were such brilliant ex- ponents of the socker code as Manchester 'City and Bolton Wanderers. Of the total -number present it can be safely estimated that at least 30,000 came from the North, •eighty-two special trains being required for the occasion. It is interesting to mention that the first of the specials reached London about 3 a.m., and between that hour and -nine and ten o'clock there was a continuous 'stream of well-filled trains. Of the corn- latants it can be said that the records of 'both in the cup ties were equal, Manchester 'City having vanquished Sunderland, Woolwich Arsenal, Middlesborough, and Sheffield Wed- nesday, whilst Bolton Wanderers were re- sponsible for the downfall of Reading, Southampton, Sheffield United, and Derby 'County. On the other hand, the City team were First Leaguers, and apart from this match are almost on a par with Sheffield Wednesday for championship honours; whilst the Wanderers are not even holding a prominent position in the Second rtherefore the apparent difference of the two</p>
        <p>THE CAPTAINS OF THE R L TEAMS. ,elevens as regards class seemed obvious. That the City won by the narrow margin of _tone goal to nothing was mainly due to -Meredith, their captain, who played the game 'of his life. But taking everything into con- sideration there was not mush room for .doubt as to Which was the superior team The Mancunians combination was much more -pronounced than that of their opponents, and was the understanding in this respect that led to Livingstone giving the pass to his ,captain which resulted in the only goal being Scored. The Wanderers, however, have no reason to be „ashamed of their performance, and if they had only displayed the energy and dash in the first moiety as in the second 'the result might have been different. At the 'Close of the game, Mr. Lyttelton, the Colonial Secretary, in a short, but happy -speech, presented the cup to the winners, and made incidental mention to the time when he -PlaYed the game. The Prime Minister also Said a few words and referred to the fact that whilst Mr. Lyttelton had spoke to them as an expert, he could only speak as an 'admiring ignoramus. He was glad that the tie had been fought out between two Lanca- shire teams, and he was sure they would "Pardon his satisfaction at the success of -alanchester City.</p>
