      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>DEATH OF A VETERAN ACTOR,</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A VETERAN ACTOR,</p>
        <p>The name of Mr. John Coleman, who died last week at Buckhurst Hill in Epping liorest is inseparably connected with the history of the stage during the Victorian 'era, though to the last generation he was alot so well known. From childhood he was stage struck, and ran away from his home in Derby to get an engagement from the famous Macready. He, however, after he had heard him recite, sent him back home, but by the time Mr. Coleman was mineteenhe had played Othello to Mac- rea dy's I ago. However, it was not so much as MR. JOHN COLEMAN. an actor as a manager that</p>
        <p>he Made his</p>
        <p>greatest reputa- Con, and, in addition, wrotemany plays -which he often produced and played in him- self. In fact, he was reputed to be the -alithor of over a hundred plays in all. Amongst 71us many-intimate friends Mr. Colem4n could count such celebrities as Macready, Phelps, _Charles "Mathews, Buckstone, Southern, and 13enjamin Webster, whilst amongst those ~,virlom "brought out" were Mr. J. L. 'mole, Mr. Wilson Barrett, and many other Popular favourites. But chief of all his -friends was Charles Reade, and he dramatised ug produced some of his novels as plays. ...Never too Late to Mend" was the greatest .uecess. In more recent times, when Sir Augustus Harris died, Mr. Coleman took over the management of Drury Lane for a little "while and produced a play of his own called ' The Duchess of Coolgarjlie„" which made a .I"usiderable success. He was widely known ,?_nd wonderfully popular, and his demise will ''ae universally regretted.</p>
        <p>TWO PROMINENT RUSSIAN OOMMANDERS.</p>
