      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SURREY THEATRE.</head>
        <p>THE SURREY THEATRE.</p>
        <p>For nearly a quarter of a century the old Surrey Theatre, under the management of the Conquest family, has been the most popular place of amusement in London south of the river. But now, owing to a difference of opinion as to rent between landlord and tenant, the long connection is to be severed shortly, and it is passing into other hands. The owner of the house thought that the success of the Surrey justified an increase in rent, whilst on his side, Mr. Conquest con- sidered that a reduction should be granted owing to certain alterations, made to satisfy the London County Council, which appre- ciably lessened the seating accommodation of the theatre. As they ;;ould not come to terms, the present lessee decided to vacate the old house, so closely connected with his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>OLD SURREY THEATRE.</head>
        <p>THE OLD SURREY THEATRE.</p>
        <p>name, at Midsummer. Under the lengthy Conquest regime the Surrey Theatre has been noted for its annual pantomime, which hos been to the East End what Drury Lane is to the, West End. and its highly coloured melo- dramas, which occupied the stage during the summer months. As with Drury Lane, the Conquests always paid the greatest attention to scenic effects and mechanical contrivances with the most sensational results. It was Mr. Conquest who first discovered Dan Leno in pantomime, when in 1886 he engaged him for the run of Sinbad the Sailor" at five pounds a week, and many other famous names are connected with the old theatre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BARONESS BIIRDETT-COUTTS</head>
        <p>BARONESS BIIRDETT-COUTTS</p>
        <p>The nintieth birthday of the Baroness Burdett-Coutts was the occasion of quite a little celebration at the well-known comer house in Stratton-street. All day long pre- sents and letters were arriving: and an extra- ordinary number of bouquets were sent from all sorts and conditions of her friends and admirers. - No woman in England is more univers- ally esteemed: and in the na- tional opinion she has always been coupled with the late Queen Victoria who honoured her with a great personal friend- ship. The Bar- oness, amongst other things, was presented with a beauti- BARON .e:SS BURDET ful bouquet by COUTTS. the little four- year - old Baroness Clifton, inscribed " From the youngest Baroness to the oldett ? " Little Lady Clifton sat next to Lady Burdett- Couts at the Coronation: and a , great affection exists between these two peeresses in their own right. The Baroness Burdett- Coutts was raised to the peerage by Queen Victoria in 1871: and it was a choice which met with unanimous approval, as for many years she had devoted much of her vast wealth to charitable and philanthropic objects: Born ii 1814, she succeeded in 1837 to the enormous fortune of Mr. Coutts through his widow, the fascinating Miss 'Mellor, who was afterwards Duchess of St, Albans. Late in life the Baroness married Mr. Ashmead Bartlett, who was a far younger man than herself and took her name. Old though she now is in years, she is very hale and hearty and bids fair to see many more happy returns of her birthday.</p>
