      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>POLLARD DIVORCE CASE SEQUEL.</head>
        <p>THE POLLARD DIVORCE CASE SEQUEL.</p>
        <p>Another chapter in the extraordinary story of the Pollard divorce case opened on Satur- day last, when four of the principal figures in the recent proceedings appeared at Bow Street Police Court. Three of the men were connected with Slater's Detective Agency, whilst the fourth (Osborn) was Mrs. Pollard 's solicitor. There was quite an array of counsel when the case was called, and Mr. Guy Stephenson, who prosecuted for the Treasury, described the charge as that of "conspiracy to pervert the course of law and justice." He then intimated that he was only able that day to call formal evidence of arrest. The various detective-sergeants wha executed the warrants subsequently told their stories, the chief interest in which lay in the statements made by the men when taken into custody. Scott, who was arrested at his home, said, "Very well, I'll come with you. lam perfectly innocent, and have not ,LATER</p>
        <p>OSBO RN</p>
        <p>POLLARD,</p>
        <p>PRACEY.</p>
        <p>been to the office for thirteen months prior to last March. I have not taken any active part in the firm for many years. Henry manages the business, but I, of course, take the money. I know nothing of 'this case. Someone is supposed to have shown me a letter regarding the case. I am as innocent as a child." Osborn when taken into cus- tody said, "This is very annoying, but I suppose • I must go with you." Both Tracey and Davis when arrested expressed surprise when the warrants were read over to them, the former remarking, "You soon got the warrant. I thought there was going to be. a new trial." Sir Albert de Rutzen re- manded all the prisoners for a week, but as Osborn was the only one who could find bail, the other three were kept in custody. Mr. Henry, the manager of Slater's, was subsequently arrested at Southend later in the day, but too late to be brought before the magis- trate. _„ -</p>
