      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 30, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 30, 1904.</p>
        <p>corner, the Wiganer crossed after a strong effort. The visitors rallied, and another raid was made on the Wigan goal which was repelled by Mason. Near the interval Leytham took a forward pass from Hector, and a try was allowed. Half time score: Wigan, two tries (six points) ; Warrington nil. Changing ends, the visitors for a time claimed a monopoly of the attack, and Mason was repeatedly called upon to save his lines. Passing between Bate, Duckworth, and Kenyon almost succeedgd in its mission, the latter failing only a few yards inside. Wigan relieved, but Knight was in rare fettle, and utilizing touch well, the game once more went even. Eventually Ander-son got away from a pack, and after a strong rim succeeded in scoring,Mason adding the goal. A few minutes later,Elector and Ley- tham joined issue in a movement, and the ex-Lancashire man threw to Anderson, who again got in. Warrington by no means ralaxed their energies, and after Fairhurst had placed his side in a strong position, the locals were penalized,and Knight landed a goal, this concluding the day's scoring. Final :—Wigan, one goal four tries (14 points) ; Warrington, one goal (two points). HUDDERSFIELD v. WARRINGTON. Played at Fartown. Wednesday talfore a small attend- ance. The visitors played their second team, whilst Huddersfield had several men off. Walton kicked off for Huddersfield, against the wind. Good passing by Brady and Holroyd enabled Battye to get away, but Larard refused a good pass. From a penalty Maxworthy kicked a goal, and at half-time Huddersfield were leading by a goal to nil. On changing ends Brady initiated a good passing bout and Larard scored at thF corner. Holroyd failed at goal. On resuming Lewis was almost over, and tneu Warrington were penalised, and Walton kicked a goal. Short then dribbled well for Warrington, and invaded Walton, but the ball went into touch. The visitors again got away, and Knight scored an unconverted try. Result: Huddersfield 2 goals 1 try (7 points) ; Warrington 1 try (3 points). Teams : Warrington—Knight, back; Kenyon,Mahoney,Jackson and Short, threequarter backs ; Bate and Duckworth, half backs ; Jolley, Coleman Harmer Hewitt, Gallop, Ashton, Foster and Fairhurst, forwards. Huddersfield—Walton, back; Battey, Muxwerthy, Larard, and Lewis, threequarter backs ; Holroyd and Brady, half backs; Ballinger, Binns. Rhodes, Charles- worth, Picken, Hardy, Micklethwaite, and Poole forwards.</p>
        <p>BRADFORD BEAT SALFORD AND WIN THE CHAMPIONSHIP. This match, which was played at Halifax on Thursday was to decide who should be the ultimate champions of the League for 1903 and 1904. Both clubs finished their fixtures this week and had each scored 52 points, but as the goal average in the Northern Union does not count this extra match had to be played. In the early part ofithis year Salford held an advantage of one point, which they lost later by losing to Hunslet, and Bradford took the lead. They however, were unable to keep it, and by their drawn game with Hull on February 13th, the'.clubs were on level terms, having scored 34 points each. Since that time neither of them have lost any further points, and when the season • finished the championship was still undecided. When play com- menced 10,000 spectators were present. Early on both sides attacked strongly, and Bradford were repeatedly near scoring, but salford defended splendidly. Neither side had scored at half-time. Early in the second half Mosby placed a goal for Bradford, and later Hutt scored a try, which was not improved, and Bradford eventually won by one goal and one try (five points) to Salford nothing.</p>
      </div>
