      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>First Division.</head>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>This, the last match of the season at Warrington, was witnessed by 5,000 people. For Salford, it was an im- portant engagement, as they must secure the maximum points in order to tie with Bradford for the leadership of the League. They therefore brought a strong team. Warrington on the other hand, in view of the approach- ing cup final played seven reserves and took matters so easily as to arouse the resentment of the spectators. Teams :</p>
        <p>Warrington—Knight, back; Fish, Brooks, Mahoney, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; Davies and Bate, half backs; Morrison, Swift, Thomas, Lunt, Fairhurst, Cook, Forster, and Edmundson, forwards. Salford—Smith, back; Bedford, Thomas, Lomas, and Kinsey, three-quarter backs; Martin and Preston, half backs; Tunney. Rhapps, Heath, Shaw, Williams, Lewis, Bebbington, and Buckler, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Kennedy, Broughton Rangers. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Jones, Runcorn, and F. Gordon, Widnes.</p>
        <p>The home men played well during the opening stages, and after two minutes' play Davies made an opening for Thomas, who scored at the corner. After this Salford attacked, and after Lomas had failed to place a penalty goal, Bedford scored in an easy position, and Lomas placed a goal. The visitors maintained the attack, and Preston scored, Lomas again converting. Warrington rallied, and Fish and Harris were each pulled up on the line. Salford again took up the attack, the Salford captain getting over after a strong run. Maintaining the pressure,Bed ford scored after running from half-wiy. Later, Kinsey charged a kick of Mahoney's, and got a soft try. At the interval Salford led by 2 goals 5 tries (19 points) to Warrington 1 try (3 points). Turning round, Warrington pressed as the result of a clever run by Harris, which was speedily negatived by Lomas. Fish then effected a smart clearance, and a sub- sequent strong run by Davies was not well supported. Give-and-take play was the order, but the defence always proved superior to the attack, though Warring- ton were short of Swift most of this half. Result : Salford, 2 goals 5 tries (19 points) ; Warrington, 1 try (3 points). HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 2,000 spectators. The Rovers kicked off with the sun in their eyes, but had the best of play for some time. Lees sooty made a grand spurt, and crossed the line, and Ferguson converted. The Rovers next scored, Carmichml bringing off a penalty goal. Guy on the restart scored a try, Carmichael converting. Just on time Tyson got a try and Ferguson a goal. Result—Oldham, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points); Rovers, 2 goals 1 try (7 points). BROUGHTON RANGERS v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Played at Broughton, in fine weather, before 5000 people. Broughton had slightly the best of play in the first half, and Bentley scored an unimproved try. In the second portion of the game Broughton had again the best of matters, and tries were scored by Winsitill and S. James. Wilson also dropped a goal. Result— Broughton Rangers, 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; Wigan, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BATLEY v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>BATLEY v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Batley. Wattle Davies was a noticeable absentee on the Batley side,and Runcorn suffered by the absence of several of their regular players. In the first portion of the game Goodall scored a try, Oakland failing at goal. In the second half Batley pressed most of the time, but failed to score. Result—Batley, 1 try (3 points); Runcorn, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>KEIGHLEY v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>KEIGHLEY v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>At Keighley, before, 2,000 people. In the first hnlf, Walker and Myers kicked goals for Keighley, and Moffat scored a try for Leeds, Llewellyn failing to add the goal points. After the change of ends, Hardacre and Tempest added tries for Keighley, Myers missing the goal shot each time. Tempest scored again, Holden failing. Result—Keighley, 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; Leeds, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WIDNES v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>This return fixture was played at Whines, before 2,000 spectators. Both teams were at their strongest. Hughes scored for Widnes in the first half, and in the second portion of the game tries were scored by Barber, Leyshon, and Peacook. Alf Taylor placed a goal. Recult—Widnes, 1 goal 4 tries (14 points) ; Hudders- field nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SWINTON v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>Played at Swinton, before 3,000 spectators. Swinton went off well. Evans kicking a penalty goal. Then Albert Valentine scored, and Evans improved. On changing ends Griffin added an unimproved try to Swinton's score, and Barnett dropped a goal. Jones scored a try for Halifax, and the extra points were earned with Wedgwood's kick. Result—Swinton, 12 points ; Halifax, 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LEIGH v. HULL.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. HULL.</p>
        <p>Played at Leigh, before 1,030 spectators. Leigh had four absentees. Leigh had the best of play in the first half, but lacked finish. Hull broke away once, and Bland scored, no goal resulting. In the second half, Silcock equalised for Leigh, Johnson failing at goal. Then, from Underwood's had blunder, Brown gained a try for Hull, Jackson failing. Result—Hull, 2 tries ; Leigh 1 try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HUNSLET v. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>HUNSLET v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Played at Hunslet. In the opening half Hunslet did most of the attacking, 13,1 t a goal was kicked for each side by Mosby and A. Goldthorpe. Half time-2 points eaEh. Afterwards, though Davies had broken his arm and retired. Hunslet attacked for a quarter of an hour. However, Upton (twice) and Dunbavin scored in five minutes. Result—Bradford, 13 points ; Hunslet, 2 points. BRADFORD v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. -</p>
        <p>At Bradford on Monday. The home team won by five points to none.</p>
        <p>WIGAN v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>On Monday at Wigan, both teams being at full strength. Result : Wigan, 36 points; Huddersfleldmil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Warrington fulfilled their postponed fixture with Wigan on Tuesday at Wigan, when there was an attend- ance of about 5,000 people. From a Warrington point of view the match was viewed with little interest, for the" wirepullers " had already made themselves secure of the South-West Lancashire cup, and with the home side secure so far as First Division honours are con- cerned, the Warringtonians could afford to send what wns really. an "A " team, reserving their cup players for the more important affair at Salford. The backs were : Knight, Kenyon, Mahoney, Jackson, Short, Bate, and Duckworth. Wigan had exactly the same team which so decisively accounted for Huddersfield the previous evening, and playing with the wind were the first to become aggressive. Bate and Knight exhibited a sound defence and cleared out the bulky Wiganers. Divies'and Ander- son were frequently in possession, but their ideas at opening outthe game were very elementary, whilst the threequarters had little or no ideaat combination. With such a crude exhibition being displayed, it was no wonder to find Mahoney/Short and Jackson upsetting all attempts to cross. Eventuaily the Warrington for- wards defeated the opposition with smart footwork and the venue was changed. A pretty cross dribbling move- ment led by Forster and Harmer ought to have been turned to advantage, and this weakness when on the attack was followed by Mason clearing effectively and Wi (ran nmidng another direct raid. The same order of pro- cedlre was witnessed, and a weaker exhibition of back play has not been seen this season from what is pre- sumed to be a first class combination. Wigan pressed hard and after Kenyon had pulled up Barr twice at the</p>
        <p>THE GREAT SKIN CURE.—" Budden's S.R. Skin Ointment" will cure Old Wounds, Ulcera,tvd Legs, Eczema, Ringworm, Scrofula, Piles and Scurvy, and all Skin Diseases. Boxes, 74d. and 1/11d. Agent for St Helens: J. W. Jackson.- Warrington : H. Nl.Ashton Runcorn: Weston and Co. Ea.rlestown : A. Peake Widnes: Lewis and Calvert, Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SPRING "HEAT SPDTS "</head>
        <p>SPRING "HEAT SPDTS "</p>
        <p>Bile Beans Remove Them.</p>
        <p>Once upon a time as soon as Spring pimples and erup- tions appeared, there was a rush to that nauseous pre- paration, "treacle and brimstone." Times have changed since tnen, and scienGe has shown that, as a remedy for any ailment, mineral substances like sulphur are much inferior to vegetable extracts. One or two doses of Chas. Forde's bile beans—which are pleasant to take, handy to deal with, and may be obtained from any chemist—have more effect in purifying the blood than a dozen nauseous doses of the old-fashioned remedies.</p>
        <p>Miss Rosina Bridge,of Broad-street, Pilgwenlly,New- port, says :—" My blood was quite out of order, and pimples and eruptions broke out all over my face. Not only was my blood impure, but I felt run down, was anmmic, and had frequent attacks of headache, which turned to sickness. The lightest duties became too heavy forme, the least thing made me short of breath, and I felt so weak that I could hardly crawl about. Now and again, to make matters worse, I was attacked with biliousness and acute neuralgia. I visited a local hospital, and took the medicine prescribed,but no bene- fit resulted.</p>
        <p>"Having tried in vain almost everything else, I thought I would give Bile Be3ns a trial, and 'I am glad I did so. A few boxes worked a wonderful improve- ment in me, add after a thorough course I found myself strengthened and restored. I brightened up, the head- aches2becamel more rare, and at last ceased altogether. The pimples and eruptions disappeared, my appetite improved, and I felt myself taking renewed interest in all my surroundings. lam now cured, and owe this entirely to Bile Beans. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are obtainable from all chemists at is. 10. or 2s. 9d. per hex (2s. 9d. box con- tains three times is. 10. size). They may also be obtained upon receipt of prices from the Bile Bean Co., Red Cross-street, London, E.C. Avoid substitutes.</p>
      </div>
