      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TRAPPED! A 1-13L11AY ROMANCE.</head>
        <p>TRAPPED! A 1-13L11AY ROMANCE.</p>
        <p>By Thomas Sauniars,</p>
        <p>AUTHOR OF "A MIDNIGHT TR.A.GEDY." THE ANGLER'S STORY," "To WIN A WIFE," "'Twix-r HUSBAND AND FATHER," ETC.</p>
        <p>"I won't believe it; the thing is absurd." "Very well," said Janson Jones. "please yourself; but let me remind you that your silly incredulity has often prevented you listening to a good story. You seem to think that only printed stories are good stories. I would tell you, however, that soma of my experiences are as good as any of your printed stuff." I did not answer, and he went on, _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I'll tell you what it is; a thing must be invested with the halo of 'distance' to some people before they can appreciate it.. Let a thing happen at their own doors, and it becomes at once common-place, even if it is full of romance." I felt I was in for a sermon, so decid)d to take the lesser of two evils. Sitting back in the chair, and twisting my legs round the front spills as I used to do when hiving a tooth extracted, I exclaimed in a tone of resignation,— _</p>
        <p>"Go on, janson, let me have your adventure." But Janson Jones was a bit hart, and I had to concili- ate him a bit before he would commence. At last he went off at full speed, and this is his story.</p>
        <p>"Alf and I were both clerks in the same office. We were not so young as you would think, for we had been married some five or six years.</p>
        <p>Every summer when the time Came for our annual holidays, we had taken our wives to the seaside. This was a happy arrangement and we had all enjoyed it. But two summers ago, Alf and I thought we would like to spend at least part of our holiday without our wives. Not that we did not wish for their company exactly, but, when you are alone, you can venture into places where you dare not if you have a lady with you, -I mean such places as exploring dangerous cliffs for birds' eggs, or entering into the innermost recesses of a disused lead mine.</p>
        <p>And so with profuse apologies for a seeming indiffer- ence to their company, we set off for a week's trip to the Isle of Man. Now W 3 had often been for this sail before in the company of our wives, and had greatly enjoyed it. But what was that enjoyment compared with the present? We sauntered about the deck of the steamer with all the swagger of millionaires. We could stop, walk on, gaze about, or do anything we liked, without having to explain everything about us to two ladies. It was a glorious sail.. Freedom, glorious freedom! We puffed away at our cigars as if all the tobacco corn-. bines in the world had been at our beck and call. We drank in the ozone of the invigorating sea air, and dis- played as much ostentation as real live lords of creation. Now and again we would stop in our parade, and gazing out across the ocean, would talk a bit to give the impression to those around us that we were old travellers. As we were about to turn once more and retrace our steps towards the stern of the ship, I caught sight of two pairs of twinkling eyes peeping out of two hoods. Tiae eves were evidently part of the features of two pretty faces, and I was attracted at once. I tugged at the elbow of my friend, and would have called his attention, but he was so engrossed in studying the beauty of sea and sky, that at first I could make no impression upon him. At last, however, he turned, and as if tha brilliancy of the seascape had dazzled his eyes, he Was fully five minutes before he could make out in which quarter of the boat were the eyes I was telling him of. When he did locate them, he was as mach entranced as I was, and with a temerity I dared not assume, he walked along towards them._</p>
        <p>---- When opposite, he pretended to relight his cigar, bat the wind was so strong that as fast as-he struck a mitch, the light followed in the wake of its predecessor. Then as though in sympathy, one of the young ladies who owned the sparkling eyes, offered the loan of her um- brella to shield him. from Cle wiad,—and his troubles were ended.</p>
        <p>It was the easiest thing imaginable to strike up an acquaintance after the loan of an umbrella: and soon we were chatting merrily to the owners of the bright eyes. Alf always took his first choice wherever a lady was concerned, and now he was but a moment in making himself especially agreeable to • the taller of the two. As luck would have it, this time his choice suited me, for the smaller of the two, was, in my opinion, the prettier. We began to entertain our fair companions, and re- lated to them all the beantiful stories we could think of. We even invented some for the occasion. Right merrily did we pass the time along. We exchanged confidences. I told my past history to my fair friend, and she told me hers, and I could not help thinking to myself how like it was to my own wife's.</p>
        <p>Of course to her I crave a fictitious story of my past life, and asstrand the very honourable position of a bank manager. She seemed to be pleased at thiz, and, encouraged, I went on to weave for her some more choice fabrications.</p>
        <p>She appreciated my attentions to her, and as time went on we began to understand each other thoroughly, until I found myself actually making love to her. Whilst not altogether repelling me, she seemed a bit startled at this, but with the tact of her sex, she put it off and took on the conversation with rapidity and ease. All this time Alf had beep getting along with his companion in fine style. He had given it out that he was the son of a ; that, in fact, he was so wealthy he found money became a burden to him, and he was taking this little trip partly to disburse some of the troublesome shekels, and partly to recuperate my failing health.</p>
        <p>He ioo was free with his blandishments, and told her that she was the first woman to whom he had given his unreserved confidence.</p>
        <p>She seemed flattered at this, until he ended in offer- ing her his hand and his heart. But like a sensible woman, she suggested that he should know her a little longer before committing himself—even if it was only a day longer. And as the time went merrily on, and before we were hardly aware of it, the steamer pulled up at the pier, &amp;rid our sail was over. We busied Ourselves in gathering together the boxes and luggage of our friends, placing them snugly in a cab, and giving the necessary instructions to the man on the box. Then raising our hats and bidding them adieu we moved aside and they dro ye away.</p>
        <p>After they had gone, Alf and I stood chuckling to ourselves on the pavesnent. "Were they pretty, Alf, do you think ?" "Well, yes, I think so,—at least they might have been had they shown their faces. I could scarcely see anything but their laughing eyes." "I tried to see more of their faces." I said. "Once or twice I caught the hood of my companion's cloak with my hand, thinking thus to reveal a further portion of her face ; but all to no purpose; she seemed so frightened of catching a breath of the cool wind that she would not allow even a corner of it to fall down. " "All the same," said Alf, "I could see sufficient of their faces to notice that they painted, and very heavily too,—yes, and if I am not greatly mistaken, their eyebrows were neatly pencilled." "But, Alf, what a fix you would have been in had she accepted you. I was most careful not to commit my- self, and I was staggered when I heard you propose to her."</p>
        <p>"No fix at all, my dear boy. I was careful not to give berany real clue to my identity. Besides, I knew when I proposed that she would not accept me. I only did it to add a little piquancy to my supposed love- making. All the same lam glad we made an appoint- ment to meet them to-night at the Nunnery Grounds." " But do you think they'll come, Alf," I said. "Certainly they will ; they were as anxious for the appointment as we were." But what would our wives say if they knew ?" I said.</p>
        <p>"Ah!Ah! Ah ! " lauahed Alf. "They don't know, and that makes all the difference."</p>
        <p>With that we both 11l tde for our temporary lodgings, to regale ourselves with some refreshments, and pass on the time till dusk out Aide the Nunnery Grounds.</p>
        <p>We arrived at the rendevou-s so-nri-e-niint-ites before our fair friends. Whilst we were strolling up and down in front of the entrance, I said to Alf,— "I wonder why they were so careful to impress upon us that they would not meet us before dusk ?" _ _</p>
        <p>'Simply because they were anxi•aus that we should not see the paint on their faces." Just then they came in sight, and we could discuss the why and the wherefore no longer. . I noticed at, once that they still retained their cloaks, and their heads \se re still muffled in the reesses of the hoods. Of course it was still a bit cold, and being dusk I thought they wer3 taking the precaution against catching cold. We went on stralliag on through the grounis, chatting and laughing quite familiarly. We were soon as free and friendly as we were on the boat, and all went merry as the marriage bell. Suddenly, as we passed beneath a lamp which threw faint shadows across the pathway, my fair companion gave a slight cough, which seemed to act as a signal to her friend who was walking on in front with Alf. As by pre-arrangement, they both stopped, and throw- ing back the hoods of their cloaks, disclosed,—not two strangers, but our two wives.</p>
        <p>The-Y st'oo—cfbefOr-e.U.sWiitiiaces as stern as they could passibly assume. Taking from the inside of their gloves a small moist sponge each, they began to wash away the pencilled eye-brows and painted cheeks. •As each mark disappeared and the likeness to the wives we thought we had left at home became more perfect, the consternation written upon our faces was intense. They uttered not a word during the process, but simply stared into our ashen faces. Alf said afterwards, that at first he felt inclined to ran; but the thought held him, rivetterl to the ground that probably his infuriated wife would fly through the air after him, and, seizing his long locks, drag him back to the scene of his perfidy.</p>
        <p>After standing like statues for several minutes, relief came in the form of a smothered little laugh from my wife. I shall always bless her for that laugh, for it seemed as if suddenly a great weight had been lifted from me.</p>
        <p>I knew that nothing very dreadful could happen after that, and as if the laugh was infectious, we all laughed together; they laughed at us, arid we laughed at our- selves.</p>
        <p>At last, Alf's wifesai " Doa't you think, Alf, that it is well you waited for another day before I accepted your hand ? " And Alf had to confess that it was. "But what a pity ha is not a real millionaire." said my wife. "Yes," said Alf's wife. "If he had been, we would have had a real good holiday now that we are here." Alf and I were surprised at the turn things had taken. At first we thought we were in for a drubbing, but when they laughed and joked about it, we were non-plussed. Some weeks afterwards, they told us that their little joke had a twofold object. First, it was to test our tidelity,and secondly, it was to test their own powers of attraction.</p>
        <p>They wanted to see, if, after a few years of married life, they could still lay seige to our affections and capture them. Their success pleased them, and it was this consideration we had to thank for our escape. As it was, that holiday cost us more than any other, for we were two penitents and willing to pay any price for forgiveness. We dared not refuse them anything, and they knew it, for they ca-n 3 home laden with more good things than ever before."</p>
        <p>With the end of the nirrative Jaason Jones gave ms a look which plainly said N mv what do you think of that?" And the only thing which seemed to suggest itself to me on the spur of the moment was to apolo- gize. This I did handsomely, and Janson and I were reconciled on the spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>011 LEAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>011 LEAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>S2rifieSS far the People. The Rev. H. Dunnico gave his second address in the Royal Court Theatre on Sunday evening on the subject "What is Christianity," dealing principally with ques- tions and objections raised with regard to his former discourse. Oae of his correspondents stated that he had assumed without proving the existence of God and the reality of religion, arid thtt he would like him to prove these things in the light of the law of evolution. In replying to this comprehensive objection, Mr. Dannico said that people would not be satisfied with the materialist's belief that everything came into exist- ence by chance nor yet with the agnostic's theory of uncertainty, for they could see that behind the universe WAS insight, forethought, and intelligence, and he claimed that there could be no mind apart front person- ality. He (Mr. Dunnico) accepted evolution broadly. Evolution did not destroy the idea of God, for it was the history of a process and not the explanation of a cause. Religion was the recognition of an essential relationship between the human soul and the power above, and the religious faculty was part of human mture, which in itself was proof of the reality of religion. Nothing had power to uplift and transform the character like religion as it had been presented by Jesus Christ, for what could be higher or nobler than the religion taught in the New Testament?</p>
        <p>- Wycliffe Sunday School Anniversary. This event took place on Sunday, and except that the collections did not reach the total amount required, it was attended with success. The preacher was the Rev. W. Evans, of Blackpool. This was his first visit to Warrington, and his discourses so delighted his con,gregations, that a return visit will be welcomed and looked forward to with pleasure: At the morning ser- vie°, in addition to the usual discourse, he delivered a delightful address to the scholars, in which he drew some useful lessons from the ward "anniversary." In advising the scholars to seize the present, and make the best of their opportunities, he referred to John Rus- kin's early life, and asked them to remember Ruskin's motto " To-day. To-day. To-day." The morning dis- course was based on the words "I have yet many things to say unto you, but yecannet bear them now." (John xvi. c. 12v.) Inability to understand spiritual truth, he said, came through want of condition. Life had as much to do with truth as truth had to do with life. The preacher showed how through friendship and suffering, the apostles of Christ, although many of them unlearned men, came at length to a fuller knowledge of spiritual truth.—The children, who filled the galleries, joined heartily in the singing of a number of suitable hymns and the chant "Tee strain upraise." The choir sang the anthem " Be glad and rejoice," the solo part being effectively taken by Mr. Bowman Hibbert. Mr. NV. H. Payton, Miss Bac. presided at the organ. Mr. Thomas Wallington read a statement prepared by the officials of the Sunday. school stating that £5O were required to efficiently carry on the work of the school daring the ensuing year. A service of song was given by the scholars in the church in the afternoon, and attended by parents and some of the P.S.A. members. Hymns were sung from the Cecelia music leaflet by p,rinission of Mr. L. C. Hill, and also from the compositions of Mr. H. E. Nichol, of Hull, which included the choral march "The Watchword." In the solo and chorus "Shall we know each other there ?" the solo part was taken by Mr. A. Cotterill. The in- fants also sweetly satin' a hymn " When Jesus Came." Mr. William Brown, the sunerintendent, conducted the singing and Mr. Tom F. Wallington presided at- the organ. Mr. E sward Wallington delivered an address, in which he directed his remarks more especially to the younger portion of the audience. There were many ob- ject lessons, he said, which could be learnt from every day life. When they saw people the victims of in- temperance, or using objectionable language, or even bringing dishonour and discredit upon themselves by some act of dishonesty, he would ask them to take these scenes as object lessons and be determined to avoid be- ing concerned in them. On the other haatl, when they saw young people rise in the esteem of their friendsand neighbours by their diligence and attention to duty and by their integrity and uprightness of conduct,he would ask them to let these be object lessons, showing the possi- bilitiesof all boys and girls. It was not sufficient for them to have good intentions. If they depended upon themselves their lives would be a failure ; they must seek strength from God.—A communication was read from the Rev. John Yonge, thanking the brethren of the P.S.A. for their kind and thoughtful expression of sympathy with him and his family in their anxious hour of trial.</p>
        <p>At the evening service there crzs a large congregation. The Rev. W. Evans preached from the words "Here- after ye shall see heaven open, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of Man." (John fc., v. 51). The preacher pointed out that here was set up the tremendous claim that divinity was conferred on human life only on the lines of Christ's life. The great possibilities of the Christian faith were not realised, he said, because our hearts were not pure and true. The choir sang the sanctus (Sullivan) and also the anthem "Seek ye the Lord" (Dr. J. V. Roberts). Mrs. R. H. Pearson erficiently took the solo part in the anthem. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. flue., is the conductor of the choir. The collections amounted to about ,C4O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services were held on Sunday at Bethesda Congregational Church, Runcorn. Two ser- mons were preached by the Rev. D. Wynn Evans of Chester, who also spoke at the P.S.A. meeting. The children rendered special music in a manner which reflected the greatest credit on the organist and choir- master, Mr. Pritchard and Mr. Worrall. The Rancorn Orchestral Society under the confinctorship of Mr. H. W. Handley played selections afternoon and evening, which were much appreciated. There were good con- gregations and collections were up to the average. STACK FIRE AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>On .Sunday afternoon the Runcorn Fire Brigade was summoned to afire which broke out in the stackyard of Astmoor Farm Halton. It seems that Mr. Edward Finch's son took the glass of a bicycle lamp into the yard and endeavoured to ignite a piece of paper by con- centrating the sun's rays through the glass upon it. The success of the experiment seemed to give him some alarm, for he dropped the burning paper and ran into the house. Shortly afterwards a woman who lives in a cottage on•the new road near the priory observed flames in the stackyard, and promptly gave an alarm. The burning paper had been dropped at the corner of a stack of baled straw, and though it was difficult for the flames to spread quickly on account of the solid nature of the stack, it was deemed advisable to stimmon the Runcorn Fire Brigade, who extinguished the tire in compaeatively short time. About half the stack was. destroyed. The damage is about £2O, and is covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>_ _ ALLEGF,D WIFE DESERTIO'S AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>_ _ ALLEGF,D WIFE DESERTIO'S AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Monday, Sydney Herbert. was charged with leaving his wife chargeable to the common fund of the Union. Mr. Naylor, relieving officer, said that on the 13th inst., he was called to the prisoner's late address, and there found his wife in a very ill and desti- tute condition. Witness ordered her prompt removal to the Workhouse, as she was expecting confinement.— Mr. Knowles, who appeared for Herbert, said that he left his wife to look for work, and had since the alleged desertion, been in Glasgow and Maryport. As late as the sth inst. he hail sent her £l, this, he contended, being good filth that he had not deserted her. The prisoner had promised to defray all the expenses of the guardians.—The bench decided to adjourn the case for two weeks, and allowed bail in one surety of £lO arid two of .25. They hope at the end of that time to hear Mrs. Herbert's version of the affair.</p>
        <p>FAILURES 'IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to Kemp's Mercantile G tzette," the total nuieber of failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Fridiy, April 22nd, was 186, viz.:—New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 91; deeds of arrangement registered, 95. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 78 ; deeds of arrangement, 75 total 153; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 81, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1904, to date, of 149.</p>
      </div>
