      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>,T 7.9 C A, . 4 S</head>
        <p>,T 7.9 C A, . 4 S</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND MORN 11.11 LES SAILINGS.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company announce that, weather and circumstances permitting, their regular daily sailings for the season will commence on Saturday next, May 7th. As will he seen in the Company's announcement in another column, the favourite steamer" Snowdon" will take up the sailings from Prince's Landing Stage daily for Llandudno, Beaumaris, and Bangor (with a connection for Menai Bridge).</p>
        <p>I CoinMencing" Thursday, 10th May, the return daily service will be extended o Menai Bridge, and during I the Whitsuntide holidays there will be increased sailings by the palatial saloon paddle steamer "La Marguerite," leaving Liverpool daily for all places to Menai Bridge. 1 This well-known and popolacLondonsteamer is expected to arrive in the Mersey aboutthe 9th May,preparatory to ' taking up the Whitsuntide sailings, and, given anything like fine weather, the passenger-carrying capacity of this luxuriously-fitted steamer should be well tested by the travelling public. We understand there has been an exceptional demand for contract tickets this season' doubtless owing to the I advent of the new steamer, "La Marguerite," and as it I is possible the Company may have to restrict the issu- ing of transferable contracts, intending contractors , should book their tickets without delay. 1 The directors of the North Wales Company have appointed Captain John Young of the "St. Elvies," to Ibe master of 'La Marguerite." The "St. Tudnos" will, 1 ,as before, be Commanded by Captain William Smart, 1 who is so well and favourably known to the passengers 1 travelling by that steamer.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Union Castle Company, whose steamship Berwick Castle ran into and sank submarine Al, have given £250 towards the fund which is being raised at Portsmouth for the benefit of the relatives of the deceased crew.</p>
        <p>Last evening the Victoria infirmary Board accepted Messrs. Taylor's tender for the extension of the infirmary at a cost of .0,900. The money is being publicly subscribed, and the extension is Mid-Cheshire's memorial to Queen Victoria. At Wednesday's meeting it was reported that the infirmary was overcrowded, and it was resolved to commence the extension immediately. The scheme provides extra beds, the regulation air space for every patient, proper accommodation for nurses, operating theatre, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
