        <p>The visit of the IRISH POLITICS AND THE KING and Qvsl3l4 ROYAL VISIT. to Ireland affords</p>
        <p>. happy illustratioo of the natural warmth of Irish goodwill. Irishrael: have decided not to treat the visit as an affair 0' party politics, although some of their bodies have gravely argued that an address of welcorae from a public body to the Sovereign ought to be part of a bargain, and that Home Rule should be he equivalent. The expectation that the peobla will turn their backs on the KING and QuEiclv til the National Parliament is restored to them says little for the sense of humour in 3°l nationalist quarters. A perception of the vell situation, however, appears in the "Dublin Pall Independent." "In all the cities wherein the KING Will make formal entry," says that "be will be received by the elected repre" sentatives of the people with all the cerernol which betokens a correct sense of municiP3 dignity and respor.sibility, but without an at°t° of slavish adulation or loss of self respect." ought pot to be difficult. The ceremony is Pre; cisely the same in Ireland as it is in E igland, shu it is scarcely possible for any municipal dignitari in either island to compromise his self-respect 11, his manner of receiving the KING "No oh°, proceeds the Nationalist organ, "is less likely t° misapprehend or to draw false concloslol39 from the attitude of our people than is tb° Monarch whom they will acclaim as the of Ireland, and who, they pray, may be spare° to throw open the doors of the desecrated terriPl° of Irish legislative freedom." This is an ingenib° way of saying that the Irish enthusiasm for ti° King of Ireland is without prejudice to the eau° of Home Rule. It would be more to the purcOer to say that the Irish people see no reason lc); mixing up this loyalty with their polities. 13° the "Daily Independent" gets as near as it cab t'°3 this admission. The KING'S speeches in Irelsrle have nothing to do -with any desecrated terollid but they are full of practical hints. What Role° f needs is a constant stimulus to avail herself ° every means to better her industrial conditgl Her own energies should be fostered by exampie of the rest of the world. Nationalist politicians who are men of busine° are quite alive to this ;but that curious body, Irish Gaelic League, seems to cherish a vet' differeLt ambition. It has issued an estrl; ordinary leaflet warning Irishmen against Euglist, ideals. These, it seems, are base and contarnive,r ing. Irishmen must not dream of making thei,lo country prosperous, for prosperity means "0 "wealth and success" which, in England, ilf`lt, destroyed moial integrity and religious Poverty is the only safeguard for Irish inerfi'd and religion. The Irish patriot must be poor 013130 a student of old Irish literature. It nas9 110 necessary for him to acquaint himself wit% to, English language, despite its contaminating jilt! 13 ence, but he must give most of his time to 110 which will prove a blessed antidote. All the vriscl°.oo he needs lies in the ancient books of his Oti. country. He need not trouble himself ab°lls European literature, or give any of his preci°°a horns to learning French and German. In a the whole duty of the true Irishman is to cut 111-a self off from the modern spirit, and bury his hil°oo in a byegone monastic age. If Ireland coul3. shifted to another planet, this fantastic Pvcleba might be feasible, though, even then, it might iv awkward if there were no convenient foreigners jo the neighbouring island to buy Irish butter. it 00 surely a very uneasy notion of ratrictism t.I/0 nationality cannot be preserved except by ari / possible boycott, not against English ideals 8111,0 but against the whole outer world. Does 00 Gaelic League prefer to stop In amen from go.con to America? Or does it expect t he Aroeflo Irish to adopt its singular creed? It would.oct interesting to have Mr. REDMOND'S private oPini of this literary curiosity,</p>
      </div>
