        <p>On Tuesday, Sir WILLIAM 'THE COERCION ANSON introduced a re- OF WALES. markable Bill which.' is likely to be described, not 'without reason, as a Coercion Bill for Wales. The object of this measure is to liberate the machinery of the Education Act from the obstruction offered by the Welsh County Councils. The religious difficulty in Wales has been made mucn more acute by a statute which has raised trouble enough in England. By Welsh Nonconformists it is regarded as a fresh endowment of a Church which does not exactly dwell in the hearts of the people. 'They have met it in a spirit which is much more advanced than that of passive resistance. The County Councils have taken the extreme measure of proposing to tbevoluntary schools the alternative either of submitting to popular control or of being deprived of their share of the education rate.</p>
        <p>This is pretty' high-handed,but it is the attitude Of people who regard the Education Act as an act of war. They have been further embittered by the failure of efforts towards a compromise. The Bishop of St. Asaph showed an accommodating Spirit, but he failed to carry with him his episcopal colleagues in the Principality. On the other hand, the position taken up by the County Councils does riot command absolutely unanimous approval among Welsh Nonconformists. Some have asked why the children should sutler. If volun- tary schools are kept out of the money Which legally belongs to them, and if they refuse to submit to the control which is not in accordance with the law, the children will have no teaching at all. Sir WILLIAM A.NSON'S Bill is intended to preserve them from this predicament. It is provided that the school managers shalllincur all the necessary expenses and charge them to the common funds. The money will be deducted, if rtecessary,from the Parliamentary grant due to the local authority. As soon as the local authority agrees to carry out the law, the procedure will resume its normal course. This, again, is likely to be regarded in Wales as an act of War. The situation is beginning to bear an un- Pleasant resemblance to the long series of reprisals Which distinguished the year of coercion in Ireland. Local authorities there refused to carry out the law, and the Government resorted to devices Which made the law still more repugnant to the feelings of the people. It was not a struggle which reflected unblemished credit on either side, but it had the effect at last of persuading even the statesmen who held that what Ireland really needed was "resolute government for twenty Years" to try a different policy. There can be no doubt that if the present Government should fail to see the wisdom of modifying the Education Act, the responsibility will have to be undertaken by their successors. This must be plain even to politicians who con- demn the action of the Welsh County Councils. That springs from an exasperation which cannot be permanently coerced. Sir WILLIAM ANSON evidently expects the local authorities in Wales to give way and let the Board of Education conduct Its affairs in peace; but the conflict is too vital to be settled that way. Toe new Bill is simply a fresh provocation. It remains to be seen whether the proposals which the Bishop of St. Asaph means to submit to the House of Lords will restore the hope of a concordat; but even that will be an ex- plicit condemnation of thb Education Act, which has made the religious difficulty a thousandfold Wore than it was before.</p>
        <p>The Town Council meets again on Tuesday even- ing, and with no rating bugbear before them the members are expected to be in a more equable frame of mind. Alderman Bennett will return to the charge on behalf of the Education Committee and the teachers and propose 'swain the confirma- on of the minutes of March 21st hav- ing reference to the scale of teachers' 'salaries. It will be remembered that, at the meeting three weeks ago, the Council in a Wild attempt at economy refused to sanction th _scale. This was unquestionably a very. hasty and Imprudent action which, in all probability,has been regretted by its promoters. Economy In educa- tional expenditure cannot be effected without the greatest care and forethought, and for the 'Council to make an effort at saving money iby taking t from the teachers was a pie 39 ..of supreme folly. The administration of the whole educational system hinges on the efficiency •of the instruction given in the schools, and there can be no cheapening of labour here without the most disastrous effects. We hope, in faot we are •eu. re that Alderman Bennett will succeed this time in getting his motion confirmed.</p>
        <p>Councillor James Evans who moved the amend- ment at the last meeting of the Town Council Which led to the rejection of the scale of salaries lor teachers, explained at the meeting of the Edu- cation Committee on Monday that he did not understand that the scale was retrospective. This Was clearly a misapprehension as the Town Conn- 'oil confirmed the scale of salaries at a previous meeting, and agreed that it was to come into 'operation a.a from the Ist of April last year. When it is borne in mind that the 'Majority of the members of the Town Council have Seats on the Education Committee it would seem that the proper place to cut down the expendi- ture in detail is in the committee itself. It will 'be impossible for the Education Committee to get 'on with its business if members vote one way as members of the Education Committee and in a oontrtry way as members of the Town Council.</p>
        <p>At the meeting last month there was a lively 41scu98ion affecting the standard wage clause in 'Corporation contracts, and as a result of state- Ments made by Councillor Stott, a contract given to Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and: Son of Stourbridge -tas withdrawn. Since then, Councillor John .v'CranS, Cotincillor Stott and the Deputy Tow! 'e.lerk have visited the works of the firm in ques- two with the result:that the contract again appears O n the minutes of the Sanitary Committee. Presum- ably all was found correct and in compliance with 'Standing Orders. We trust that either the Chair- Than of the committee or Councillor Stott will vouchsafe some explanation on Tuesday, for it is hardly the Council's duty to doubt the word of respectable contractors, and then pry into their Illethods of business. It. is possible for the Stand- rig Orders to be too strictly observed.</p>
        <p>- The widening of Warrington Bridge by canti- levers will come before the Council once more, but there is no reason why any discussion should Il In the past the question has been loronghly threshed out, and now that the A-urough Surveyor has reported favourably on Mr. J. Webster's scheme it only remains for 01 uorporation to get on with the work. We are Riad to note that they are taking really forward ePs at last. The General Parposes Committee ee resolved that a specification be prepared and 10nder8 for the work advertised for, and this reso- ution only awaits confirmation at Tuesday's Conn- ;.g meetirig. It will be a great improvement when :4_o widening has been carried ont,for the carriage- .o3, is to be extended in width from 20ft. to fin., aud the footways from 4ft. to 10ft. This Brnould suffice for some considerable time—at any _ate sufficiently long to more than justify tho Voclerate expenditure involved. The luxury of a and new £20,000 or £30,000 bridge will have to ti(ls deferred until the rates are considerably less au 78. 108. in the 2.</p>
        <p>We note that the Gas Committee has acceptsd a tender for 43 "retort mouthpieces" at £2 10s. 6d. each. Ar 3 these for the personal use of members of the Town Council, we should like to enquire? If so we must certainly protest against what we consider an unnecessary expense. There is plenty of " gala" in the Council Chamber, though it is not used as an illuminant, and most of the members are in no need of " retort " mouthpieces. They have been sufficiently well endowed by nature to cope with the exigencies of the occasion. A few " retort courteous" mouthpieces might be of some value, however, in the course of an acrimonious debate.</p>
        <p>Owing to the lack of funds the soup kitchen at the Ragged. School is, like many of the forges and work-shops, closed down. For six weeks it has continuously performed a splendid work in reliev- ing the distress existing among the poor of the town, and Mr. Skelland and his co-workers de- serve the thanks of the community for their self- sacrifice. But it is not thanks these gentlemen ask for. They require hard cash in order to carry on this charitable office, and so far the people of the town have responded poorly to the appeals made to them. It will be a great pity if the soup kitchen has to remain closed for want of a little public support, and we trust the charitably in- clined will come to Mr.Skelland's assistance. They can rely upon it, that under his personal super- intendence not one far thing;will be wasted, nor re- lief given to the undeserving.</p>
        <p>Another link with the Warrington of long ago has been severed this week by the demolition of the Old Crow, an hostelry which has nestled for centuries behind Bridge-street. The house was approached by one of those narrow entries which are characteristic of our four main thoroughfares, and bear evidence of the building arrangements of a byegone age. The late Mr. Beamont in one of his interesting talks on old Warrington tells us that the Old Crow had seen better days,and that at one time it was the town mansion of Captain Crompton,of Crompton Fold and was called Crompton House. No date mark was found upon it, but the architecture and building materials suggest that the :house had at least weathered three centuries. The framework was entirely of oak, and in place of brickwork in the upper walls there was an admixture of chopped straw, twigs and daub. In those days buildings were evidently not erected for an age but for all tima, and that the Old Crow has seen several centuries is a tribute to the quality of the workmanship. To the local antiquarian the old house had some inherent interest. Could those rafters but speak they might tell us of many a stirring event in the good old town. They have now been levelled to the ground and all that remains to tell future genera- tions of the existence of the house is a photo- graph.</p>
        <p>Messrs. John and R. T. Fairclough and H. Roberts met the Rivers Committee recently to consider a letter from Mr. J. K. Bythell (chair- man of the Ship Canal Company) in which .he suggested a conference "with the view of arriving at a complete settlement of all the important questions involved and thus avoiding further pro- tracted legal proceedings." The communicAtion resulted in the Corporation and Traders stiffening their backs and they unani- mously agreed that the terms of the late Mr. Justice Byrne's judgment must be accepted by the Ship Canal Company as a condition prece- dent to the conference. This is, we think, the proper attitude to take. It is all very well for the Shio Canal Company to suggest conferences in which they have everything to gain and nothing to lose, and at the same time move Heaven and earth to avoid the judgment in question—a judg- ment too which was the result of the most exhaustive inquiry and deliberation by the late learned judge. This is Warrington's sheet anchor, and any shickening on our part will but give the Ship Canal Company an opportunity of disregard- ing their obligations to Warrington.</p>
        <p>Honour the brave! It is very easy to give vent to the sentiment. Will Warrington people be as ready to put it into practical effect ? The town has always been proud to identify itself with the famous "Fighting Fortieth," and it is but fitting that Warrington should possess a memorial to the men of the old regiment, who not only endured the rigours of South African warfare, but sacri- ficed life itself in the service of their country. In order to erect a statue of Lieutenant-,olonel MacCarthy O'Leary, who fell in the Pieter's Hill charge, £1,500 will have to be raised, and the sum already in hand totals a little over £6OO. Despite" hard times "many workingmen are showing a commendable willingness to contri bate their share. The Workingmen's Committee is putting forth every effort, and the co-operation of the employees at some of the largest works has been enlisted. Others, it is expected, will follow suit, although fifteen firms are still unrepresented. We hope the committee's second appeal will meet with a sympathetic reception from loyal War- ringtonians.</p>
        <p>The increase in the duty on tea is the worst feature in a Budget which bristles with points inviting criticism. It is bad because it infallibly means a reduction in the consumption of an article of universal diet. Not merely will the quantity consumed be less, but the quality will be of an inferior kind. When the duty was imposed during the war period in 1902, one of the largest tea firms in the country declared that the immediate effect was that the poorer class of customers, who had hitherto purchased tea at le. 6d.,turned to a lower- grade tea at is. 44. That the same tendency will be again noted cannot be doubted. While every householder at home is thus subjected to additional burdens, the very Government which proclaims its desire to develop the trade of the Empire at at costs has devised an effectual means of restricting one of the greatest of our industries in India and Ceylon.</p>
      </div>
