      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Disastrous Farm Fire.</head>
        <p>Disastrous Farm Fire.</p>
        <p>Parkside farm, Aston, near Preston Brook, which is in the occupation of Mr. Robert Shepperd, was the scene of a dis'astrous fire on Monday. Information of the outbreak was received at the Warrington Fire Station at 4-30 a.m., and the brigade at once turned out with the steam fire-engine Major, under the direc- tion of Deputy-Superintendent Turner. On arrival it was found that a large building, comprising shippons, stables and granary, was well alight. About 200 head of cattle and 20 horses had been removed in safety. A good supply of water was obtainable but a strong wind fanned the flames, and the struc- ture was almost gutted. Valuable machinery was des- troyed, and the damage is roughly estimated at about £2,000. Fortunately it is covered by insurance. The brigade were successful in their efforts to prevent the flames spreading to adjoining property. The Runcorn Fire Brigade arrived upon the scene about an hour after the Warrington men, but as their services were not required they returned to Runcorn. The War- rington contingent was at work until one o'clock in the afternoon, and did not return to the Fire Station until 2:30. The farm, which is one of the largest dairy farms in Cheshire, is Owned by Mr. Harvey Talbot.</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority. At. the Borough Police Court on Monday, George Atkinson was granted temporary authority to sell at the Manx Arms, School-brow, in lieu of Thomas Hatton.</p>
        <p>BASSINETTES ,AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>To-morrow the Sunday School Anniversary will be held at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. In the morn- ing and evening the Rev. J. 0-range Bennett, one of the Wesleyan Connexional Evangelists will preach, and in the afternoon, at 2.30, there will be a special musical service, at which anthems and hymns will be rendered, together with solos by Mrs. A. Booth, of Warrington, and Mr. S. Horton, of Leeds. The fact that the Rev. J. Grange Bennett was once a boy in the Silver-street Sunday School, and that his father, Mr. John Bennett (now of Padgate), was for a long time very closely connected with Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, lends a special interest to the services of to -morrow.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Yonge will preach at Wycliffe Church to-morrow (Sunday). morning and evening.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Minnie() will conduc—t a service the Parr Hall to-morrow (Sunday) evening and will preach his monthly sermon to yousig people. Knutsford Royal May Day Festival will take place on Monday and many new and attractive features are announced. The procession will leave the Town Hall at 2.15 p.m. The Warrington and District Property Owners' . Association will meet in Cairo-street school on Mon- day evening to consider what course to adopt in view of the increased rates. A sale of work in c)iin?ction with St. BArnabas' Church will be held in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club on Wednesday and Thursday. Lady Greenall will perform the opening ceremony on Wednesday afternoon. The proceeds will be devoted to the parochial funds. The annual meeting of the Warrington and District branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children will beheld at the Town Hall on Thursday, May 12th. Lynam May Queen. Festival will be held on Whit Thursday, May 20th.</p>
        <p>FOR CLA.SS AND CHINA. of every description try</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Discretion of Licensing Justices. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday, a resolution passed at a public meeting, was submitted, requesting the Council to petition Parliament against any interferenoe with the discre- tion of licensing justices. It was resolved that no action be taken in the matter.</p>
        <p>The Inquiry. The inquiry into the trading transactions of the Warrington Corporation has been brought to a close. The last sittings were held this week and it is anticip- ated that the Commissioners will draw up their report at an early date. Councillor Dodds appeared before the Commissioners on Monday, and answered a number of questions regarding the trading between members of the Council and the Corporation.</p>
        <p>Warrington Cemetery Report. Mr. Young, the Cemetery Ragistrar, has prepared his annual report for the year ending March 31st, 1904. The receipts amount to £1,401 Os. 5d.; c.pr- responding period last year, :c.:1,290 Bs. 10d. There have been interred during the past year 1,501 adults and minors, and 120 stillborn children and prema- ture ; corresponding number last year, 1,153 adults and minors, and 127 stillborn children and premature, showing an increase of 348 burials and a decrease of 7 stillborns, and an increase in receipts of :£l7O 16s. 7d. There have been 177 interments during the past year from the County Asylum, Winwick.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF Tostcs.-63, SINKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Band of Hope Festival. A very attractive programme has been arranged for the Band of Hope festival to be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday and Friday next. The principal feature will be the cantata "The Band of Hope Queen." Elsie Wright has been selected to take the part of the re- tiring queen. and Ethel Cooper will be the queen-elect. The coronation will, of course, be carried out with due ceremony, and there will be some pretty dances as well as stirring choruses. The miscellaneous part of the programme will be varied each evening, and ,will include songs, violin solos, drills,and humorous items Mr. It. A. Naylor will preside on Thursday evening and on Friday the chair will be occupied by Mrs. Simpson. Mr. A. Spruce will conduct, and Mr. F. Podmore, M.1.T111., and Master Clatworthy will pre- side at the organ. Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes. The ordinary monthly meeting of the Council was held on Tuesday, Mr, Ogilvie Duthie presiding. The report of the conference of the examiners in com- mercial subjects and of representative teachers from institutions sending up candidates for examination in those subjects together with the Examinations Com- mittee of the Council was presented. The CounCil had considered the fact that too many students took up isolated subjects, the subject so studied having no connecting link towards a, definite course. To this end the Council had framed definite courses of cog- nate subjects—eight groups in all—which would lead to a definite issue. It was also reported that the total number of papers applied for was 64,806, as against 55,751 last year. The number of exercises worked was 54,219, being 85 per cent., as against 46,121 last year, which was 82 per cent.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Wedding—Cheshire Associations.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Mr. Rdvc ard Egerton Leigh-White, eldest son of Egerton Leigh, D.L.,J.P., Che shire, of Jodrell Hall, Holmes Chapel, and West Hall High Leigh, both in Cheshire, and grandson of the' third Earl of Bantry (title extinct), with Miss A rethusa ' Hawker, eldest daughter of the late Mr. Peter Duff Hawker, of Longparish, Hampshire, took place on Wednesday in St. Paul's Church, London. The ser- rice was fully choral. The Rev. Neville Egerton Leigh officiated, assisted by the Rev. Prebendary Montagu Villiers, of St. Paul's Cathedral, and vicar of St. Paul's, and the Rev. J. Baden Powell, precentor of St. Paul's. Mr. R. Harrison was best man, and there were six bridesmaids in attendance. The re- ception held by Mrs. Hawker at the Hans Crescent Hotel, Chelsea, was largely. attended. Early in the afternoon 3,lr.and Mrs. E. L. Leigh-White left -London, en route for the Italian lakes, where the honeymoon will be spent. The presents numbered over 300. Marriage at Holy Trinity Church. There was a pretty wedding at Holy Trinity Church on Wednesday; the contracting parties being Mr. Archibald Marsball,third son of Mr. John Marshall, of Wakefield, and Miss Leonora Nolland, daughter of Mr. Robert Nolland, of Warrington. The Rev. A. Inglis officiated. The bride was becomingly attired in a pale grey voile dress, trimmed with white silk and guipure lace. She wore a grey chiffon picture hat and carried a bouquet of roses and lilies of the valley. Her ornaments were a pearl necklace,the gift of her `father,and a gold curb bracelet and diamond ring, the gifts of the bridegroom She was attended by two bridesmaids—Miss Elizabeth ' Jaques, of Doncaster, and Sliss Florence Arnold, of Warrington. Their dresses were of pale grey crepe- de-chine trimmed with silk and lace medallions, They , wore black picture hats and their bouquets were com- posed of marguerites and lilies of the valley. The bridegroom's gifts to the bridesmaids were gold brooches. Mr. T. Astill, of Warrington, discharged the duties of best man. The reception was afterwards held at the house of the bride's- father. Numerous presents were received. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Accident. Ann Kirkham, aged 57, residing in Plumpton-street, was admitted to the Infirmary yesterday morning suffering from a fractured skull, caused by falling downstairs. Personal. - -</p>
        <p>_ To-day Colonel Richardson, commanding the 8-40th Regimental Districts (Liverpool and Warrington) will inspect the Liverpool 'Volunteers. In view of Mr. James Pinion's approaching depar- ture from Belfast to take up the management of the Cheshire Lines Committee, a number of his personal friends marked their appreciation of his abilities as a railway manager, .and of the genial qualities which have made him so popular, by entertaining him on Friday evening to a complimentary dinner, at which occasion was availed of to present him with a hand- otne gold hunter watch.</p>
        <p>ASK for CIINNDIGHAMS HIGH-CLASS I4WERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Military Appointments. ' Second-Lieutenant P. R. Bland has resigned his commission in the Militia at Warrington, to which he was appointed on its second embodiment for South Africa in 1901. Captain H. N. Packer, of the War- rington .Militia, ! who served with Bethune's Mounted Infantry in the Boer war, has been selected for em- ployment with the Malay States Guides. BacE BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt</p>
        <p>Cup Winners, Reception at Warrington. Manchester City, the winners of the English Cup had an enthusiastic reception at Warrington on Mon- day evening on passing through the Central Station after their encounter with Everton. A large crowd assembled, and in response to repeated cheering Mr. Tom Maley held aloft the trophy, Meredith, the cap- tain of the team, whose goal gave the City the victory appeared at the carriage window and was greeted with much cordiality.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Mission Fund. The trustees of the Wesleyan Million Guinea Fund are proposing to invite architects to submit prelimin- ary designs for the erection of the Wesleyan Methodist Hall and Connexional Offices at Westminster. The buildings will cover an area of about 35,000 square feet, and are to be of a monumental character, and worthy of the important position they will occupy, i but n view of the special requirements of the Wesleyan Church, no plans of Gothic design will be considered. The amount proposed to be expended is £120,000, exclusive of the cost of the site. The trustees have practically settled the terms for the letting of their adjacent surplus lands on the Aquarium site.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Wesleyan Synod. The annual synod in connection with the Liverpool District of the Wesleyan Methodist Church will be held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church on May 10th 11th and 12th. An interesting series of public meet ings has been arranged, commencing with a temper- ance meeting in Bewsey-road Church on Monday- May 10th, to be addressed by well-known speakers. On Tuesday, May 11th, the Rev. Henry Burton, M.A., D.D., of Lytham, will preach the official ser- mon in Bold-street Church, and the following even- ing there will be meetings at Bewsey-road, Penketh and Padgate. On Thursday, May 12th, a meeting on behalf of the Liverpool Mission will be held in Bewsey-road Church.</p>
        <p>Child Fatally &amp;laicled. An inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Tuesday evening by Coroner Brighouse, touching the death of Frederick Jones, aged a year and eleven months, son of William Jones, brick-setter. Marsh- house-lane. The evidence showed that on the 18th inst. deceased's mother had filled a can with hot tea, and was putting the teapot back on the hob when deceased pulled the can over. The contents were spilled on the child's right arm and side, but after the injuries had been dressed deceased appeared to be progressing satisfactorily. On Monday his condition became so serious that Dr. Anderson was summoned, but death took place before his arrival.—A. verdict of "Accidental death" was returned Local Failures.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning at the Warrington County Court, on the application of Mr. T. S. Steel, a receiv- ing order was made on the debtor's _ potition against Edmund Green Barratt, now residing at No. 63, Hall- street, Warrington, but form;lrly residing in Longford- street, and carrying on business at a stall in the Market Hall, as a cake and biscuit merchant. The debtor was also :adjudicated :bankrupt.—A. receiving order has been made in the County Court on a credi- tor's petition against Edwin Page, general dealer, of Longford-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and Snow, UNDER the CLOCK, Bain GE-STREET.—Ad7t. The Volunteer Active Service Companies.</p>
        <p>The members of the active service companies sup- plied by the local Volunteer Battalions during the South African campaign held their third annual hot- pot supper and smoking concert at the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Saturday evening. In addition to a large number of non-commissioned officers and men of the Ist Battalion, stationed at Warrington, there were in attendance a number of service men belonging to the 2nd Battalion, and hailing from St. Helens and Widnes. Among the officers present were Major B. Fairclough, Major F. M. Appleton, Sergeant-Major Rawlinson, and Sergeant-Major Delmage. The proceedings proved highly enjoyable and successful.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS AfINER WATERS.—Advt. Asylum Inmates Entertained. _</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a concert was given at Winwick Asylum, by " TheStamdara Concert Party." An attractive programme had been arranged, including glees by "The Standard Glee Party," Messrs. J.Dooley, W. C. Brooks, F. Warburton' and B. Bate, _which merited great applause. Miss Nellie Armstrong was heard to advantage in "The Flight of Ages" and "Ever of thee." Miss Win* Brooks (contralto), did herself justice in " The Gipsy Duet, the part of Mani ico being ably sustained by Mr. W. C. Brooks. Mr. Dooley rendered "Mona" in capital style, and Mr. F. Warburton" The Village Blacksmith." Mr. B. Bate was in good form, and won much favour by his rendering of " My beloved Queen," Mr. T. Mannion contributed two cornet solos in a praiseworthy manner. The humorous part .of the programme was supplied by Mr. W. Fidler and Mr. Joe Haines and Miss Earle, of Manchester. Mr. Bert Smith was the accompanist and ,was responsible for a well-played pianoforte solo. The band of the Asylum contri- buted two overtures.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BEFIT. UNDER tll3 CLOCK tor BEST BOOTS in LANCASHIRN.—Advt.</p>
        <p>I.L.P. Social. Under the auspices of the Warrington Independent Labour Party, a social was held in the Vnitarian schoolroom Cairo-street, on Thursday evening to com- memorate the election of three of the memlters to public positions. There was a good attendance of members and friends. The proceedings were pre- sided over by Mr. S. Wright (sen.), who welcomed the visitors and referred to the steadily increasing member ship of the party and its growing influence over party politics. Brief addresses were given by Councillor Stott, Mr. James Ball, elective auditor and Mrs. Wilkinson' gilardian of Orford Ward. Refreshments were served out by an efficient staff under the super- vision of Mr. Warburton the branch secretary. Songs were rendered during the evening by Messrs. Wright A. Pemberton, and Foden and a violin solo by Miss, Weaver. Dancing and games were indulged in and a pleasant evening was brought to a close by the singing of the well known Socialist song "England arise." Mr. W. PemberOn made an excellent pianist.</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. H. 141. ASHTON'S LIFE Tone is the Knco or TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. The Coroner and Burning Fatalities. - -</p>
        <p>An inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Monday evening by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, touching the death of Mary Jane Rustage, aged seven years, 43, Heath-street, Stockton Heath.—The evidence showed that on Friday evening deceased went into the house of the next door neighbour, Mrs. Beswick, i who °was out. The fire was very low, and n order to make it burn more brightly deceased placed a sheet of paper before it. The paper became ignited and set fire to her clothing. She rushed into the street screaming and, with difficulty, her mother ex- tinguished the flames. It was stated that the fire was entirely unprotected. The fire irons &amp;c., had ,been cleaned and put :away.—The Coroner alluded to the large number of deaths from burning. More people, he said, were burnt .to death in six months than were killed at level crossings in twenty years. The per- centage of deaths from burning was greater than from any other cause, and they might all be avoided with a little care. Mrs. Remick stated that when she left the fire unprotected, she never expected such an accident happening. The Coroner said it was generally the unexpected that happened.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MEETING OF LOCAL TRADESMEN.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF LOCAL TRADESMEN.</p>
        <p>THE EFFECT OF THE BUDGET ON</p>
        <p>PRICES.</p>
        <p>Corporation Expenditure Discussed.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>The m3mb3rs of the Warringtonaud District Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association,and the Tobacconists' Association discussed matters affecting their re- spective trades last night at the Horsemarket-street Cocoa Rooms and afterwards held a joint meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>GROCERS AND CiIEAP FARES ON MARKET DAYS. •</head>
        <p>GROCERS AND CiIEAP FARES ON MARKET DAYS. •</p>
        <p>The meeting of grocers and provision dealers was presided over by Mr. E, Richardson. Ilr. Gorst (the secretary) reported that he had received two letters from the railway companies, in reply to his communica- tion asking them to issue tickets to Warrington on market days from neighbouring districts at reduced fares.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Committee wrote :—" With refer- ence to your letter re market tickets from local stations to Warrington, arrangements have now been made for the issue of cheap tickets on Wednesdays and Satur- days to Warrington from the followinc, stations: Cadis- head, Irlam, Glazebrook, Farnworth: Widnes, Hough Green and Halewood.</p>
        <p>Mr. I. T. WILLIA.MS, of the London and North Western Railway Company wrote: I am obliged for your favour of the 14th instant with reference to cheaper tickets from Frodsham, Helsby, and Widnes on Market days and urn having inquiries made with a view to seeing what can be done in the direction indicated. • THE MEETING OF TOBACCONISTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'JOSEPEI BES WICK presided at the meeting of ocal tobacconists. There was a representative lattendance.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMA.:si announced that they were met, to consider prices in view of the Budget proposals. Unfor- tunately, manufacturers had not sent in their revised price lists and it would be difficult for them to come to a definite decision on all points until they knew what the manufacturers intended to do. He did not think any difference could be ,:made to workingmen in the price of nailrod, although the tobacconists would have to pay more for it. To increase the price would practically kill the trade in heavy tobaccos. There were, however, other directions in which they might make slight advances. He would be glad to hear an expres- sion of opinion respecting the price of cigarettes. Mr. CHARLES J. NEWTON (hon. secretary) pointed out that the cigarettes which were formerly sold in Warrington in packets of 10 for 3d. were now selling at 2:4d. The wholesale price was bound to go up in view of the increased duty and he thought the present an opportune time for tobacconists to return to the old price of 3d. .</p>
        <p>This view met with the approval of all present, and a resolution was unanimously carried raising the price of ten a packet cigarettes from 2W. to 3d. Mr. NEWTON suggested that as the grocers were equally affected by the rise in the price of tobacco representatives of the Grocers' Federation who were on the premises, might be asked to attend the meeting This was agreed to and a joint meeting was held, pre- sided over by Mr. Joseph Beswick. The decision with regard to ten a packet cigarettes was confirmed, and a provisional uniform trade price was agreed upon for cheap cigarettes and heavy tobacco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PROPOSED DEPUTATION TO THE CORPORATION.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED DEPUTATION TO THE CORPORATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. KAY said that the grocers had been trring to form a deputation to wait upon the town authorities with regard to the enormous expenditure going on at the present time. Another sixpence was to be added to the rates this year and probably something more later on.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN; It is very easy to spend other folk's money, I shall be pleased to join you in a de- putation. Mr. KAY: We have syritten'to kindred associations in the town with this end in view. Mr. RICHARDSON We think it advisable to impress upon the members of thc Council the necessity of keeping a watch on the estimates. These estimates are largely in the hands of officials and we think the committees have failed to give that rigid attention]to and strict regard for the require- ments of the town that they ought to have done. Mr. Gorst : They have no right to charge such a high price for gas. It is really taking the money out of our pockets and giving a portion or it back again. The CIIIIFtMAN: You should have electric light. It costs about twice as much. (Laughter.) The Cor- poration are so generous in raising the salaries of officials who do not ask for advances. (Hear, hear). Mr. RICHARDSON : We are paying 3s. id. per thou- sand feet for gas in Warrington against 2s. in Widnes. The £B,OOO profit given in relief of rates is really taken out of the ratepayers' pockets. The matter then dropped. It was decided to hold an adjourned meeting at an early dzte to further consider the price of tobacco when the revised lists of the manit-. facturere have been. receive{i,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>VALUATION OF PROPERTY. • ProposecTlieforins.</head>
        <p>THE VALUATION OF PROPERTY. • ProposecTlieforins.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday. Mr. Long introduced, under the ten minutes rule, the Govern- ment's Valuation Bill. Remarking that they were committed in all quarters of the House to a reform of local taxation, he said it would be impossible to reform the present system unless they first dealt with the law which governed the valuation of property. In the Bill the Government had adopted with some slight modifica- tions the recommendations of the Royal Commission on Local Taxation. The Commission recommended the application to the rest of the country, with certain alter- ations, of the London system, which had worked with considerable success, and the Government proposed to act on that recommendation. What they wanted was that there should be in the first place a single authority for valuation and assessment purposes, instead of the 'numerous authorities which now existed, and, in the second place, that there should be uniformity of valua- tion. They proposed that the valuation anthlrity should be the County Council,and in the case of county boroughs the County Borough Council. In order to secure uniformity they proposed to associate with the valuation authority the surveyor of taxes. That official wild have the right to sit and to vote with the authority. The valuation list would be submitted to him, and if he had reason to believe that the valuation was an improper one be would be en- titled to make his, own valuation, which would stand unless it could be shown that it was unjust. Under the London Act there was a scale of deductions to he made by the valuation authority from the gross estimated rateable value. That was slightly altered in this bill, though in practice there would be really no alteration, because, although the deductions included in the London Act weraintended to be maxima, in practice they had been adopted as general reductions. The Hone would agree that it was desirable that the deduc- tions to be made should be general, and should be established by the House rather than left to the discre- tion of the local authorities. The Government proposed to adopt the London system as it had been established in practice, and they had put into the Bill a schedule of deductions to be made, with power to the valuation authority to vary the deductions in spacial cases where some exceptional reason could be shown. THE POSITION OF THE WARRINGTON CO M ITTFI E.</p>
        <p>Yesterday,a reporter of the "Examiner" waited upon Mr. J. C. Sutton, the Clerk to the \Varrington Assess- ment Committee, with the object of ascertaining his views on the subject. In answer to inquiries, he said he had only just received a cony of the Bill and had not had time to consider it fully. He was therefore not in a position to express an opinion upon it. A confer- ence of representatives of Assessment Committees throughout Lancashire, he added, was to be held next week at Manchest2r, when the Bill would, no doubt, be fully considered.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jor.T.Ev, the Chairman of the War- ington Union Assessment Committee, was also waited upon. He stated that he had not seen the Bill, but from Mr. Long's statement in the House of Commons it appeared to him that the Government were en- deavourinmb to make the London system of valuation general throughout the country. Until he had read the provisions'oflthe Bill, he NVIS unable to say what effect the Bill would have on the Warrington Union Assess- ment Committee. He was awaiting with interest to see what the positions of Boards of Guardians and Overseers were under the new Bill. Until then he could not express an opinion on the merits or demerits of the Bill.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>OVAL</p>
        <p>RICH TEA</p>
        <p>BISCUITS. (In ilb. Packets, &amp;c.)</p>
        <p>THE CRAWFORD" QUALITY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PUDDLER'S SHOCKING DEATH. Found Under the Furnace at Dallam Forge.</head>
        <p>A PUDDLER'S SHOCKING DEATH. Found Under the Furnace at Dallam Forge.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>An under-hand puddler named George Charles Archer, 19 years of age, of Stamford-street, met with a terrible death whilst following his smployment at the works of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles at Dallam early on Wed- nesday morning. The circumstances were inquired into by Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner and a jury at the Police Station yesterday. Mr. F. G. Taylor, solicitor, represented Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, and Mr. Clarke, inspector of factories, watched the proceedings on behalf of the Home Office.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that from the police report it ap- pearedjthat deceased left home at a quarter to five on Wednesday and went to work. He charged his furnace about five o'clock, and nothing further was seen of him until 5.30. Patrick Conway, who was passing the fur- nace, then saw the deceased lying in the ash-hole. He was dead when got out, having sustained fearful burns. The supposition was that deceased, who was subject to fits, was in the act of stirring the furnace fire when he was seized with a At and fell forward into the ash-hole. Ttiomas Archer, 1, Stamford-street, said the deceased was his nephew and resided with him. When he set out for the works on Wednesday he was in his usual good health. To witness's knowledge, deceased had had two fits, one about two months ago, and the other before Christmas. He was seized with them suddenly and they lasted about ten minutes. He was not at work when the seizures took place. William Smith, puddler, 3, Erwood-street, said deceased came to work at five o'clock on Wednesduy morning and charged his heat of pig-iron as usual. At 5.30 witness was told that deceased was in the ash-hole, and he assisted in getting the body out. Patrick Conway, blacksmith's striker, 39, Dolman's- lane, stated that at 5.30 he was at the rear of No. 2 furnace and saw deceased lying in the ash-hole. He at once obtained assistanceand pulled him out. Deceased was fearfully burnt and on examination was found to be dead.</p>
        <p>In answer to the Coroner, witness said the rmigh dimensions of the furnace were 2ft. 4ins. high by 3ft. wide. He had never heard, until after the accident, that deceased was subject to fits. The Coroner: It is only human nature that no ;one would tell the masters even if they knew deceased was subject to fits. (To Mr. Taylor) : Tf your clients had known they would not have employed him. Mr. Taylor: They would certainly not have had him in such a dangerous position. The first witness says deceased never had a fit in the works.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that if deceased had had epileptic fits—and the evidence anpeared to show that he had—the workmen would probably not only not tell the employea3 but they would carefully conceal that knowledge from them. In face of the Compensation Actemployers would naturally be averse to employing any man in that class of work if they knew that he was subject to fits. If the jury assumed that deceased was not subject to fits, the question was whether the furnace was a dangerous place or not. None, however, suggested that it was. Taking into consideration that the deceased had previously had two seizures, he thought everything pointed to the fact that he had another'seizure and was unable to rescue himself from the danger. Mr. Whittle (foreman of the jury) said it appeared to him that deceased must have had‘a seizure, or it would have been an utter impossibility for him to fall into the ash-hole.</p>
        <p>At the request of the jury, deceased's father, who re- sides at Darby, was called and said he had never known deceased to have fits. Fie had not lived with him for nine years. Samuel Davies, puddler, said deceased was his under- hand. He had never known him to have a seizure whilst at the works, but he once saw him in a fit at home. The Coroner: Why didn't you tell your masters ? Wasn't it a very dangerous thing to have as under-hand a man who had seizures like these?</p>
        <p>Witness said deceased WAS a strong youth and never appeared to " all anything." A verdict of " Accidental deathl" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>IMPROVED PASSENGER TRAIN SERVICE.</head>
        <p>IMPROVED PASSENGER TRAIN SERVICE.</p>
        <p>We have been favoured with an advance copy of the improved passenger train alterations which the London &amp; North Western Railway Company intend carrying out in this district on and from the Ist May. It will be gratifying to the traders of Warrington and adjacent stations to learn that the necessity cf the Fleetwood boat train stopping at Warrington has at last been recognised. For some 'years past repeated applications have been made by our Chamber of Commerce and others for this much needed accommodation to be given, but all in vain ; nothing but a negative answer could be elicited much to the disappointment and annoyance of the many whose business transactions call for frequent journey* the London, but happily the difficulties have now been overcome and henceforth it may be regarded as an accomplished fact. The dining car express, timed to leave Einton at 5.35 p.m, will start at 5.30 p.m, call only at Crewe and arrive Warrington at 9.9 p.m, "the Ispeed throughout being accelerated so as to allow of the journey being done in the short time of 3 hours 39 minutes. IThis improvement combined with the new 'express service to London in the morning—which took effect in February last—places Warrington traders in a position equal to those of Liverpool and Manchester, so far as London is concerned. Stated brieily, passengers can leave Warrington 8.10 a.m., have breakfast en route, arrive London 12 noon,leave thereon the returrijOnthey at 5.30 p.m.—a dining car being attached to the train— and reach Warrington 9.9. thus performing the double journey within 13 hours and allowing 31 hours in Lon- don for business purposes. The new luncheon car ' express for Manchester and Liverpool which leaves Ruston at 12.10 noon connects at Crewe with a train leaving at 3.20 p.m. and due at Warrington at 4.15. On the 12.15 noon train from Easton there is a through coach to Blackburn which can be used to Warrington. These are excellent services and will give immense satisfaction,. not only to Warrington but to many residing at Latchford, Thel- wall, Lyncra, Broadheath, St. Helens, Earlestown, &amp;c. The alterations also afford a greatly improved service from Warrington Co Preston, Fleetwoodand Ireland and will likewise remove the loud complaints from pas- sengers returning by the nightboat from Belfast who at present have to change trains at Wigan, where, after waitingifor over an hour:in the early morning,they come forward by a crawlintr train and change at Newton. In future the Belfak Boat Express will call at Warrington to set down passengtrs from Ireland. We are very pleased to observe that further improve- ments are to be made in the low-level service to Man- chester. The 2-20 p.m. train, which at preseat runs to Broadheath only, is to be extended to Manchester and this will provide a convenient afternoon service to that City from the suburban stations and will also be very convenient daring the cricket season at Old Trafford. We observe, too, that an additional train is to leave Man3hester at 2-55 p.m. for Broadheath, Lynam, Thel- wall, Latchford and Warrington. These alterations together with those which were carried out a couple of months ago shew that the London and North Western Railway Company are now keenly alive to the needs of their customers in this district and we heartily con- gratulate them upon the improvements they have made. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday. the thanks of the meeting were tendered to Mr.T. Williams district goods manager of the London and North-Western Railway Company for his continued efforts to improve the train service to and from War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WEKS COMPETITION FINAL. Fletcher's v. Rylands's.</head>
        <p>WEKS COMPETITION FINAL. Fletcher's v. Rylands's.</p>
        <p>The Works Football Competition in aid of locel charities was brought to a close last evening, greet interest being centred in the contest between Ryland's and Fletcher's teams. About 1,009 spectators assembled and the players were encouraged by the cheers of a large body of their fellow work:nen. Mr. W. Nevins was the referee and the teams lined up as follows Fletcher's : Cirtwright., Blackshaw, Barry and Tsher- wood ; Kay. Fleming, P. Knight, Boardman, Walsh, Holbrook, Hardy, and Turner.</p>
        <p>Rylands's: Edelsten;Wright. Morris,and Allen :Floyd and Stnithaleesom,Simister,Mason.Phillips,fligham and Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>Fletcher's attacked from the start, and passing among the three-quarters seemed certain to end in a score,but Berry failed to psss to Isherwood at the critical moment. After further pressure by Fletcher's, one of Rylands's half backs failed to field the ball, and gave the wireworkers an opening. Fleming obtained posses- sion and transferred to Berry, who dashed over at the corner amid excitement. The same player failed at goal. Rylands's now assumed the aggressive and scrimmaging took place near the line. Kay got the ball and tried to relieve the pressure by kicking down the field. Edelsten however, was on the alert and made a mark. Morris succeeding with the goal kick. Play continued to wage in the engineers' quarters but several promising passing movements came to nothing. Half time arrived with the score:—Fletcher's one try (three points) ; Rylands's one goal (two points.) Rylands's maintained the attack in the 'second half but did not make the best of their opportunities, and Fletcher's were fortunate in running out winners by a single point. At the close of the match Mr. J. J. Nield, president of the Warrington Football Club, presented gold medals to the winners and silver medals to the runners-up. He expressed regret at the poor attendances of the public during the competition, in consequence of which they would only be able to hand over a smell amount to local charities. Trade had not been of the hest and other circumstances had militated against the success of the competition. Mr. J. L. Davenport, on behalf of Messrs, Eustance and Company, then presented the handsome silver cup given by the firm to the champions of the Works League which is a new venture this season. Mr. Marshall accepted the trophy on behalf of the Locos who had ecured the top position by a victory over Rylands's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MR. H. ROBERTS J.P., AND THE "GUARDIAN." -- Criticism of the Paper's Policy.</head>
        <p>MR. H. ROBERTS J.P., AND THE "GUARDIAN." -- Criticism of the Paper's Policy.</p>
        <p>".PROST/TUTED TO UNWORTHY ENDS."</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of Mackie and Co., Limited, was held on Monday, Mr. W. McNeil, Crewe, presiding. He moved adoption of report, which was criticised at con- siflerable length by Mr. H. Roberts. Speaking upon what he described as a somewhat personal matter, Mr. Roberts denounced the policy of the paper in regard to the recent agitation. in. strong terms. The editor had refused to insert replies in some instances, in others he had omittedphrases without just cause, and been so unfair as to disgust many people who expected better things and juster treatment of public men who had devoted many years' energies to further- ing the public weal. It had been regrettable that any respectable paper should have taken up the attitude which the "Guardian" had done. Men who had deserved well of their fellow towns- people by reason of the sacrifices they had made for the public good such as Alderman Monks and the late Alderman Shaw Green, had been driven into private life by those who had lent themselves to the rag-tag and bob-tail with, it might be, a few exceptions. Somt, of them, he and his brother magistrates had had to deal with on the bench. He was certain that Alderman Shaw Green's death had been hastened and his last days embittered by the agitation and the statements which had been published in the 'Guardian." He had been assured that the attitude of the " Guardiar "itself had not been dictated by the direc- tors, and he objected to the capital and resources of the company being prostituted to unworthy ends. There had been strong personal bias against himself in the tone of the articles, and fairplay had been withheld from correspondents and others who wrote on his side of the question, and this had enstranged many old sup- porters of the paper. He was in a position to prove that the Company had lost advertisements and other orders owing to the attitude they had taken up on this ques- tion. It was only right that the policy of the piper should have the acquiescence of the directors, and he trusted the bad policy which he had mentioned of mak- ing the most of one-sided and ex-parte statements, and heaping insult and dishonour upon those who deserved better things, would henceforth be put an end to. He intended to go back into public life if his health re- mained as it was at present. He did not want to go to his grave with a dishonoured name. The dearest thing to him was an unblemished character before his fellow men. (Applanse.) The balance sheet was eventually adopted. The three retiring directors—Messrs. T. B. Moreton, W. C. Baines and W. Skelton—were re-elected, as were Messrs. Cooper and Cooper, of Bolton, as auditors. An extraordinary general meeting was then held, and a special resolution giving power to the company to reduceits capital was passed unanimously, after con- siderable discussion, in which Alderman Bennett,Messrs. L. Greening, H. Roberts, C. D. Parkinson, and E. Payne took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON fOWN COUNCIL. Business of the Next Meeting. A meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening, when the business will include the following matters.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON fOWN COUNCIL. Business of the Next Meeting. A meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tues- day evening, when the business will include the following matters.</p>
        <p>THE STANDARD WAGE QUESTION. At a meeting of the Sanitary Works Committee, the Chairman reported that he had withdrawn from tho Council minutes the resolution accepting the tender of Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Son for galvanised pails on account of statements made at the Council meeting to the effect that Messrs. Rhodes did not pay the rate of wages recognised in the district.—lt was resolved that the Chairman and Deputy Town Clerk be instructed to v:sit the works of Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Son, at Stourbridge, to consider and report upon the whole question. The report of the Chairman,. Mr. Councillor Stott, and the Deputy Town Clerk on their visit to the, works of Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Son, of Stourbrklge, with reference to the question of the rate of wages paid by that firm to their workpeople was read, and after dis- cussion thereon, it was resolved that the tender of Messrs. Thomas Rhodes and Son, to supply 500 galvan- ised steel pails at 4s. 41d. each, and 500 ashbirts ILL 4s. lid, each, be accepted.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER LOAN FOR PRIVATE STREET WORKS. • The Paving and Sewerage Committee have resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to apply to the Local Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of .C 6,000 for private street works.</p>
        <p>A FIYE TON CRANE. Acting on the report of a deputation, consisting of Councillor John Roberts, Councillor John Evans and the Gas Engineer, it is recommended that the tender)t Messrs. J. 11. Wilson and Company, of Liverpool, for the supply of a 5 ton crane at £5lO be accepted. COMPENSATION FOR DISTCRBANCE. The Water Committee recommend that the sum of £175 be paid to Mr. C. W. Davenport, which sum he has agreed to accept in settlement of his claim for loss and disturbance during the erection and use of the tem- porary plant at Delph-lane Pumping Station and by tha works carried out by other contractors. THE DISMISSAL OF TRAMWAY EMPLoYEES. At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee, the Tramways Manager reported that he had given one week's notice to terminate the services of 19 employees in accordance with the instructions of the committee. It was resolved that each man so dis- missed receive one week's extra pay. The committee considered the electrical engineer's report on the "one man "cars which was not favourable to the introduc- tion of such ears, It was resolved that, the matter Pty. left in the han'll of the engineer for further inquiry ; that a charge of not less than ..£1 10s. per week be made for the use of advertisements in the cars .not exceeding two in number and not exeeeding 181 n. by 22in. in size. The Town Clerk reported that the Deputy Town Clerk and the Tramways Manager had visited Manchester, Liverpool and Salford and other places with the object of finding employment for the men dismissed from the Corporation service. It was, resolved that they be authorised to visit Blackpool and other places with the like object. The Town Clerk reported that he had been in communication with the Board of Trade with re- spect to the claim of the Postmaster-General, and that it might be necessary to appear before the board by Counsel. It was resolved, that the Town Clerk be authorised to take such steps in the matter as he may consider advisable. The Town Clerk submitted draft specification prepared by Messrs.Preece and Carriew for the construction of light railway No. 1, which was approved. It was resolved that the Paving and Sewer- age Committee be empowered to make and pave the new footpaths required in Old London-road.</p>
        <p>THE SUSPENSION OF STANDING ORDERS. The TOWN CLERK laid before the General Purposes Committee a statement with regard to the suspension of the Standing Orders during the last ten years, from which it appeared that the Standing Orders had been suspended on 15 occasions; 13 times by special notice of motion, and twice only without notice, but on both occasions for the purpose only of obtaining the sanction. of the Council to a proposed increase of salary which was specially mentioned in the Council Summons.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR MAG INN AND TENDERS. ' Councillor Maginn has given notice of the following motions: That it be an instruction to each committee of the Corporation when they have obtained tenders for goods, or for labour regaired, or both, to accept the lowest tender, except only in cases where any person tendering clrries on business within the Borough and in which the committee for special reasons may consider it advisable not to accept the lowest tender, and that in accepting any tender each committee shall require the persons tendering to give the narrses of two responsible sureties who shall guarantee the due execution of the contract. That the Standing Orcters be amended so as to give effect to the above resolution.</p>
        <p>A STOKER'S WAGES. Mr. Councillor Stltt has given notice of the follow- ing motion :—That in the opinion of this Council the wages paid to the stoker appointed for the Winwick Pumping Station in March last are not sufficient for the number of hours he is employed per week, and that the Water Committee should immediately consider the ques- tion of an advance of wages being given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP FOR WARRINGTON BOYS. Negotiations bra New Site.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP FOR WARRINGTON BOYS. Negotiations bra New Site.</p>
        <p>We understand that the hon. secretary (Air-.TV-. H. J. Jenkins), after considerable inquiry and negotiation, is likely to have the privilege of using this year's summer camp for Warrington boys a splendid piece of land at Prestatyn near Rhyl, which is in every way suitable for camping purposes. The land forms part of the estate of Lady 11cLaren,wife of Sir Charles McLaren,Bart.M.P.,of Bodnant,Denbighshire. The proposed site for the camp has a magnificent frontage to the sea, with ample facilities for sea bathing. Immediately adjoining is a large tract of common land, standing high and dry, and in every way suitable for cricket, football and other outdoor games. As soon as the introductory negotia- tions are complete. Mr. Jenkins will bring the matter before the local committee, whom we have every reason to think, will be highly gratified at the success which has attended Mr. Jenkins' efforts to find a suitable site or this year's children's camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL CO.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL CO.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivers Committee on the 25th April, Mr. R. T. Fairclough, the representative of the Corporation on the Upper Mersey Navigation Corn mission, reported on the last meeting of the Corn missioners "That the Commissioners would ask for clause to be inserted in the Manchester Ship Canal Bil relieving them from responsibility for the navigation of the river between Monks, Hall and Company's works and their boundary by Crostleld's Wharf."</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER SHIP CAN&amp;L BILL. The Manchester Ship Canal Bill is down for hearing on Wednesday, May 4. The Corporation of Warringtoa and the traders will appear in support of their petition against the Bill. Mr. Hughes M. will represent the orporation</p>
      </div>
