      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NOW IS THE TIME.</head>
        <p>NOW IS THE TIME.</p>
        <p>Do you enjoy perfect health? If ncst. uhy not? The simplest way fort man to secure immunity from disease is to follow, as far as practicable, the laws of nature, and if illness overtakes him, to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The only natural remedies are vegetables, and the chief vegetable remedy of the present age is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic. G wILYM EvA.NS'_ QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a preparation now -extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from debility, nervousness and general ex- haustion, and if any value be attached to human testi- mony, the efficacy of this medicine is established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical pro- fession, and others, and corroborated by the written testimonials of eminent men. OUGHT TO BE IN EVERY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contains not only a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well known herbs:—sarsapar- illa, saffron, gentian, lavender, burdock, and dandelion. At this season of the year no one should be without Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Best Remedy of the Age for various ailments. GWILY.II EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is recommended for indigestion in its different forms, such as sick head- aches, heartburn, cramp, flatulency, sense of fulness and oppression after eating, drowsiness,•and pains, in the region of the heart. It is, without exception, the best remedy for depression of spirits and melancholia. Being a vegetable" Pick-me-up," it is strongly recom- mended for nervous diseases, such as undue anxiety, de3pondency, fainting fits, neuralgia, and nerve pains generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>STRONGLY RECOMMENDED.</head>
        <p>STRONGLY RECOMMENDED.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has successfully treated (after all other known preparations have failed) severe cases of affections of the chest, such as common colds, bronchitis, asthmatic colds, shortness of breath,influenza, ttc. It purifies and enriches the blood. It is a healthy and safe tonic. Should be taken by all who are delicate, as it strengthens the system and promotes good health. People suffering great weakness, either after an illness, .or through long confinement in ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause, find Gwilyra Evans' Quinine Bitters most valuable as a great strength reviver. GWILYM EV.4lgS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each or will be sent, on re- ceipt of stamps, carriage free, direct from the Sole Pro- prietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. BEWARE OF IMITATIONS.</p>
        <p>When you ask for Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, see that you get it, with the name " Gwilym Evans" on the label, on the stamp, and on the bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>DECEPTION LEriDS TO IMPRISONMENT.</p>
        <p>The Warrington magistrates heard on Wednesday morning an extraordinary case of obtaining food, lodgings, and clothing, to the value of £l, by false pre- tences. The prisoner was Jenny Owen, whose husband Richard Owen, has not been heard of since the beginning of the month.—She pleaded not4guilty.—The Chief Con- stable (Mi. Luke Talbot) pointed out that the parties lived at Wigan at Christmas-time, but since then they had been tramping about the district. The prisoner was the daughter of an ex-inspector of police, who lived at Pentrrth, outside Bangor. On the 12th of April, when in search of her husband, she met a man named James in Warrington, whom she had previously known. The women was in charge of a little boy, six years of age, and topther the three of them went in search of lodgings. On the strength of a representation that she wait the niece of a distinguished Warrington gentleman wilt° was going to adopt the little boy, the prisoner succeeded in inducing Mr. and Mrs. Hilton, of G, Ernest. street, to give them food and lodgings. The man James departed on the following Sundtv, but the prisoner remained until the 20th inst. On the last- named date the prisoner elaborated her story of the relationship with this Warrington gentleman. and borrowed some articles of clothing from Mrs. Hilton, on the pretext that she was going to see him. She departed and left the child behind. When she did not return, the police were communicated with, and as the result of inquiries she as arrested at Chester. It appeared that she had represented herself to the Chief Constable as the daughter of a deputy Chief Con- stable of a certain Welsh county and obtained his sym- pathy. He had obtained admission for her to a home where she was staying, when the police received a com- munication from Warrington respecting her. The Chief Constable added that the local gentleman, whose name had been used by the prisoner for her own pur- poses, had on one occasion assisted her, and that was how she rewarded his charity towards her.—ttichard Hilton, Ernest-street, and Mrs Hilton gave evidence bearing out the Chief Constable's statement.—The Chair- man of the Bench (Mr. T. Bircball) said it was very unpleasant for a gentleman and his family to have their names used as the prisoner had used them. She had been carrying on a regular system of deception, and would be sent to prison for three months.—A further .charge bad been preferred against the prisoner at the instance of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children for abandoning the child. Mr. Browne, who prosecuted, consented to a conviction being recorded against the prisoner without any addi- tion to the term of imprisonment being made. WOULD NOT WORK—NEGLECTFUL FATHER SENT TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>GAOL.</head>
        <p>GAOL.</p>
        <p>James. Byron, 7, Alder-la,ne, was charged at the War- rington Police Court yesterday with neglecting his two children.—Mr. Browne prosecuted and said Inspector Hackett had had the matter in hand since the 9th November last. Defendant had been given every possible chance, but he would not work. A summons was issued in February but it was withdrawn on the prisoner obtaining a situation and promising to follow his employment regularly. Since then prisoner had lost three successive places through drunkenness. He -would not get up in the morning and on one occasion the inspector found him in bed at 11-30 sleeping off the effects of a drinking bout. On Friday last P.C. Clough saw him helplessly drunk. He gave his wife hardly any money, and but for the kindness of a lodger and the help given by Inspector Hackett the children would have been starving.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Pliant' Cases.</head>
        <p>Pliant' Cases.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>I:MoNnAv.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Thornton, Messrs. Birchall,</p>
        <p>Hayward, and Wright. ASSAULT ON A CONSTABLE.—C EVILIAN TO T FIE RESC t:E.—John Noons, wheelwright, Church-street, was -charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street at 3.50 on Saturday afternoon, and with assaulting P.C. Hesp.—The officer stated that prisoner was lying on his back in a drunken state near ithe corner of Dial-street. Witness lifted him up</p>
        <p>and requested him to go away and a young man also volunteered to take prisoner home. Prisoner refused to go, and again fell down. Witness then took him into custody and he became very violent, deliber- ately kicking witness on the side of the head. Mr. A. Downs, newsagent, rendered assistance until the arrival of P.C.Phillips. Prisoner was very violent all the way to the Police Station.—Noons, who made his 12th appearance, stated that he had his head "knocked" 18 years ago, and when he took drink he did not know what he was doing.—A fine of £1 and costs was imposed and Mr. Downs was thanked by the Bench for his services.</p>
        <p>A DISGRACEFUL FIGHT.—John O'Neil, 3, Heaton Square, was chlrged with committing a breach of the peace by fighting on a public footpath in Arpley Fields at 6.30 on Saturday evening.—Detective Barlow stated that acting under the Chief Constable's instructions, he and other officers were on duty in Arpley Fields. Near the cricket ground they saw a crowd of men and boys. Amongst them was the prisoner who was stripped to the waist, and in an exhausted condition. He bore marks of severe punishment. On being asked what he had been doing he said he had been having a fair fight with another man.—The Chief Constable said he saw the fight taking place. It was a most disgusting exhi- bition. The men were stripppd to the waist and were fighting like two savage beasts.—Prisoner was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs. CARTERS FINED.—Two carters named Charles Bishop 26, Portland-street, and James Donogue, 3, Dolman's- lane, were summoned for being such a distance from their horses and carts as not to have control over them. P.C. Stewart said he saw two horses and carts going over Warrington Bridge unattended. The drivers appeared upon the scene shortly afterwards, and stated that they had been in a public-house.—Bishop who was very voluble in the box and would not admit that he was at fault, was fined ss. and costs. Donogue, who had very little to say. was fined ss. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. T. Birchall (chairman), Mr.</p>
        <p>H. Roberts and Alderman Hutchinson.</p>
        <p>ABSCONDING FROM THE WORKHOUSE.—George Mather was charged with absconding from the Work- house and taking away clothing the property of the Watrington Union.—Mr. Constable, an officer at the Workhouse proved the case, and said the clothing was valued at 15s.—The prisoner was committed to gaol for 14 days.—The Prisoner: Make it six months.—The Chief Constable: Take that man down.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. C. White (in the chair)</p>
        <p>F. %V. Monks, and J. R. Young. CHARGE AGAINST A PAUPER DISMISSED.—EIIen Painter, an inmate of the Workhouse, was charged with refusing to perform the task allotted to her and with assaulting Florence Willis, matron's assistant.—lt was alleged that prisoner absolutely refused to work ,and on being remonstrated with, struck Miss Willis in the face. —Prisoner stated that she was not expected to be work- ity, at that time. She was, she added, subject to fits.— The magistrates dismissed the case._</p>
        <p>FOURTEEN DAYS FOR. BEGGING.—Thomas Martin was sentenced to fourteen days' hard labour for begging in Museuia-street.--The evidence of P.C. Baxendale and Mr. Rice, of Bold-street, showed that prisoner was go- ing about begging whilst under the influence of drink When taken into custody hem ade use of filthy languag</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET.</head>
        <p>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET.</p>
        <p>its Great Importance.</p>
        <p>All doctors agrae as to the great importance of fishes an article of diet, but, unfortunately, there is a same- ness in the way of serving fish that prevents its wider use. We must have our palates tickled, or we do not relish what we take. What we want is a greater variety in serving the commoner sorts of fish. To obtain this, fish should be served with a variety of simple yet tasty sauces. Such sauces are now within the reach of all, and in Brown&amp; Poison's booklet, "Simple Fish Sauces," written for ordinary households, by a leading expert, a number of excellent recipes for such sauces are given. These are quickly and easily made, and give snap, flavour and richness to the commonest fish. The thickening for such sauces is Brown &amp; Poison's "Patent" Corn Flour. Application for this booklet should be inade at once, to Brown &amp; Poison, Paisley, enclosing id. stamp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEA.D CEIILD COMES TO LIFE,</p>
        <p>At B3dford, on Nlonday, Arthur Pinchon, described as a draper's manager, was charged with obtaining a sovereign under false pretences. The prosecuting soli- citor said that the prisoner deserted his wife and four children three years ago. He had recently taken a leading part at Bedford among the Wesleyans,and,under the pretence that one of his Aildren had died at St. Helens, he obtained a sovereign from a member of the Wesleyan Church. He subsequently advertised the death of the child in a Bedford paper, but evidence was given showing that the child was still alive. He wa. committed for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OFFICE BREAKING AT Sr. HELENS.</head>
        <p>OFFICE BREAKING AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. Tickle, solicitor, on Monday morning dis- covered thatihis office at the corner of Hardshaw-street and Claughton-street had been entered and ransacked. The office of Mr. Blumberg, neKt door, had alsq been ransacked, and the thieves secured the contents of the safe. A sum of money, variously stated at from £lO to £2O. is said to have been taken, bat the safe bore no appearance of having been forced. The police have the matter in hand. THE NATIONAL SERVICE LEAG</p>
        <p>A branch of this League was formed at St. Helens on Monday. Colonel Richard Pi lk ngton, M.P., was elected president: Sir H. Seton-Karr, M.P., Sir David Gamble, Colonel W. W. Pilkington, Colonel W. Lee Pilkington, Alderman J. C. Gamble, Alderman J. Forster, Councillor H. Martin, Mr. W. Gtmble, Colonel J. D. Murray, and Lieutenant-Colonel W. J. Cook, vice-presidents; Dr. F. Knowles, treasurer; and Mr. W. N. Pilkingtan and Mr. C. Willcocks, joint secretaries._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>KINDNESS ILL-REQUITE.</head>
        <p>KINDNESS ILL-REQUITE.</p>
        <p>Joseph Rigby,a St. Helens labourer, was given a job by a flatman. named Johnson, and, on telling Johnson that he had no money and nowhere to go, Johnson took himtoa lodging house, where they stayed the night. Next morning, however, Rigby got up first and went off with Johnson's bnndl of clothes, which he, pawned, getting drunk with the money, and returning to the lodging-house later in the evening. On Tuesday the St. Helens magistrates sent him to prison for fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PAINFUL CASE AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL CASE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Teacher Charged with Theft.</p>
        <p>A somewhat painful case was heard at St.HelensPolice Court on Thursday when Esther Middleton, teacher at a school in Park-road, was charged with theft. The defendant who was defended by Mr. E. Swift, is the daughter of a Parr tradesman. Chief Constable Wood said that Miss Middleton was a student at the Gamble Institut,. In consequence of complaints Mr. J. Critchley, who has charge of the institute, marked a sixpence, two halfpennies, and four farthings, and sent his daughter to put them in the pocket of a lady's coat and hang it up in the cloakroom on the top floor of the Gamble Institute. Detectives Curren and Staveley then concealed themselves, and Curren saw the girl go int-) the cloakroom, almost-close the door, and go up to the coat and take out the purse. She then went through the other jackets hung up. Curren, who was watching her, then gave the signal to Detective Staveley, who entered the room. The girl took the purse out of her pocket and said, "This purse is the only thing I have taken, sir." At the police otlice she said " I -didn't intend to take it ; truly I did not, if I was to .die this minute."—Mr Swift called the defendant, who said .she had had gloves, overshoes, &amp;c. taken. She lost a pair of gloves on Monday, and intended to go through the whole of the jackets, etc., to see if she could find her gloves, and then she intended to report the matter to the authori- ties.—Another girl student at the institute said that defendant told her on Monday about her gloves being missing.—The head master, t.ecretary, and superin- tendent of the Park-road Wesleyan day and Sunday schools gave defendant an excellent character.—The magistrates said they were satisfied that whatever the reason for it was Miss Middleton did steal the purse, but in consideration of the trouble she had brought on her parents and herself they would simply bind her over to come up for judgment when called upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>TiE FREE I. U311.1.R7 QUESTION AT EARTMTONVg.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a Crowded meeting of the rate- payers of Earlestown and Newton was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, to protest against the act iort of the District Council in rejecting the offer of .C 4,000 for a free library made by Mr. Carnegie, and of a plot of land on which to erect the Library, by Lord Newton. A short time ago a poll of- the township was taken, and it was decided by a large majority that the offer made by Mr. Carnegie should be accepted. Despite this, however, the Cpuncil, by a majority of one have decided that neither of the offers shall be accepted and the meeting was called to protest against this action.—Mr. D. Forrester presided and all shades of opinion were represented by those who sup- ported him on the platform. The meeting was a fully representative one, and there was an air of determina- tion pervading it throughout. The speeches, however, ‘vere of a temperate character and a resolution request-' log the Council to reconsider its decision was unani- mously passed amidst much enthusiasm. A further resolution asking Mr. Carnegie and Lord Newton to hold their offers open was also unanimously passed, and at the close a deputation was appointed to wait upon the Council in order to lay before the members the decision arrived at by the meeting.</p>
        <p>Professor Haman, ef Bale, has taken a rough census of the Jews of the world, and comes to the conclusion that the children of Israel now nurnbernearly 11,000,000. Of these a good two-thirds are found in Europe. Russia comes first with 5,500,000, then Austria-Hungary with 1,860,000, Germany with 568,000, Roumania with 300,000, Great Britain with 200,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 30 1901</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 30 1901</p>
        <p>expenditure on education came from the rates, and he believed the same statement applied to Salford. Other , authorities which had had School Boards in the I past derived at least one half of their incomes from the rates. They in Warrington could not he con- tended, be charged with extravagance. The increased expenditure which the Mayor seemed to think had come from salaries being raised had not come from that source. It was largely due to the fact that they had• had to considerably increase the staffs of the schools and to employ more highly qualified teachers. It had been a serious reflection upon the Educational Govern- ment of the country for a long time, that more than half the children, who were in the voluntary schools, had been imperfectly taught as compared with the children in the Board Schools, because the poverty of the managers had compelled them to employ partially qualified or unqualified teachers. Now those days had gone, and it was a grand thing for Warrington, and a grander thing for the children, that they had. Alluding to the retrospective character of the scale, Mr. Upson pointed out thatas Warrington was one of the first authorities to put the Act into operation, the drawing up of the scale was deferred until information could be obtained as to the amounts paid in other towns. In order that the teachers should not become restive, they were assured that when a scale was adopted it would be made retrospective from April Ist, 1903—that was practically from the time they became servants of the committee. Much had been said about the fact that two teachers would draw something like £lOO each for arrears., Those teachers had been miserably paid in the past but they came under the control of the committee on April Ist, 1903, and the committee said they 'would not treat them so unjustly as they had been treated in the past. If those men were to leave the borough the committee would be bound to appoint successors at the same salaries. The teachers of Warrington thought the committee had dealt very fairly by them, and they believed the Gouncil would do the same. The condition of things in the teaching profession was such that the 300 certificated teachers employed in Warrington could walk out of the borough the following morning to commence work under another authority at the same salaries they were now receiving. By their re- fusal to put the proper scale into operation and main- tain their present fully-qualified staff, the committee would imperil the Government grant of £30,000 per annum.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON pointed out that the objection in regard to retrospective salaries only affected forty- one teachers. There was no need for the others to be restive.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said the resolution which the Chairman had submitted was, in his opinion, an ideal one to the occasion. He was afraid that to some ex- tent the Town Council did not realize the situation on the evening when they refused to pass the increased salaries, and, perhaps, to some extent he was to blame. The reason was that he considered it to be unnecessary to speak at length on the question. He had always followed that course for the reason that matters were thoroughly discussed in the Education Com- mittee, and he knew also that the majority of the mem- bers were also members of the Council, and were pre- sumably familiar with all that took place. The business of the Council tended to get exceedingly lengthy, loquacious, and voluminous and he thought it had not been desirable to go over the work of the committee again. He had, therefore, confined his statements to the shortest limits, never imagining that the members of the Council,and especially those who werealso mem- bers of the committee would fail to realize the true position of affairs. He was absolutely staggered with the result of the Council mooting. No one got up to support him and no one seemed to dream that it was possible the Council could so stultify itself as to send the resolution back. CouncillorAEvans and Wilkinson had said that their remarks applied only to the retro- spective application of the scheme. Councillor WILKINSON: That is sci. Alderman BENNETT remarked that he did not remem- ber a single word being said-in that sense at the Council meeting, although he did not dispute the intention of Councillors Evans and Wilkinson. They did not how- ever, make it clear to the Council or to himself. Hn thought the generality of the Council was of the opinioe that it was the intention to refer the whole scheme back " lock, stock and barrel." He did not think the Council wished to snub the Education Committee or to starve the education of the town. It was a fit of per- haps natural but misplaced economy. In honont to the teachers and in duty to themselves the Council had now no alternative but to agree to the scale. Councillor WILK-risoN : You are all harping on one thing—thatl we object to the scale. We don't. We object to the retrospective salaries. Mr. BUCKTON pointed out that it was the ratepayers who had to bear the brunt of all this, and the ratepayers through theiP representatives had to be satisfied that their money was properly applied. It was as well the authority should be pulled up so that they could make it clear to the ratepayers an their representatives what was being done with the money. It was the Town Council who applied that the appointed day for the Act to come into force should be the Ist April, 1903. That was the crux of the whole thing. If the appointed day had been the Ist April, 1901, the committee would have had 12 months in which to get into working order, to draw up a scale of salaries, and send it to the Town Council for confirmation. Then from the Ist April this year the schools would have been taken over,and they could have levied the rate and paid the salaries accord- ing to the scale. As it was the teachershad been in the service of the committee since April, 1903. _ _</p>
        <p>C)uncillor DODDS observed that as a member of the Town Council he should, he glad if the discussion could be brought to an end at the earliest possible moment, because the more that came out the more absurd and ridiculous became the position of the majority uf the Town Council. He agreed very much with what Mr. Buckton had just said about members of the Council being careful with reference to the expenditure, but he thought that questions relating to the expenditure of the Education Committee should be brought before that committee by councillors who were members of the authority, instead of forcing the matter upon them at the last moment at the Council meeting. He had no iclealthe amendment would have had any chance of being carried, or•he would have supported the deputy-chairman of the committee (Alderman Bennett) in speaking in favour of the resolution. The more that came out, the worse the position of his colleagues in the Town Council appeared.. _ _ .</p>
        <p>• Councillor LOTil) said he voted against the resolution in the Council.lbut as to the retrospective salaries, even the Town Clerk did not know they were to be rotro- snective. That was brought out at the meeting of the Finance Committee held that afternoon. -</p>
        <p>Mr. E. PLINSTON said the councillors who wore mem- bers of the committee ought to have given a reply in the Council Chamber, instead of leaving the matter en- tirely to Alderman Bennett. -- --</p>
        <p>Councillor BURTON' thought the Council was entitled to a little more sympathy and consideration than it had received. If the Council and the committee were more in touch with each other, things would go on more smoothly than they appeared to be doing at present. He did not think for a moment that tho Council would decide to go back upon its actions, and if Councillor Dodds or anyone else had given the explanation Mr. Monks had given that evening the matter would have passed the Council. Councillor CriA.lli said he was out of the town at the last meeting of the Council, and he was amazed when he saw that the minutes had been' referred back on Councillor James Evans's proposition, considering that Councillor Evans seconded the Rev. John Yonge's pro- posal to do the same for head teachers that the com- mittee had already decided to do for assistants. if the members of the committee did not choose to read their minutes they would fall into the hole Councillor James Evans had fallen into, and Councillor Wilkinson had great pleasure in flopping in, with him. (Laughter.) Cmincilllor Evans might have saved a lot of time, if he had admitted his ctrror.</p>
        <p>, Councillor TINNION thought the Council had been somewhat slated.(" No, no.") That was his opinion. He was not apologizing for the Council. He thought it was justified 'in having a considerable control over .the expenditure of the committee. Many preached economy, hut the fact remained that there was a con- siderable addition to the rates on behalf of the educa- tion of the town, and the ratepayers felt it very much. The opinion of the town generally was that the members of the Education Committee generally, apart from the members of the Council, did not care what they spent, and they, therefore, relied on the Council- lore to check them. The only explanation he could give of the Council's action was that they were suffering from the fever of the increase of rates and looked round for some department in which to curtail expenditure. They also wnnted to teach that committee the lesson that the Cotinciliwas going!to snpervise its expenditure. The resolution was carried unanimously. THE E.KOOB.BOL soaoor, FEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 3. Jotai.BY moved the conarnation of the minutes of the Fin:rune Cmurnittee, ‘vliich he slid xv,are self-explanatory. Mr. Bucwros seconded. •</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON alluded to a relolution which ro-td alg follows:—"A letter was submitted from the managers of the Fairfield School, suggesting that, for the future, and with a view to meeting the excess of fees received (to which attention has been drawn by the Board of Education), no fees he ch-irg•Ad in respect of the chil- dren in standards VI. and VII. in the girls' department, and that no fees b 3 charged this year for the weak in which Walking Dty falls. The proposal of the managers of the school in the matter was approved." He observed that the school had charged more than it was entitled to, and other schools had slightly stepped over the boundaries. Recently every head teacher in Warrington had received a fees book, the filling up of which had entailed a considerable amount of clerical work upon the head teachers Tire book had not been sentout by the Director of Education or by the authority ofa sub-committee but 69 an official of the Corporation. He protested against any official of the Corporation sending intothe schools any return to be filled up unless it had the authority of one of the sib-committees. They had been warned to Us aware of letting the con- ' trol of educaticrn get too much into the hands of the officials. He protested against any official of the Cor- poration having the power without the consent of the committee to send such a thing as that which he had complained of. It contained no fewer than eighteen questions to be filled up. I.lr. JOLLF:Y rose to a point of order. Mr. Upson was laying something before the members which might be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMIT7EE. SALARIES QUESTION AGAIN DISCUSSED.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMIT7EE. SALARIES QUESTION AGAIN DISCUSSED.</p>
        <p>Town Council Asked to Reconsider its Decision.</p>
        <p>HEAD TEACHERS AND THEIR PAST SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Nearly two hours were occupied in transacting the business which c tale before the Warrington Education Committee at the monthly meeting on lUonday evening. A number of important questions, chief among them being the Town Council's recent refusal to confirm the increases of salaries of teachers and head teachers, were fully discussed. Mr. F. Monks presided, and there were also present :—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Alderman Bennett (vice-chairman), Rev. Morley Stevenson, Rev. John Yonge, Rev. E. J. Wilcocks,Aldermenrierpoint,and Smethurst,Councillors S. Davies, James Evans, Wilkinson, Ellison, Dodds, Hulse, Craik, Burton, John Roberts, Tinnion, -Lord, Messrs. B. J. Guest, C. H. Unsworth, G. Walker. E. Plinston, J. Fulton Smith, J. Upson, T. May, R. T. Fairclough, J.D.lBuckton, A. J. Jolley, J. Moore Murray (director), and J. Fairhurst (treasurer).</p>
        <p>THE SALARIES OF TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL ASKED TO RECONSIDER ITS</p>
        <p>DECISION. "THE EDUCATION SHIP TORPEDOED."</p>
        <p>The Director read a letter from the Town Clerk inpmating that the Council had referred back the com- mittee's recommendations with reference to the pro- posed increases of salaries for teachers and head teachers. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved : "That in view of the fact that the Town Council have already conffrmed the scales of salaries of both head and assistant teachers, thisConvnittee, having entered into agreements with the teachers on this basis, requests the Town Council to re-consider their resolution of April 12th in reference to the proposed increases of teachers' salaries." He thought that resolution would deal with the matter in the way that would be most agreeable to the Town Council and to the Committee. He could not help believing that there had been some misapprehension in the matter. _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR then read the resolutions bearing upon the matter which had been confltmed by the Town Conn- cll. The scale of salaries had to come into operation as from the Ist April, 1903. The CFIAIRMAN said he thought there could be no question that the committee had acted in perfect good faith with the teachers and that they could follow no other course than the one which they had adopted. (Hear, hear). Having agreed to the scale, his opinion was that the Town Council would have to rescind their own resolutions to put themselves in order. Then they could send the minutes back to the committee. Until then' he thought the committee's only course was to adopt the resolution he had proposed, and which opened the way for an arninable and pleasant settlement. From the. remarks made at the last Council meetinghe did not think sufficient credit was given to thatcomrnittee for a desire to work economically. (Hear, hear.) It was to the interests of some of the members, at any rate, to be as economical, and more so than some people who made a great noise about economy. For himself he was certainly one of the most heavily-hit in regard to the education rate, because his interests were pretty con- siderable in a firm which paid a very large rate. He did not want to say too much, but he thought that when the Council came to view the facts and the difficulty that there was in getting teachers, even at the salaries which were offered, they would agree that there was reason for them to pause. He was told on very good authority that the County Council now required a thousand teachers. He did not know whether that committee could make bricks without straw, but his feeling was that the Town Council, by its recent action, was asking them to do an utter impossi- bility. The Ray. MORLEY STEVENSON seconded. He thought it was obvious to them all that if the Town Council were to persist in the action it had taken, thecommittee would be placed in a very serious difficulty indeed. (Hear, hear.) He did not doubt for a moment that after the very lucid explanation which the Chairman had given, the Town Council, whose relations with the committee had always been so extremely courteous and kind, would see its way to removing that difficulty from their path. He trusted the Council would not strain at the gnat of the education rate whilst it swallowed the camel of the rates of some of the other committees. (Laughter.. The MAYOR thought the position was being a little misunderstood. At their last meeting the committee referred back the resolution as to the salaries of head teachers because it was felt that past services should be recognized. The scale, however, was not altered, but the Town Council was asked to approve it as it stood. The scale was referred back as a protest. Last year they were told that it was expected the education rate in future would be 4d. By some means it had been raised to 104. People were asking whether they were receiving full value for their money. Was the educa- tion of the town better for the additional expenditure? He did no; expect the Town Council would want to in- terfere with the payment of proper salaries to the , teachers. The only question was whether the scale drawn up was too generous for the services required. In some cases the salaries received under the old system had been doubled. An ordinary business man could not understand that kind of business, although it might be all right for trade unions. The Town Council was entitled to be shown in a proper way that that was the most economical way of spending the ratepayers' money. Councillor Wrraussoe said some members of the committee appeared to think that he and his friends made a direct blow at the raising of teachers' salaries. It was nothing of the kind. They certainly recognized the scale, and all teachers in Warrington should be paid according to it if he had his way. What he objected to was the payment of retrospective salaries. The Finance Sub-committee had passed the salaries accounts with the exception of the retrospective salaries which the Borough Treasurer had been ordered to keep back. If he (Councillor Wilkinson) took over a business he would not increase the wages of the staff until they had shown him at the end of twelve months th at they were efficient and had proved themselves worthy of an advance. In granting increases to teachers let them consider the services rendered since the com- mittee took charge of the work. Councillor JAMES EVANS said he did not. move the resolution at the Council meeting in any spirit of opposi- tion to the payment of the sum'which,in the opinion of the Education Committee, ought to be paid to every class of teacher in the town. It:would, he thought, be frankly admitted on every hand that perhaps that committee itself did not understand that the scale was retrospec- tive. Certainly the majority of members of the Council were not under that impression, and he never heard the scale explained at a meeting of the committee as being re- trospective.He did not think the members of the Council were conscious of what they were doing when they voted. As far as the general feeling of the Council was concerneci,it was not in opposition to paying the teachers but to expenditure,by that. or any other committee,which might be avoided. Whilst he quite recognized that the chairman was perhaps one of the heaviest ratepayers, Mr. Monks might have the strongest back to bear the burden. Considering the times through which they were passing, the Town Council should not only have the fullest information, but the education authority, like all the other committees, should be urged to be as economical as possible. He endorsed every word of the resolution. What he wanted was a thorough under- standing between the Council and the committee. He did not believe a single member of the Corporation wished to retard the committee's work. Mr. MAY thought that was a good opnortunity for him to put in his protest. f Laughter.) He had been told that he was often wroncr. He freely acknowledged that that was the Case, and that he was determined to be put right. The committee was chopped up into a lot of little sub-committees, and hitherto they had been working in the dark. In proof of that, he would point out that the date and hour of every sub-committee meeting was now published on the agenda. That inform- ation had not been supplied during the past year. Now the members would be able to attend the meetings. and. with a great deal of trouble to themselves, be able to get information. The CHAIRMAN: I think you must keep to the reso- lution. (Hear, hear.) Mr. MAY lam putting in my protest against the way in which the business is conducted. Continuing , he said that during the past twelve months the corn- mittee had been building a ship. The Town Council ; had now bursted a torpedo under it, and it had been suddenly exploded—(laughter)—thus putting them in an ignominious position. That, was due to working in the dark, te misunderstandings and to sob-committees which prevented members like himself from know- ing all the business in hand. Even when they got up to sneak they were met with interruptions and with derogatory and ridiculous remarks. He appealed to them, after such an exhibition as they had had, to adopt another method, so that when the minutes were brought forward at general meetings the Chairman should be responsible for giving them accurate information on every point. Mr. UPSON said Councillor Evans had spoken in a very temperate way, and he was entitled to the fullest, information he could receive from the committee on the subiect. The Mayor's quarrel was not altogether with the committee but with the Government, of which he (Mr. Upson) believed Alderman Bolton was a supporter. (Laughter.) The expenditure was bound to follow the passing of the Act by the Government. The committee had no option at all in the staffing of the schools. He would point out that only one-fourth of the funds of the committee came from the rites. The remainder clime from the Government. If they read the accounts of the meetings of other education authorities they would find that committee was one of the most economical authorities in the Kingdom. In Manchester more than half the I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>interesting, but a notice of motion ought to be given. It had nothing to do with the resolution which had been quoted by Mr. Upson. _ _</p>
        <p>The Cti.unmiii said there was a reasonable explana- tion to be given and he would ask Mr. Fairhurst to give it. Mr. FAIRHURST explained that it was not necessary for him to receive instructions front any sub-committee or from the General Committee, as to how he should deal with accounts. He was the accounting officer of the authority, and healone was responsible to the Local Government Board auditor for the correct keeping of the accounts. If they did not wish the fees to be checked in a proper manner it would be better to do away with them. There must be some system adopted as to the manner of entering the fees. He did not ask for them to be entered every week, but it was necessary that the fees struck out should be entered in the log book or some other suitable book. As long as the managers gave authority for the writing off of the fees, he wanted that authority. If any fees were to be written off, if the authority would say who was,to do so, that would be sufficient for him. This was a matter of account. He denied the statement that extra clerical work was being imposed on the head teachers, and he could assure the committee that he endeavoured all through, to give the teachers as little trouble as possible. All he wanted was to have these fees entered in a satisfactory manner so that anyone could check the whole thing right through. (Hear, hear.) Mr. UPSON said he wanted to say they had kept for thirty-three years a return of the fees for the Govern- ment Inspector that had satisfied him. The MAY 011: That is not on the minutes. (Laughter.) The minutes were then agreed to. THE TRAINING OF TEACHERS. THE EXCLUSION OF THIRD CLASS PUPIL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee having recon- sidered the resolutions.which were not approved by the Education Committee at its last meeting, together with a letter from the managers of the St. Mary's School, Latchford, it was proposed and carried :—" That, in applying the scale of salaries, each five years of service up to a maximum of twenty years as a head teacher in a school in the borough, with an average attendance of less than 500 children, be counted as one year of service under the committee,the salary in no case to be increased above the maximum of the scale." The following amend- ment was voted upon and lost :—"That this committee, whilst recognizing gratefully the value of the services rendered by the head teachers in the borough in the past, do not see their way to recognize length of service as a reason for special treatment infixing their salaries." Applications for special consideration in respect of the salaries of the head teachers of the senior mixed depart- ment of the St. Ann's School, the St. George's Mission School, and the Infants' Department (Cairo-street) of the Warrington British School, were submitted and considered, and it was resolved that the committee ' could see no reason for special consideration of the salaries in those cases. The committee proceeded to reconsider the resolution with reference to the salary of the headmaster of the mixed department of the St. James' School, Latchford, which was not approved by the Education Committee at its last meeting, and it was resolved: "That consideration of the matter be deferred until further information is obtained from the managers of the school upon the subject." The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON, in moving the con- firmation of the minutes, alluded to the appointment of Mr. Paul Fisher, B.Sc., as chemistry master in the Secondary School and Technical Institute, in place of Mr. Shepperson, resigned,at a salary at the rate of £l4O per annum. He said Mr. Fisher came from one of the best Liverpool schools with a good reputation and he hoped in him they would have a thorough andcapable teacher. Another important resolution was that relat- ing to the training of teachers. The committee had resolved :—" That in order to facilitate the discussion of this question, and to enable some decision to be arrived at, it be suggested to the Lancashire County Council that the following resolutions be submitted to the adjourned conference for consideration, viz :—`(1) This conference is of opinion—(a) that increased accom- modation for the training of teachers should be provided withinthe areas of the counties of Lancashire and Ches- hire ; and (b) that the existing training colleges should be utilized as far asipossible in providing such increased accommodation." Some time ago, Mr. Stevenson added,a, conference washeld in Manchester which some of those present attended as the representatives of the committee In Manchester there was a disposition to advo- cate a scheme which drew a circle of twelve miles round Manchester, with Manchester as the centre, and they said, "We will undertake to provide teachers for all classes within that circle." It was probable if Man- chester did that,lLiverpool would act in a similar way, and a glance at the map would show the members present that both those circles would exclude Warring- ton. What did it mean? It meant that Manchester and Liverpool would take within that circle the places with the greatest ratable value, and would exclude the rest of the counties of Lancashire and Cheshire for which the ratable value was very much less, and they would get no help at all from Manchester or the Liverpool district. They ought, therefore, to protest. There were already bodies formed such for instance as the Rivers Pollution Committee, to deal with all Lancashire and Cheshire as a whole, and to spread the cost of what was done over the whole of the two counties, and not to'place it upon the richest rateable area and leave the poorest to look out for itself. The object of the resolution was to advocate something of the kind—that Manchester and Liverpool should not I take care of 'themselves and leave the rest of the county out in the cold, but that the supplying and training of the requisite number of teachers should be spread over the two dounties. (Hear,hear.) Alluding to the case of the headmaster of St. James' School, Mr. Stevenson said the committee had the matter under consideration and they were waiting for further in- formation. With reference to the resolution respect- ing head teachers' salaries, he would only say that the resolution involved an extra expenditure of £250 this year, and a further additional sum in the future. The extra amount to be paid this year was in excess of the expenditure. Referring to a letter submitted from the managers of the Fairfield and Warrington parochial schools, with reference to the attendance of two pupil teachers, who only succeeded in passing the recent King's scholarship examination in the third class at the pupil teachers' classes, Mr. Stevenson said the committee objected to appoint those who had only passed the King's scholar- ship examination in the 3rd class. It was not advisable that a class of children should be entrusted to the hands of a teacher who had not shown herself or himself suffi- ciently capable to reach the extremely moderate standard of the second class of the King's scholarship examin- ation. They did not think the dearth of teachers should be used as any argument for the appointment of ineffi- cient teachers. (Hear, hear.) The committee would be pleased to hear that, whereas one or two months ago he was obliged to say, in speaking at a similar meeting to this that they had 25 vacancies in their schools and that they were unable to fill them with certificated teachers, that at the present moment that number had been reduced to nine. When they heard that the Lancashire County Council required one thousand teachers he thought they would feel further justification for the action that they intended to take in asking the Council to confirm the scale of salaries that had brought them a supply of duly qualified teachers. Councillor CRAIK seconded. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH pointed out that it was a very hard case in regard to the pupil teacher of the Parochial schools who had been refused admission becauses she had not passed higher than the third class. The managers agreed to indenture this pupil teacher, and if she passed a third class scholarship, they agreed to continue her services. Mr. .Mkv • Is she a competent teacher?</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH : Yes, she is. Mr. JOLLNY said he rose with some reluctance to move an amendment to the resolution with reference to the application of the scale of salaries to head teachers. His amendment was in accordance with the Standing Orders which limited him to this form, namely: "That resolution c 519 be not approved and adopted." His object was to emphasize as strongly as he could the previous decision of the School Manage- ment Committee, which in effect was here reversed. He aimitted the voting was extremely close, but he hoped he would not be accused of undue conceit if he said that the arguments were preponderately in favour of the former decision of the com- mittee. (Laughter.) Trio analogy between the case of the asiistaat teachers and the head teachers in respect of length of service was erroneous. The expression of "market price" had been used on a former occasion and also that evening, and he thought it might be reasona.bly used in matters of this kind. The scale that had been decided upon for head teachers and approved was a rea.sonable one and the salaries repre- sented as nearly as cauld be judged the "market price." The estimates for the year had been framed on the original minute of the Schoel Management Committee, and if the general eoamiittee passed this resolution they were going back.to some extent on the estimate, but if they supported his amendment they would be endorsing what they had already done, namely, approve the estimates agreed to at the last meeting. He wished to say that n.ny figureshe submittted as chairman of the Finance Committee had been carefully verified, and he was not going to take it lying down when a suggestion was made that anything had been concealed or that an attempt had been made to smother things up. (Hear, hear.) If the members were determined to pass the reSolution now put forward he would aSic them not to lose sight of the fact that they were increasing the salaries allowed for in the estimates. (Hear, hear.) Councillor DODDS seconded, and said that while he believed in recognized length of service in any class of service he did not think it ought to be a special reason for fixing a salary. Mr. Jolley had stated the case very fairly indeed. Mr. MAY asked when they arranged to give a head master or head mistress from £2OO to £3OO what was it they took into account? Was it that he or she was doing a great deal more work, or better work than his subordinate? He thought it was often the case that the head master had less to do than his subordinate. What they vi-ere taking into account when they gave a headmaster double what his subordinate got was not that he was doing so much more, but simply and solely length of service. The scale fixed for the head master had been fixed solely on account of length of service. If he was wrong he should be glad to be corrected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>I Mr. UPsoN: You are, Mr. May, very wrong. : (Laughter). Mr. BIICKTON did not think length of service should always be ignored, but the resolution seemed to go to the opposite extreme. In effect, it said length of ser- vice shall always be considered. Sometimes a man got into office and afterwards proved he did not deserve it. The Rev. JoHN YONGE said he would not imitate the mover of the amendment by assuming a modesty which he did not feel—(lauglater)—neither would he follow him into the subtleties of finance. (Hear, hear.) It seemed to him that this was a question of what was fair. To his mind—some people would object if he called it "clerical" mind but at any rate to his minis- terial mind, which was not exactly in the opinion of most people a business mind—it did not seem to him to be fair to treat one set of teachers one way and another set another way. If it was right to recognize length , of service in regard to junior teachers, he maintained that in nearly every case it would be right to deal with senior teachers on the same principle. He did not i know whether it would help to pass the resolution fhe suggested that the word "may" should be put in, namely "may be counted as one year of service under the, committee,'' thus giving the committee the option by means of that wotd to deal with cases, or rather not to deal with cases which were not considered to be 1 deserving. He hoped that what had been said might lead to a vote to settle the question on his side of the case.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR asked whether if the resolution were approved, there would be an additional increase of salaries to those included in the estimates? •Mr JOLLEY : Yes. ... _ _</p>
        <p>The 11,1A.YOR : Then the Council was perfectly justi fled in sending the Eninutes back. As a business man,he , did not understandrthe matter at all for the reason that it was not put in a business form. Tbe CHAIRMAN: You must admit that the matter is in proper form. There is a scale on which head teachers will be paid and this is a resolution giving them that inci ease for length of service. The,MA.yott contended that they were asked to vote in the dark. Which headmaster would get the bulk of the money? Anyone who was not in touch with the question, one who was not on this very committee,could not understand it ; unless it was that more money was wanted from the ratepayers. Councillor Wilkinson also disapproved of the proposal Mr. UNS WORTH thought this was rather an inoppor- tune titre to bring the question forward, because the minutes would have to go back to the Council to be confirmed. After what took place at the last meeting was it at all likely this would be confirmed?. It would be much better to postpone the matter for 12 months, and for that reason alone he should vote for the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr. UrsON contended that it was perfectly fair for the committee to take into account the long services of the head teachers, when fixing their salaries. He pleaded more especially for the headmistresses because their scale of salary was not as good as that of the masters. They had in Warrington many women who had worked well as mistresses. They had earned the highest grant and got splendid results, and these head teachers were to start now on the minimum scale. As a matter of fairness the committee should count five years of service as one year on the scale. It was not a large sum but it would satisfy these head teachers who had done well in the past. On a vote being taken, twelve voted for the amend- ment and six against. The amendment was therefore carried.</p>
        <p>MORE OBJECTIONS FROM MR. MAY. MR. W. H. STANSFIELD'S SALARY. THE RECENT ICI G'S SCHOLARSHIP EXAMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY moved that the minutes be confirmed with the exception of the one relating to the salary of the headmaster of St. James' school. He did not think Mr. Stansfield should be harrassed any longer by uncer- tainty in regard to his salary. The committee had selected the wrong man to make experiments with. Instead of selecting the worst they had chosen the best of their masters hi order to put him at the bottom of the scale. •</p>
        <p>The CH AIRMAN: There is no miqunderstanding about that. The committee has asked for certain information which has not been given. Until the information is given, the matter is in abeyance. Mr. MAY thought it desirable that the salary should be raised to the amount originally granted by the managers. He contended that they were dealing harshly and inconsiderately with Mr. Stansfield in regard to sending the question of his salary to and fro in this manner. If he thought it would do him any good be would proceed with his amendment. As it was evident it would do no good he would proceed to refer to the following resolutions :—" Letters were submitted from the managers of the Fairfield and Warrington Parochial Schools, with reference to the attendance of two pupil teachers who only succeeded in passing the recent King's scholarship examination in the third class, at the pupil teachers' classes, and it was resolved that the committee cannot depart from the terms of resolutions Nos, c 470 and 403." He moved that the minutes be confirmed with the exception of the foregoing. The ratepayers he con- tended, were being put to undue cost by the exclusion of competent teachers who had failed to pass higher than the third class. He would like to know the reason why so many pupil teachers had passed third class this year, whilst in previous yews the returns had been so much better.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: They are short of brains.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUG seconded, and said this was not a case of being short of ability but of means. (Hear, hear.) --</p>
        <p>Mi. UPSON said the effect of the amendment, if carried would be to add £75 per annum to the expenditure. Mr. BUCKTON supported the amendment. lie said teachers who desired to continue in the profession were being ejected, and there was no immediate ”rospect of filling their places. The vacancies would be filled by the appointment of certificated teachers so that the cost to the town would be two or three times as great. The probable reason why many candidates did not pass higher than the third class was because during three or four years of their apprenticeship they could not obtain the facilities for learning and study which were now afforded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. M. STEVENSON did not consider it was for the welfare of the pupil teacher that they should allow her to enter the profession with a third class, as Art. 50, wit i:2 the practical mpossibility, or rather he would say the improbability of her ever being able to rise to any creditable post in the profession. The teachers were not being ejected from the profession. They would be retained at a small salary and taught for another year at the pupil teachers' centre. It was hoped that after the further instruction they would be able to pass the examination creditably. If once they opened the door of admission to the third class people, and allowed them to enter the schools as teachers, they would io them no kindness but harm. The King's Scholarship examination was a competitive examina- tion. Those who passed high-in the first class showed undoubted ability, but those who passed low in the second class showed only very ordinary capacity indeed. When they came to the third class they came to those who had not reached a creditable standard. As a general rule it markeda very low level of intelligence, and a very small stock of knowledge. Was it just to take any class in their schools and entrust it to a teacher of that capacity? They would not be doing what was right by the children if th.y placed them under the care and teaching of unqualified teachers who were barely recognized by the Govern- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr.MAY said his amendment only applied to two pupil teachers whom the managers said were qualified. On a vote being taken there voted For the amendment : Councillor Hulse,Messrs. May, Fairclough and Buckton-5</p>
        <p>Against: The chairman, the Rev. Morley Stevenson Alderman Pierpoint, Counoillor Dodds, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, Fulton Smith, and Upson-7 The minutesilvere then confirmed. SICKNESS AMONG SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>THE LATE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE INSPECTOR. At a meeting of the School Attendance'committee,held on thel4th April,the Chairman having reported the death of the School Attendance Inspector(Mr.Williani Jones), which took place on the evening of Monday, the llth April, it was resolved: "That this committee desire to place on record their regret at the loss sustained by the death of the School Attendance Inspector, and to con- vey to Mrs. Jones and her family their deep sympathy with them in their bereavement." A report as to the methods obtaining in other districts in respect of the issue of medical certificates for children who are unable to attend school owing to sickness was submitted, and after careful consideration, it was resolved "That the committee is of opinion that it is undesirable at present to make any change in the existing system ; and that in cases where sickness is said to be the cause of absence, and there is no doctor in attendance,the Superintendent or Director may, on consideration of the report of the officer, refer any such cases to the medical adviser of the committee for his opinion thereon." In moving the adoption of the minutes, Councillor ELLISON paid a tribute to the twenty-four years' ser- vices of the late Inspector Jones who, he said, was a very faithful (Aar. He regretted to state that there was still a great deal of sickness among school children. The cases reported last month were 1211 ; this month there were 1,340. He hoped there would soon be an improvement.</p>
        <p>1 Mr. WALKER seconded. Mr. UPSON endorsed Councillor Ellison's remarks with regard to the late Mr. Jones. He had known the deceased gentleman for about eighteen years and he was exceedingly sorry to hear of his death. He should like to know what was to be done to supply Mr.Jones's place. If they appointed another superintendent they I would duplicate the office. They might save money if they appointed a senior officer instead of a superin- tendent. They had a director of education and a n office staff.and what they wanted was an officer to visit the schools and streets. Councillor DODDS said he was about to make a similar suggestion, and he hoped the matter would be con- sidere.d. Mr. MAY: I believe the committee has decided not only not to replace the superintendent but to go to the bottom end and employ a boy in the office. Councillor ELLISON : We have taken no steps about the superintendent, and shall not do so until our next ; meeting. I The minutes were then agreed to.</p>
      </div>
