      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The!Sites and Buildings Committee recently resolved' —" That the Law Clerk be requested to give necessary notice under Section 8 of the Education Ac" 1902, of the committee's intention to provide a ters' porary school available for the following areas, Part of Bewsey, part of Orford, and part of St. JAW' wards." The tenders for the erection of a temporal iron school building on a portion of the recre,atia. ground in Orford-lane having been submitted, lb was resolved :—" That the erection of a t.,_eel; porary school be not proceeded with; and we, resolutions Nos.e 39-41, with reference to the acceP". ance of the offer of the Open SpmesSub-committ,ee of a portion of the recreation ground in Orford-lan and the issue of the requisite notices by the Law Clerad be rescinded." A report by the Director, which b been prepared in accordance with the instructions oft committee, as to the number of sites which will Fob ably be needed for new schools to meet the require...L. merits of the next seven years, and their localities, sv.' considered. It was resolved:—"That the report be approved, and be accepted as the basis of the culn- ruittee's scheme for the provision of sites for schoolsill the borough during the period named." a The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes, ald alluding to the proposed temporary school, said it woe'. involve an expenditure of £l,OOO with details. M - new school would cost only £3,000 it was thought to b; better to go on with the new school. Mr. Murray be been asked for a report as to what would be needed 00 the outskirts of the town but the report was so elaborate meaning eight new schools, that it was decided tho 'three of them only should be gone on with at once. SOOR as the matter was in hand the committee %lA' come before the General Committee and let them kill what they proposed to do. It was likely a school wow be erected behind the barracks. Councillor HLTLSE seconded. Councillor ELLISON urged the committee to push OI with the work as soon as possible. It was importaw' they should have a new school built in order to save the grant.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : We will do so as early as possible. The minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>DATES OF MEETING.</p>
        <p>- -------• On the motion of the CHAIRMAN, seconded by Alder- man BENNETT, it wls decided, "That the August, October, and January meetings of the Education Committee be held on the 29th, 24th, and 23rd of those months res' pectively."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LEADiNG FJOD BEVERAGE.</head>
        <p>THE LEADiNG FJOD BEVERAGE.</p>
        <p>Don't ovorrate your powers of endurance. Take duo precautions, and face the world with the comforting assurance that you will be able to stand the strain. What you really need to do this effectually is a bever- age at once refreshing, pleasant to the palate, noci strengthening to both mind and body. Such a food beverage is Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. It has made haPPY many a home which otherwise to-day would have beer' wretched, and the ultimate effects of which it is possible to foresee. For breakfast, dinner, supper", indeed at any hour of the twenty-four—Dr. Tibbles Vi-Cocoa will be found light and refreshing; it lends the way among all beverages, and nothing can equal it'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MUSIC IN THE PIARK. The Work of the Associatioa of</head>
        <p>MUSIC IN THE PIARK. The Work of the Associatioa of</p>
        <p>Bands.</p>
        <p>Warrington is indebted to the Association of Bant for the enjoyable musical performances given in Bant` Park during the summer months. Before entering uPee another season, the members of the Association assembled at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening ferf their annual meeting, and a very satisfactory state 0, affairs was reported. In the absence through indispas_L- tion of the President (Mr. T. J. Down) the chair Ara' occupied by Mr. T. Armstrong. THE PRESIDENT AND THE " DISREPUTABLE APPEARANCE "OF THE STAND. Mr. A. DOWN read some notes prepared by his father_ in which he stated that it was a great disappointraeo to him to be prevented from being present that nigh!, but he felt that it would not only be unwise, but unsale for him to venture out. Under those circumstances he felt sure the members would exeuse his non-attendance He was pleased to find from the notes supplied to hiel by the secretary that the association continued to pros; per, that thebalance owing to the bank a year ago bO,l been paid off, and that this year there was a swap balance in hand. As their only source of income 011'1 the .£7O received from the Corporation'it was importan that the total expenditure should be kept within the limit. He suggested that the Secretary be instruct el to write to the chairman of the Town Hall, Parks an Baths Committee calling attention to the present disre- putable appearance of the band stand, and respectfullY requesting that it should be painted and decorated in time for the opening of the coming season. (geaf hear).</p>
        <p>LAST SEASON'S WORK. The SECRETARY (Mr. B. Wadsworth), presented the annual report. The season in the Park opened on MO,l 3rd of last year with a performance given by the Ler'. street Band, on September 27th. A total of thirty-one performances had been given,and at eighteen the bands had turned up in full strength, that was twenty-live members. In hardly any case had there been more than one absentee, and only once during the year had the attendance been as low as twenty. That was. he thought extremely creditable, and spoke much for the inter, taken by the players in their work. (Hear, hear.) The. must remember that often, in order to be present at evening performance a man had to miss a portion of WS work. That was more especially noticeable at the beginning and end of the year, when the performance commenced rather early on account of the light. Even then, they found a full band on the stand before the: public had arrived. He thought it would be agreea that the class of music performed had been quite up te the standard of previous years, and perhaps even a little better. (Hear, hear.) It was hoped that a friendlsr rivalry would continue among conductors to place the best compositions before the public. Without doubp their performances .supplied a demand, but at the sani,e time they must endeavour to educate their listeners Sy that in future they would be able to better appreciat° the highest class of music. (Hear, hear). The balanl sheet showed that after paying expenses, which include' £66 2s to the various hands, they had a balance hand of 7s. Id. (Applause.) The Secretary conclude(' by alluding to the regret they all shared at not having their President with them that night, and expressed th pleasure it gave them to see his son, Mr. Arthur DOME' (Hear, hear.) The report and balance sheet were adopted. THANKS. Hearty votes of thanks were accorded to the Presi- dent, Vice-President, Treasurer, Seci•etarv, and the members of the Town Hall,Parks and Baths Nmmittee• THE LIFEBOAT SATURDAY FUND. Mr. Stanley Wilson wrote asking the Secretary to con: vey to the Association an expression of grateful that'll! from the Lifeboat Saturday Committee for the vajuah assistance rendered by the bands in connection with Oa parade, which resulted in the realization of so handsoMe a sum.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Mr. T. J. Down was re-elected president, and the vice-presidents were elected as follows :—The Mayor? Alderman Heaton, Messrs. F. Monks, A. H. Crosfield,A• Down, R. A. Naylor, members of the Town Hall, Parkf' and Baths Conimittee.—Mr. J. Fairhnrst, horougli treasurer, was re-elected treasurer, and Mr. B. Wads" worth, secretary.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS. Mr. A. DOWN said he had often thought it would 11 desirable, if possible, to have the band stand provide,. with light, and also with some kind of wind guar," Another suggestion was thrown out by a member to 0,7 effect that a mass performance should be given by ai the bands at the close of the season. The CHAIRMAN said the matter was brought up 134 year, but it was found unpracticable. The proposed improvements at the band stand Wel.° referred to the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CURE FOR RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>A CURE FOR RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>Thos. Wm. Turner, Veterinary Surgeon, 31.R.C.V.5" of Sleaford, Lincoln, makes this statement : "In November last I was suffering from rheumatisci The pain in my legs at night when I got warm in be was almost unbearable, and I could get very little reseti I got a box of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and as they seem to be helping, I soon after procured a second box. , "I used to take one pill three times a day, and aft,e,; taking three boxes in this way I had no further attecis, of the Rheumatism, and I have not had an ache or pain since. The pills seemed to help me from the 110,11 commencement, and should I ever have a return of Ot rheumatic pains. I shall use Dodd's Kidney Pills 3 once, as I know they are an excellent medicine." Such unqualified statements of fact from reliable me f and womenare appearing every day from all parts the country, and unquestionably they prove that poda: Kidney Pills cure rheumotism and all kindred kidueL diseases. These plain truthful statements leave bY room for doubt as to the genuineness of the cures this remedy.</p>
        <p>In the Clerkenwell coroner's court on WednesdaP when Dr. Danford Thomas held an inquest on the boclY of Harry Dairs, aged 15 months,Dr. Jones said that tbef baby's brain weighed 420 z., about the average wei 1 gjt oe an adult's, and the largest he had ever seen. But tbo baby's death was in no way attributed to this; it sc• caused by pneumonia. On Wednesday, Mr. Councillor Brnnning, of Becket: ham, was taken to Wandsworth Prison, where he inten; to spend seven days because he objects to pay an edtlP a tion rate amounting to 7d. Mrs. Brunning accornpale her husband to the prison gates. Mr. Brunning saYs he is prepared to go to prison every six months until t' Act is repealed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>THE LICENSING BILL. tillt 18 not too much to say that the question at°Bl,agltating the minds of Free Churchmen Idue present time is the Government's Licerl,sing Bill, which has just been introduced. ti,813443 of many gloomy statements made from •L'• I's to time the Temperance Movement has uu until steadily growing during the last few years, ---"ay, within the Free Churches at least, - Te united .body of feeling in favour of mperance Reform. All Free Church- ;en feel that there is a crying need for legisla- On dealing with licensing, but they cannot feeling that the present Government's iteasure does not supply that need. Some of aueLadmißsions of the Government are welcomed, re,ueu, for instance, as that there is need for the nction of licenses, but the proposal to corn- ?ate refused licensees awakens uneasy feel- ...ugs even in the minds of those not usually 'suciated with the Temperance Movement.</p>
        <p>COMPENSATION. th„Qiu,ite apart from the question as to whether "?ense duty from which compensation is to thpraLLl is not a proper return to the State for 10,11141°110poly created by licenses, and there- tie' property of the public, it is felt that val effect of the Bill will be to at once raise the pi Ile of the worst type of Public House. ti aces over which is hanging the threat of ex- halietien, and which under recent conditions beve been most worthless property, will now cenlle substantial investment.</p>
        <p>13RSTRUCTION OF MAGISTRATES' DISCRETION. gardlllere seems to be no two opinions with. re t - tfo the mischief of removing the discre- lconarY power from Brewster Sessions—which fa °lva local circumstances—to Quarter Sessi▪ ons, „iszt;,rernoved from the localities under seea " Just before the introduction of the f 3,_sure the Bishop of Chichester, as Chairman Church of England Temperance Soci▪ ety, aplnst ,I),Fessed the strong protest of Anglicans tol,- the proposal. The Temperance senti- eii„llt 11,es greatly increased in the Established they .°f late years, and it is to be hoped that will take a leading part in agitating the question.</p>
        <p>2lit ATTITUDE OF THE FREE CHURCHES. !or? e Free Churches are marshalling their re 88 in order to take strong action. It will be Icertlenlbered that at the National Council at conwdeastle an emphatic resolution was passed erevernning any interference with the dis- °eary keetin power of magistrates. An urgent of th gOf the special Temperance Committee tor e National Free Church Council has been ed, at which the proposals of the Govern to litt will be considered, and the line of action recommended to the Free Churches "gheut the country will be decided upon. R •F. B. MEYER'S PRESIDENTIAL VISITS. The to 4 Presidency of the National Free Church 110,11.eil has never been regarded as a merely Ite;ll,nal ellice, and this year's President, the tha •B. Meyer. is entering on his duties with of ra,oteristic energy. He has formed the idea toov„,,ing all the District Federations of orcf "e"B into which the country is divided in o,er to get into touch with the vast machinery brjeted by the National Council. Those who tot: accompanied him on his visits say that quit: of the features of his meetings are to linique. He usually devotes one day Federation, speaking to a gather- -1I tOf ministers from the whole district 1348:4,,e. Morning, preaching in the afternoon, iotiliunig over a Conference following tea, and ton.° ernVening speaking at a public demonstra- teaturA-Lhe afternoon conference is a unique ef .R .-; In order to ascertain the exact state exEClalre in the district Mr. Meyer cross- of tZ'Oes the local Secretaries as to the ntate (14 eir work, and he gives many valuable hints I'7ll from his own extensive experience.</p>
        <p>PREII CHURCH YEAR 800%. A. yob, Zlnsidc, -, u141,° which is always welcomed with e interest—the "Free Church Year °PeEtk has just been published for 1904. For erB and writers on the Education ques- Whil, the debates of the K9,tional Council, 4 Etre printed verbatim, will afford of information and illustration. ,Ib6Ports of some of the sermons and by 4: -,'lBses will be read with much interest, both t°se privileged to hear them and the gati3l: number to whom attendance at such remb:lngs is impossible. There were some thee 4 notable addresses at the Newcastle aow"ngs, such for instance as the Rev. J. H. et.t's inspiring discourse and Dr. Fairbairn kengl4fioent sermon on "Faith." Free Church- ° eto• will be particularly interested in the tn,B n Itics of their denominations, both itlfr,g'and and abroad, and the mass of other 114:Nation makes the year book a necessity _ Work of reference.</p>
        <p>PIIER CHURCH FEDERATION ABROAD. ool7°ln time to time information reaches this ntry showing how the Free Church Federa- %t: Movement is spreading in the Colonies and 14,4°ad. The Council of Churches in Australia 1541.14 a report of good work done during the ts, 't,vear, on similar lines to that accomplished Fra„ue Free Churches in this country. .In ' rep„'T too there has been an awakening with Pr7,-Drq to Free Church Union, and there is a of °sal of four of the most important sections %Int.:ll'lh Protestantism to come into closer Protestant Federation is to be formed the I.;_ue object of defending, where necessary, Of f lib object of Protestant worship and the rights prerated Churches. It will also endeavour tloa"' Protestant Churches and workers into E'r relations with one another.</p>
        <p>e SOME DEVOTIONAL VOLUMES. th,cllsiderable interest has been aroused in series of "Little Books on the keve2it Life," edited by the Rev. F. B. sireVre,. ,11. A. and published by the National `-'nurch Council. They are to number 14rsCten, and the price is Is. 6d. each. The 4S- tl;%°' "The Possibilities of Obscure Lives," ‘6,41,ev. Alfred Rowland, 8.A., LL.B.. D.D., l'irop:"ssons from the Cross," by Rev. Charles 410411, are now in the press, and will be issued The other writers in the series in- To:". G. Campbell Morgan, D.D., Rev. W. 14,-.llsend, D.D., Rev. Charles New, Rev. F. qrlet', 8.A., Rev. G. S. Barrett, D.D.. Rev. Mantle' Rev. J. Monro Gibson, Rev. Renclal Harris, D.D., Rev. R. .u.D.,-ipt:'°/1, M.A., D.D., Rev. J. B. Meh arry, -ev• George Hanson, M.A., D.D.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY IN JAPAN. Of 044 j g touching Japan and the Japanese -ritere. a. at the nresent time. The Free ;11:1,ter,-,7nan for May gives an informing article • 'flristianity in Japan." The Missionary klitlitl6Tet2t, it appears, has taken wonderful u 4198 eti,°' late. "There has been a great revival, Ihttinr 'writer, "of Christian activity. It is Se "14).11 that no fewer than 2.5.000 converts 10 added to the Church within twelve )" e etc.' Luring this Revival Campaign 0„11? Ilse was made of the printing press. "nd small posters and leaflets were ttl•tyilitaion toi:tis mrteeeat corners, in public baths, in rail- tri boat stations, and everywhere r, cities. These posters set forth in reaper. .attractive fashion the truths of the I.:64flete oarly three million copies of tracts, kilitistian poems &amp;c.. were iiistributed --°Rt viondarffil</p>
