      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>YEAR'S CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>A YEAR'S CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S RETROSPECT.</p>
        <p>THE PROSPECTS OF PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>POLITICAL interest for the next few days will be centred upon the forthcoming meeting which Mr. Chamberlain is to address at Birmingham. It is not yet twelve months since the then Colonial Secretary launched his famous scheme of Colonial Preferences. Shortly he will address a similar audience of the same Unionist Association in the same hall from which we first beard of Tariff Reform on May 15th, 1903. Much has happened since then; not the least important change has been the change in Mr. Joseph Chamberlain. Then he was the most important member of a powerfnl Government; now he is a private member of a helpless and hopeless party! Then he had returned from the lonely veldt of Scuth Africa, while the country was yet igr.orant as to how far his policy in that country had failed. Now he has come back from the solitary desert of Egypt, knowing that rot only has his policy of Preference and Protection failed, but that the country has realiz.ed the meaning of his policy in South Africa. Last year he was at the height of his popularity, and received something like a Royal welcome. This year he came back from Egypt to find his popularity gone and his political fortunes at the lowest ebb; not until he rcached Birmingham could they raise a cheer on his 'behalf. In the short space of twelve months Mr. Chamberlain has destroyed his own influence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Fall:</head>
        <p>The Fall:</p>
        <p>Mn. CHAMBERLAIN probably owes his rapid fall in public estimation not so much to his advocacy of a policy which was not readily acceptable to the people, but rather to the fact that he lacked the staying.power necessary to maintain his advocacy of that policy in its original form. Had he stuck to Preferential tariffs as a necessity of Empire, according to i his original scheme, he might, n time, have gained some measure of real and solid success. He might have attracted the support of disinterested persons, and created a new ideal Empire. But in his desperate haste to popu- larise Preferential tariffs, he was forced to seek the support of those for whom Preference for the Colonies and sacrifice for Empire were only acceptable in so far as it meant Protec- tion for their own trades and interests. These followers exacted the price of their service. Preference for the Colonies soon had to be given a second place in Mr. Chamberlain's programme, while Protection for certain home industries became more and more prominent. When Mr. Chamberlain talked of a 25.. tax on corn Mr. Chaplin was asking for ten times that amount; each trade which suffered by foreign competition asked that the people should be taxed for its benefit. Preference was soon forced to give place to Protection, and the plea for Empire was lost in the demand for taxes on foreign imports. It was when Mr. Chamberlain Lad adopted wholesale Pro- tection that the opposition to his schemes began to gather in force. Mr. Chamberlain's appeal to the Imperial instincts of the British, even if unacceptable and impracticable, would have at least excited some appreciation; but his descent from that lofty height to an appeal to sectional and trade interests has only created hostility and derision. Preference for the Colonies would at least have secured respectful nearing; out rrotection for the privileged classes, at the expense of the com- munity, was doomed from the first. Mr. Chamberlain has sometimes been described as a strong man; but strong men are never in a hurry to win popular applause, they know how to make haste slowly. It is the only sure way of building up a policy which will stand the storms of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Then and Now.</head>
        <p>Then and Now.</p>
        <p>IT seems a long cry back to those days when Mr. Chamberlain first preached the need of sacrificing the food and trade of our own people in order to consolidate the trade of the Empire; a long cry from then to these days when a committee of his friends are sitting with the view of re-arranging the taxation of the country so as to benefit themselves. The citizens of Great Britain have not been slow to realise the change, and quick to express their emphatic condemnation. Then it was said to be all sacrifice; now it is seen to be all self-interest. Then, judging from the by-elections, the nation was inclined to halt and consider the matter; now, few candidates who hope to win dare approve the scheme. Then the country was undecided whether scme sacrifice for Empire was not necessary; now it knows that only class interests are at stake, and its decision against them has been firm. Facing Facts.</p>
        <p>AIR. CHAMBERLAIN has , never been afraid of facing facts. Directly a cause seems lost Mr. Chamberlain has always found a good reason for directing; his activities to some' other quarter. He has never_yet spent any of his valuable time in fighting a losing battle,. and it hardly seems likely that he will now contradict the habits of a lifetime. Pro- tection stands condemned in nearly every constituency throughout the United Kingdom which has had an opportunity of giving a decision. Mr. Chamberlain-is fully capable of judging how far those constituencies represent the general opinion of the nation. He is faced with the fact that, should similar results follow in the other constituencies at the General Election, his party would not be merely defeated—it would cease to exist as a power in the House or country. If the late Colonial Secretary realises the situation in this light, what is he likely to do? The only answer seems to be that he will allow his propaganda to die down as quietly and unostentatiously as possible. Without abso- lutely abandoning Protection, he will probably assist us to forget it. -- A Surprise.</p>
        <p>OF course it may be possible that Mr. Chamberlain has brought back from the deserts of Egypt a new and startlipg develop- ment of his Fiscal policy. It originated on the lonely veldt, and it may have been rejuvenated in the solitary desert. He may have some surprise for us when he next speaks, as he had for his colleagues on May. lOth last year. However this may be, it is important to remember that Protection is not dead, even should Mr. Chamberlain abandon it to the faithful and fostering care of Mr. Chaplin. The old Protectionist party was dormant before the late Colonial Secretary took it up, but it soon proved itself to be far stronger than he was, able to control. As long as there are classes in the community who will benefit by Protection at the expense of their fellow- countrynnn,we shall always have Protectionists among us. Like the germs of disease which lie dormant in the human frame, Protection only requires a low condition of political vitality to be roused into maligilant activity.</p>
        <p>The Original 00000,, and a Speciality,</p>
      </div>
