      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 30, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>EC WARRINGTON AND I),ISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>SANKEY CHAPEL HOUSE V. BROOKLANDS.—The match between these clubs at Sankey resulted in a win for the home trundlers by 47. Details:— CHAPEL HOUSE. . MOORLANDS. B. Deakin l3 C Grant J. Sparks l5 G. Warburton J. Barton l5 W Wadsworth 4 R. Dutton J. Plumpton R. Beddard l5 R. Healey l3 F. Jefferson lO T Rigby W. Ellison</p>
        <p>14 J. Hutchinson F. Turner _</p>
        <p>J. E. Jefferson W. Foster l5 R Williamson l2 J. Golding</p>
        <p>- HORSE AIM JOrTCEY V. LIBERAL CLUE.—These clubs met on the former's green. Scores</p>
        <p>- JOCKEY. LIBERALS. T. Bretherton l5 J. Jennings T. Wells l5 Greenhalgh T. Hackett Hardman T. Harrison l5 J. Sankey l3 D. Daniels P. Wells J. Bolton W. Carter 9 H. Williams l5 W. Mee l5 J.Houghton 4 G. Higham J.Holbrook l5 F. Rigby</p>
        <p>167 127 Majority for Horse and Jockey, 40. CEMETERY HOTEL V. PENKETH PARK.—Penketh Park were visitors to the Cemetery Green, and after an interesting game found themselves losers by 47 points. Scores :</p>
        <p>CEMETERY. PENKETH. J. Woods 0 W Goulden l5 J. Forster l5 J. Graham 8 W. Hill l3 J. Higham l5 T. Bolton l5 J. Bowyer ll H. Williams (capt) Jos. Harrison l5 J. Hill E. Taylor l5 J. Wareham 8 Jno. Ashton 5 E. Bond l5 C 4. Ball J. Berry if l5 A. Higham T. Ellison l5 H. Bowyer 4 J. E. Harrison</p>
        <p>153 106 wro WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETINON.</p>
        <p>This competition which was such a decided success last season, re-commenced on Saturday on the green at the King and Queen Hotel, Padgate-lane. There was a. large attendance, and considerable interest was taken in the competition. Pearson and Knowles No. 2 defeated Wilderspool Brewery in easy fashion winning every game and scoring the highest aggregate. Whitecross No. 2. failed to turn up to play Longford Wire Works, on account of a breakdown at their works. According to rule NVhitecross should forfeit the game, but the committee under the circumstances have decided to leave it to the teams to arrange another date. The fol- lowing were the scores : PEARSON &amp; KNOWLES NO. 2. WILDERSPOOL BREWERY. W. Bradshaw James Bradshaw l5 Jack Arnold 2 'D. Furguson Peter Hill (captain) l5 T. Holcroft 0 R. Tickle l5 T. Langley T.Hatton l5 W. Taylor 3 John Bradshaw T. Combs l5 T. Andrews W. Kenwright l5 George Ward (absent) 0 J. Hewitt l5 R. Harmer 0 W. Leigh l5 Joseph Arnold(captain) 1 J.Boyle l5 W. Speed 5</p>
        <p>Total 180 Total 47 %No contests have been arranged for to-day (Saturday) owing to the final round of the Northern Union Cup.</p>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>The bowling green attached to the Horse Shoe Inn was opened for the season on Saturday, when 32 mem- bers of the club competed for prizes. There were some very close contests, the successful competitors being: 1, H. Hankinson ; 2, S. Taylor; 3, A. Owen; 4, J. Taylor, jun. After bowling, an excellent knife and fork tea was provided gratuitously by the host and hostess. When the tables were cleared Mr.J. Shaw was appointed chairman, aiid a very convivial evening was spent. songs &amp;c., being well rendered by members of the company. On the motion of Mr. Rigby seconded by Mr. Shaw, thanks were accorded to Mr. and Mrs. Maines for their kindness to the members of the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT NEWTON</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT NEWTON</p>
        <p>Lord Newton and the Budget.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Newton Division Conserv- ative Association was held at Newton on Saturday. In proposing a vote of confidence in the Government, Lord I Newton said Lord Lansdowne, by bringing to a success- ful issue the agreement with France and the Anglo- Japanese treaty had earned a place among distinguished foreign secretaries. The great difficulty hitherto with regard to British foreign policy was that we frequently did not seem to know what we wanted, therefore we could not expect other nations to realise our wants, but with the Japanese treaty we now knew exactly where we stood, and had it not been for it some other Power in addition to Russia would have been drawn into the present conflict, and Japan would have been absolutely crushed, with loss to British trade. They beard very little from their opponents, he continued, about the French agreementand the Japanese treaty, but the subjects they preferred to discuss were such absorbing topics as the place in the House of Commons where Major Seely should sit, how many people would remain in thn House of Commons to hear Mr. Winston Churchill speak, or dwell upon the suffer- ings of the passive resisters and see their boot-jacks and tit-bits sold by ruthless euctioneers, or the imaginary sufferings which the unfortunate Chinese were to undergo trt the hands of our representatives in South Africa. Those who had taken an interest in politics knew the lengths to which politics had gone,but he could not have credited their opponents for the extraordinary success which misrepresentation had achieved on the Chinese labour and education questions. On the Chinese question it would not surprise him to bear that Chinese labour had invaded this country, and that Colonel Pilkington had obtained an exceptionally favourable quotation from Lord Milner to supply him with Chinese labourers for the mines and glassworks at St. Helens nor would he be more surprised if their opponents de- clared in reference to the compensation of publicans that that compensation would come out of the pockets of the total abstainers and manufacturers of aerated waters. (Laughter.) In reference to the Budget, he congratulated Mr. Austen Chamberlain on having intro- duced an honest Budget!in spite of having to face a large deficit, due to the extraordinary conduct of his prede- cessor Mr. Ritchie. Were Mr. Chamberlain's fiscal proposals adopted, the revenue would be largely increased not by taxing ourselves, but other people. Were the Liberal party in power the country would be tied down to Free Trade and Home Rule, which latter question was being kept in the background. Mr. Fulton Smith seconded. He said it was for- tunate that such able commanders were in charge of Ithe ship of State, and to steer it successfully during the I past years of exceptional trial and difficulties. So long as we had the leadership of competent statesmen who could hold their own against bitter and obstructive criticism of every policy—domestic or foreign—the ! prestige and honour of the Empire was safe. The recently concluded agreement between France and j England was a triumph of diplomatic ability, securing ' that esprit de corps between two important European Powers at a critical juncture when it as essential this country should keep clear of the embroglio in the Far East. The treatment of the Labour question in the I Transvaal was another instance of the astuteness of the Government in dealing with so intricate a question. I When Lord Londonderry replied to a letter sent him by I the Free Church Council of Glasgow protesting against j the introduction of Chinese labour into the mines, he enclosed a copy of a resolution by the Free Church , Council of Johannesburg which strongly deprecated the English Free Church Council's agitation against Chinese I labour, and pointed out that further knowledge would I modify its views regarding the conditions of service, I which had long prevailed with little change in South. I Africa. Lord Londonderry said be could not help think- ing that the opinion of the Free Church Council on the spot was of more practical value than even the opinion ! of the provincial Synod of Glasgow. I Colonel R. Pilkington, M.P., in supporting, contended that any bill dealing with important questions like I those in the Trade Unions Bill should be Government ' measures, and held that as a Royal Commission on the subject was sitting it was inopportune to have brought iu the bill last week, as it would not only be improper, but dangerous. to interfere with legislation until the Commission's 'report was received. In reference to the Licensing Bill, be supposed there was a desire to reduce the number of public-houses, and held that com- pensation should be given. Another aspect of it was I that if a public-house licence could be taken away at , the end oft welve months, public-house property should not be rated for death duties on a twenty ...years' pur- I chase. He would be glad to see the measure pass. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>LIVELY SCENE</p>
        <p>AT RUNCORN POLTCE sTAnos.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Saturday, Denis Burns was•charged with being drunk and disorderly and breaking the plate-glass window of the Rose and Crown public- house. Prisoner was refused drink, and in his rage smashed the window,and assaulted Constable Bratt,who apprehended him. At the police station the combine.d efforts of several officers were required to place him in the cells. He was sentenced to • two months' hard labour and placed on the black list.</p>
      </div>
