      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Soup Kitchen at the Ragged School.</head>
        <p>The Soup Kitchen at the Ragged School.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l find that many people in the town are under the impression that the soup kitchen, which myself and a few workers have kept open continuously for six weeks, has received support from the Ironworkers' Distress Fund, and from the fund under the control of the Corporation. Will you please state that, although I made personal appeals for help, not one penny has i been received from these sources. The following s a full list of all.monies sent in:— Warrington I.L.P J. Charlton Parr, Esq. 5 0 0 Mrs. ,loseph Harrison Charles E. Parker, Esq. Miss Pierpoint (St. Austin's) Mr. Alfred Hulme</p>
        <p>11 17 6</p>
        <p>The total outlay is £29 13s. 7d., leaving .f.t deficit of £9 16s. Id., which, unless farther subscriptions are received, will have to be paid from oar limited mission funds. We have receivea large quantities of meat, bread, vegetables, Scc.' from tradesmen and others, or, but for this help, the kitchen could not have remained open near so long. I take this opportunity of personally thanking all those who have helped me to try to allevi- ate the distress in Warrington during the last few weeks. The distress has been greater and more real than what has been implied, and, unhappily, still con- tinues.—Yours faithfally, JAMES SKELLA'ND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>"The Papyrus Tree Dying Out."</head>
        <p>"The Papyrus Tree Dying Out."</p>
        <p>Sir.—The following is an extract from a paragraph which appeared in" The Manchester Guardian" of the Bth of this month under the aboveheading :—"Very few people (writes the Pall Mall Gazette's Rome Corres- pondent) know that Italy, among her other treasures possesses a small forest of papyrus trees, which grow 013 the banks of the Anapus, near Syracuse." And per- haps as few may be aware that the same tree is to be found in the sacred land of Palestine. Ten miles, in a direct line, from the northern end of the sea of Galilee —so intimately associated with thelife and ministry of Jesus—lies the small lake Hooleh, or waters of Meront between the hills of Badhan on the east, and those of Naphtali on the west. This lake receives the three mountain streams that make up the Jordan which, rushing from the lake at its southern extremity in one rapid river, passes on through the Sea of Galilee, and is finally absorbed in the gloomy waters of the Dead Sea. In 1868-1863 the late J. Macgregor, M.A., explored this lake and its environs in his canoe "The Reb Roy." In the exten- sive marsh at the Northern end he saw a vast area of floating papyrus, believed to be the largest mass in the world. And entering what proved to be the mouth of the Jordan he found the papyrus of the richest green, and upright as two walls on either hand, and so close was its forest of stems and dark recurving hair-like tops above that no bird could fly into it, and the very few ducks that had wandered in by swimming through the chinks below, were powerless to get wing for ris- ing ("The Rob Roy on the Jordon Sm." London: John Murray.) The panyrus is remarkable as it was out of it that the Egyptians made ;their writing material. Cutting the wood into very thin slips, and placing other thin slips across these, and gumming both together, they could produce whatever size of sheets they re- quired. But a deeper interest attaches to the tree on account of its connection-with Moses. When that great Lawgiver of Israel, and-of the world, was a child, 3,475 years ago, he was consigned to the waters of the Nile in an ark made of papyri's to escane destruction by the Pharaoh who knew not Joseph.—Yours truly, lONA. April, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Chinese Labour Question:</head>
        <p>The Chinese Labour Question:</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of March 26th 1 find a letter from Mr. Gricoshaw written in defence of the Government's policy touching the employment of the Chinese in South Africa. He claims to give "a few plain facts" and refers to people on the platform and elsewherespeaking on matters of which they have no knowledge. Well, I think I have some little acquaintance with the Chinese character.ll have rubbed shoulders with Chinese,and more than that, I have, out in ths lumber camps, been the only white man working in the Chinese gangs. Belicv, me,.l know "John." I love the Liberal party for what it has done, and I detest the Tory party for the opposition it has consist- ently given to every progressive measure. In the practical politics of to-day I have no further use for either of the political parties. They ire dead, but bury them decently. Within a few days after the vote was taken in the House, I saw Mr. Pierpoint had voted in favour of the employment of the Chinese in South Africa, and I thought, "Yes, it was ever thus; rich men are always on the lookout for cheap labour." I may be told that Mr. Crosfield belongs to the wealthy classes. I admit the force of the remark and merelyanswer,that I hope, as a member of the Liberal party he will never vote in favour of making a special effort to import either Chinese or Japs into any portion of the British Empire. Mr. Grimshaw does not believe the "indentured" Chinese will be slaves when they reach the gold mines, I do, nay, I am certain that the "African venture syndicate" once feeling that it has the British Govern- ment behind it, and the German Lord Milner as a near- by friend will put the atrocities of the Congo in the shade and will make the British nation weep for the blood it has shed and lament the treasure it has wasted all to put money in the pockets of who? Has Mr. Grimshaw gone over the list of names com- posing the syndicate that will employ these Chinese? To a man they are Jews. In London, Paris and Berlin they are to-day to be found, and, workingmen, I tell you these men are grinding you,cven you who are not in south Africa down to the very dust. The above is somewhat beside the question. The point at issue is, shall the yellow man be allowed to take to South Africa his race characteristics and place them in competition with the white man's? The Chinese emigriSe is a very different man from the one he leaves at home. As a rule he is the lowest meanest skunk the sun ever shone on. The better class stay at home in China. Thanks to the British he has learned to love opium. The gentle sex of the Chinese race are not noted as emigrants. As a consequence'all the sins of either modern or ancient Babylon coupled with those of Sodom and Gomorrah fail to excite him. He soars beyond these, and cultivates his own little tricks, and were Mr. Grimshaw to spend an hour with me in the back alleys of the Chinese quarter of San Francisco I think I could make his hair stand on end. Tell me Mr. Grimshaw, is a Christian Government will- ing to reap a Drat out of this kind of thing? Now, my fellow workmen I tell you plainly and out- side of all politics, it is a question of paying a living wage. That and nothing more. I have at my elbow near upon a thousand letters, all written in South Africa, and by white men who are willing and anxious to work, but they cannot compete with the Chinese. If you fought to make South Africa a portion of the British Empire, make it fit fora Britisher to earn his living in. If you fought to double your national debt for the Chinaman and the Jew take your medicineand may it agree with you. If you fought to have another Irish question on your hands, don't say you had no warning. Now then let me put a plain question. If .British labour is not to be employed in the mines, what did you lightlfori? Answer, Mr. Grimshaw. H. WHITHAM, 1318, Guerrero-street, San Francisco, U.S.A. April 9th, 1901.</p>
        <p>Alleged Attempted Suicide at Poulton. Before Mr. T. Halton, at the Warrington Occasional Court on Wednesday morning; Henry Kennerley, of Gorton,'near Manchester,was charged with attempting to commit suicide by tilting ammonia at Poulton, on the 13th inst. He was found on the roadside by a Warrington cabman named Drinkwater and on being asked 'what he had been drinking, replied," Ammonia Go away, I want to die." Drinkwater conveyed him to the Infirmary in • a cab. Dr. Hodgkinson gave evidence as to the condition of the prisoner when ad- mitted into the Infirmary. In his opinion, the man was sane.—Prisoner was committed to the Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE 'STOMACH, LIVER, _KIDNEYS AND BLQOD. SEA-WEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, infinitely greater than ordinary medicines. This is now regarded as conclusive by eminent authorities, who recommend it daily since Veno's Sea-weed Tonic was first introduced. A pure palatable extract of Sea-weed is one of the principle ingredients of Veno's Sea-Weed Tonic. This remedy excels in the cure of INDIGESTION, WIND,HEAD- ACHE, NERVOUS COMPLAINTS,KIDICEY TROUBLES,WEAK AND PAINFUL BACK,TORPIDIVER,FEM ALE TROUBLES, POORNESS OF BLOOD and "HABITUAL CONSTIPATION." Its most brilliant effect is produced in Stomach. Liver and Kidney Complaints. The remarkable cures Veno's Seaweed Tonic effect even in the worst cases is positive proof of its therapeutic value. Ask for VENO'S SEA-WEED TONIC, is. lid. and 25.9 d. per bottle, at Chemists and Drug Stores.</p>
