      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION</p>
        <p>CUP FINAL</p>
        <p>Warrington's Prospects at Salford.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. By " Ovali7st."</p>
        <p>The day has arrived, and it finds the Warrington team in fine fettle for the grim struggle at Salford for the ultimate possession of the Northern Union Cup, the highest honour possible to local footballers. People who have noticed that Warrington have played —and gracefully lost—a couple of matches during the present week, may possibly fear for the freshness of the Lancashire representatives. Their fears are, how- ever groundless. Neither the Wigan nor the Fludders- field match took much out of the Warrington cup team, seeing that not a single member of the fifteen donned jersey or pants for either engagement. They have been taking great care of themselves during the last ten days, and if official reports are anything to go by the men are in tip-top condition. This being so they have only to reproduce the form shown in the semi-final to effectually dispose:of Halifax and thefr cup pretensions. I don't believe any team in the League would have stood a chance with Warrington at Far- town on that day, and there is every reason to hope and believe that the same men will:equally distinguish themselves to-day.</p>
        <p>There are' some Warrington partizans who imagine the final to be what they euphemistically call" a snip "for thelocals, but they might as well get that idea out of their heads at once unless they want to be unpleasantly disillusioned. Halifax are no mean anta- gonists—in fact lam ingined to think that in a cup struggle they are amore formidable lot than Bradford —and as the present holders of the trophy they can be relied upon to strain every sinew to retain it.</p>
        <p>Lovers of the Rugby game should b 3 interested in the contrast in styles that the final will afford. Halifax play a typical Yorkshire cup-tie game—close scrum- maging, a minimum of hand-to-hand passing, and powerful kicking. The front rank have the reputation of being exceedingly clever, and well-led by Jack Riley, while the backs all know their places. War- rington rely, as is well-known, on quick heeling out and combination between half and threequarter backs. The whole half-dozen generally show an admirable understanding which spells success. Against Brad- ford their movements were prettyness and effective- ness combined. The ganie ought therefore to be ex- ceedingly interesting, and an admirable exposition of the possibilities of Northern Union football.</p>
        <p>A comparison between the performances of the two teams in the cup tourney prove them to be equally matched. The records are :---</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Halifax -15 St. Helens ... 0 0-0 Halifax -11 Barrow Halifax Halifax giving a total of 41 points to 10. G. T. P. G. T. P. Warrington ... 00— 0 Swinion ...... -0 Warrington ... 4 4-20 Swinton 0 0-0 Warrington ... 0 1— 3 Wigan 0 0-0 Warrington ... 2 2-10 Pontegract -1 Warrington ... 0 1— 3 Bradford -3 Warrington ... 1 2 8 Bradford -0 or 44 points to seven.</p>
        <p>Ages and weights also interest the enthusiast, and for his benefit I append the latest returns. It will be seen that Halifax have provisionally selected 18 men from whom to make the final selection on Saturday morning. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>Height. Weight. Age. ft. in at. lb. Little 2l ... 5 81 ... 13 7 Wedgwood Joe Riley 2l ... 5 9 ... 12 2 Riag Williams Hartley 23 ... 5 61 ... 10 2 Morley 24 ... 5 51 ... 11 4 Nettleton 26 ... 5 64. ... 10 6 Gledhill Bartle 23 ... 5 101 ... 14 3 Bulmer 22 ... 511 ... 12 12 Langthorn Mallinson 27 ... 5 8 ... 13 3 Morton 26 ... 510 ... 13 12 Jack Riley Swinbank 23 ... 5 91 ... 13 4 Winskill Hammond 23 ... 510 ... 13 7</p>
        <p>Warrington are relying on exactly the same side as against Bradford, viz: WARRINGTON. Height. Weight. Age. ft. in. St. -lb. J. Hallam 25 ... 5 81 ... 11 6 J. Fish E. Harris .D. Isherwood 27 ... 5 84 ... 10 12 G. Dickenson D. Davies 24 ... 5 81 ... 10 6 T. Hockenhall 23 ... 5 8 ... 10 6 G. Thomas A. E. Boardman 24 ... 5 10i ... 14 1) D. Morrison] A. Naylor 27 ... 5 9 ... 12 6 G. Cook 4 24 ... 510 ... 13 0 G. Jolley E. Lunt 26 ... 5 94 ... 11 10 J. Edmondson 23 ... 5 91 ... 12 4</p>
        <p>Halifax, like most Yorkshire clubs, have some ad- vantage in weight against Warrington, but the latter seem to have learnt how to discount the odds in avoirdupois very nicely.</p>
        <p>The Cup-Committee in!t in Manchester on Tuesday, and to the great satisfaction of Warrington appointed Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes, referee, with Messrs. J. Priestley (Salford) and E. H. Smirk (Wigan) as touch- judges. There is no more competent official in the Union than Mr. Smith, and his determination to have the game played as it ought to be played can be relied upon.</p>
        <p>At Weaste, the Salford folks are making great preparations for entertaining a crowd of 30,000—and given the fine weather we all hope for, the number should not be fat short. How many will go from Warrington, the Fates only know, but nine or ten specials will go from the two principal railway stations. The London and North-Western Railway people have wakened up quite suddenly and made excellent arrangements to run trains to and from Weaste,which is only three minutes from the ground. Facilities are afforded for people to return from Manchester if they desire. The Qheshire Lines Committee, as usual, have catered handsomely and will doubtless secure a big share of public patroaoge.</p>
        <p>The Cup will be presented to the winners by the President of the Union, Councillor R. Collinge, and afterwards the teams will dine at tin Grosvenor Hotel.</p>
        <p>There will be innumerable little " Fishes " at Weaste. The workshops of the town have been working full-time (dinner time) during the week in turning out copper and brass " Fishes " with pins for attaching the little badges to the coat. I have heard, too, of one man from the market who is going to take the biggest Aberdeen haddock he can find, but this is a risky piece of partizanship. Ile may get it rammed down his throat.</p>
        <p>Another report, this time from Winwick, is to the effect that a whole block of padded rooms is being reserved for those people in Warrington who go mad when the cup comes home.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that no less than eight of to-day's team played in the Final at Headingley on April 27th, 1901, viz., Hallam, Fish, IsherWood, Dickenson, and Harris Boardman, Edmondson, and Morrison. Swift would have made the ninth had he not been injured. The half-hacks on that occasion were R. Bate and Duckworth, both of whom have been playing together this week in the Wigan and Hudders field matches.</p>
        <p>If Warrington are successful they will return in possession of the Cup by the 8-30 train from Man- chester Exchange, due here soon after o'clock. The subsequent proceedings will be promptly arranged. Already the Borough Band and llessrs. Crosfield's Band have offered their services.</p>
        <p>Another item, conditional on victory, is a trip to Knutsford May Queen Festival on Monday, in charge of Mr. Harry Ashton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>OPINIONS FROM HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>OPINIONS FROM HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>A Halifax correspondent writing on Thursday night, says Halifax people believe that the blue and white brigade will win the Cup again. At the same time great respect is being paid to the abilities of tho Warrington team. Those who saw the Lancastrians defeat Bradford at Fartown in the semi-final are not at all optimistic. At the same time, it is not believed in the broad-acred shire that Warrington backs will have the same liberty of action as they had at Far- town. Those who support the Halifax position have behind them the argument that the Halifax team have figured in seven finals and have been successful on six occasions. Six times they were in the last stage of the Yorkshire Challenge Cup tussles, and were five times successful. They have only appeared in one final under Northern Union auspicies, and that was when they whacked Salford—with quite as clever backs as Warrington—at Headingley last year.</p>
        <p>" I have just coins away from the Salford v. Brad- ford encounter, further writes our Halifax corres- pondent, who, of coarse, sees through blue and white glasses. If Halifax can play the same game as Brad- ford have done in their " final " with Salford they should win. Of course, a lot depends on the front rankers, and that is where Halifax people expect their team will have the pull. With Warrington front-rankers beaten, Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris will not have much chance of playing the same game as they did at Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>The constitution of the Halifax team is not yet known. It will not be definitely settled until Satur- day morning. Twenty men have been picked and they are all reported sound and well and available. Joe Riley, the centre, who had a little finger badly damaged against Batley close on a month ago is re- ported all right now. The weather will largaly rule the final selection. It may be taken for granted that Little will appear in the full back position (D.V.) The threequarters will probably be Williams and Joe Riley in the centre, but there is a lot of uncertainty about the wing players. There are four other threequarters chosen, viz., Rigg (the veteran who knows every move on the board), Hartley (a fast youngster, who scored cleverly against Hunslet in the semi-final), Hadvren (as consistent a player as ever stepped on to a field, if not brilliant, and quite cool and collected), and Wedgwood(limby,full of bone-and muscle, young, and fleet of foot.) If wet weather pre- vails the half-backs will probably be Mosby and Gled- hill (the latter of whom will "go down " in shoals of mud), if fine look out for Mosby and Nettleton. As for the forwards, they cannot be much different from the eight who figured in the final last year. If they get the mastery Halifax should win.</p>
        <p>But I don't think the Halifax team are:really as keen about winning the cup as they were twelve months ago. Then, Halifax and Salford were lively rivals for both honours going and the Yorkshiremen were very anxious to put the Salford nose out of joint. Having accomplished that, there does not appear to be the same amount of devilment (for want of a more genteel if less expressive word) about them. All the same,all hope of annexing the cup again has not been abandoned. •</p>
        <p>Whatever may happen in the cup final, Yorkshire i not without honours, for the League Championship went to Bradford on Thursday when a deciding game was played between that club and Salford at Halifax. The Park Avenue men were slightly the better side all through and won by a goal and a try to nil.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For. _ Against.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. P. Pts. Bradford 35...26... 7... 2...46 72-308...18 20-96...54 Salford 35 .25. 8. 2..58 84-368-16 27-113..52 Br'ghton R 3l-21... 9... 4...33 80-306-29 28-142..46 Hunslet 34...22...11... 1...50 50-250...20 39-157..45 Lacy's 33...19... 9... 5...27 47-195...19 33-137..43 Oldham Warrington 34...17...14... 3...29 52-214...27 33-153..37 Hull K't'n R 34...17...15... 2...31 43-19L..22 41-167..36 Halifax 34...14...17... 3...25 25-125...32 28-148..31 Swinton Widnes 34..11..18. 5..24 26-12g-30 61-213..27 Hull Wigan '33...11...17... 5..32 36-172-26 39-169..27 Batley 34 .11..20. 3...23 30-136-47 52-250..25 Runcorn Leigh Keighley 34... 8...21... 5...33 21-129...50 73-319..21 Hudder5fi1d33...10...23... 0...17 40-151-50 77-331..20</p>
        <p>The South West Lancashire Competition up-to-date is as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Warrington Wigan *St. Helens 5 ... 2 ... 2 ... 1 22-40 ... 10 Runcorn 4.4eigh 9 ... 2 ... 5 ... 2 28-55 ... 6 Widnes . • 8 ... 1 ... 4 ... 3 22-39 ... 5</p>
        <p>*St. Helens matches, not being home and away en- gagements, each count 4 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>A signal victory was achieved by the Warringtonians on Saturday. South Manchester will not retain very pleasant recollections of their visit to Arpley, for they returned vanquished to the tune of 122 runs to 24. Judging from their present form, the Warrington players mean to do themselves justice this season. When the footballers have made their exit, we may hope for a ra-awakening of interest in the summer game. Large gates would be decidedly welcome to the club and would go a long way towards placing it in a sound financial position.</p>
        <p>Lynes was again the hero of Saturday's contest. The visitors could make no headway against:his bowling and five wickets fell at a cost of only 10 runs. The profes- sional also displayed conspicuous ability with the bat, and ran up the top score of 36. His performance was a highly creditable one and called forth warm apprecia- tion. E. A. Connor, the captain, also bowled effectively and captured three wickets for six runs. His innings realized a useful ten. P. B. Hawtin batted in capital style and knocked up 23. T. Wright was responsible for 13, and G. Et trbsdell and E. E. Wood (not out) each placed 11 to'his credit. None of the Manchester players attained double figures, but there was a goodly array of "ducks." Mr. " Extras " figured with the highest score of 8.</p>
        <p>Fairfield opened the season in away entirely satisfac- tory to themselves, Penketh being defeated by a margin of 46. F. Norman(24) batted splendidly for the viotors and also took 2 wickets for 8 runs. Whittle did the best work with the ball, his figures being 6 wickets for 8 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Golf.</head>
        <p>Golf.</p>
        <p>The Mersey'Golf Club opened their season on Satur- day, with a match between teams selected by the captain and secretary. The course was in excellent condition, and a pleasantly-contested game ended in a tie. The first monthlycompetition for a prize presented by the Captain (Mr. C. R. Adamson) will take place to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The Cantilever Scheme Again</p>
        <p>Recommended.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday. the Borough Surveyor submitted the follow- ing report with respect to the widening of Warrington Bridge beg to report that I have zarefully examined the plans, report and estimate submitted by Mr. J. J. Webster, M.lnst. C.E., of Victoria-street, Westminster. for widening l the bridge by means.; of eantilevers. At the present time the total width of the carriageway between kerbs is 20 feet and from the side of the tram lines to the kerbs averages 4 feet, and the width of the footways on each side are 4 feet. It is proposed to widen the carriageway to 30 feet 6 inches between kerbs and 8 feet 9 inches from the side of the tram lines to the kerbs on each side. The width of each footway will be 10 feet. The gradients of the roadway will remain as at present. When the tramways were being constructed cast-iron pipes were laid so that the tie bolts could be put through to connect the cantilever brackets on each side so as to prevent the cutting of the paving, concrete,and tram lines. I notice in the plans that provision has beenma.de for carrying gas, water, air, telephone and electric pipes in accordance with the requisition of the respective engineers. The design practically is good. I find it will not be necessary to interfere with the property on the South-East side of the bridge, but a strip of land on the South-West side about 10 feet wide belonging to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley cc Co., Ltd., at Bridge Foot Inn, will be required; which I do not con- sider will be costly to obtain. I have seen the alteras tion to Cardiff Bridge and also photographs of bridge- at Bristol which are almost identical with Warrington and are somewhat similar to the design proposed,Scc.,an are reported to me as being satisfactory. The following recommendation of the Bridge Sub- committee was approved :—"That the General Purposes Committee be recommended to proceed with the widen- ing of Warrington Bridge by means of cantilevers in accordance with the scheme prepared by Mr. J. J. Webster." It was also resolved that a specification be prepared and tenders for the widening of the Bridge, as proposed, be advertised for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 30, 1904 ARCHDEACON MADDEN'S</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 30, 1904 ARCHDEACON MADDEN'S</p>
        <p>VISITATION.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS AT WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. --- Church Controversies and Divisions. THE "ORNAMENTS" QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The VEX ARCHDEACON MADDEN hlld his annual visitation at the Warrington Parish Church on kVed- nesday, when the newly-elected churchwardens and sidesmen were admitted to office. _</p>
        <p>In the course of his charge the Archdeacon said : The history of the Archdeaconry of Warrington during the past year is one of steady work rather than of startling enterprise. Clergy and laity have pursued the "even tenour ' of their ecclesiastical way, doing their duty with] earnestness and sobriety. Three vicars, well known in the archdeaconry and greatly respected, have resigned their beuefices on receiving preferment in other dioceses—the Revs. Canon W'lllink, IL Gresford Jones, and Thomas Simcox Lea. The Rev. Cyril Bardsley, vicar of St. Ann's, Nottingham, succeeds Canon Willink. Not only for his own sake, but as the nephew of a former archdeacon of Warring- ton (Bishop Bardsley), he will be cordially welcomed amongst us. The trustees of St. Ambrose Church, Widnes, appointed to the vacant benefice a curate from one of our poor Liverpool parishes. For seven years the Rev. H. P. Hiscoke has laboured with great success in the parish of St. Polvcarp's,Everton, and we have no doubt his ministry in Widnes will be equally successful. It is an encouragement to those curates who have borne the" burden and heat of the day" in the heavy city parishes to know that the services of one of their brethren have been recognised by trustees within the diocese. We are sure that patrons and trustees of bene- fices within the diocese of Liverpool will not forget our own hard-working curates when vacancies arise. Two notable events in the history of our diocese will take place this year—the laying of the foundation-stone of our new Cathedral, and the first meeting of the Church Congress since the establishment of the see. The King is expected to lay the foundation-stone some time in summer, but the date has not yet been fixed by his Majesty. In the meantime, we require £16,000 to make up the £200,000 for which we appealed. This sum we expect, will be forthcoming before we proceed with the actual building. The money received up to date is ZlBl,OOO for the building, and £25,500 forspecial objects such as the Chapter House, memorial windows, fout, tta. If the Cathedral testifies to the devotion and liberality of Liverpool Churchmen, the Church Congress will give visible proof of the vitality and moral earnest- ness of the National Church. Whatever else may be said of the Church of England, no one can now fairly say she is indifferent, or timid, or fast asleep, or in- disposed to trouble. THE CHURCH MOVES ON.</p>
        <p>earnestly, enthusiastically, and, on the whole, doingher Master's work at home and abroad in the strength and power of the Holy Ghost. The danger with us all is to take too short views. Compare the Church of England of the twentieth century broadly with the Church of England of the opening years of the nineteenth century, and your verdict must be that if there is less calm,there is more life ; if less peace, more power. There may be the calm and peace of spiritual death. Better by far spiritual life, with its controversies, its crises, and its disturbing eccentricities. Hundreds of the clergy— the clergy of whom we hardly ever hear—are spending their strength in evangelising the poor and tempted in the lower parts of our towns and cities; others are offer- ing their lives in passionate devotion to the evangelis- ing of the heathen. These are the men who are the "soul of the Church"; these and such as these are the "salt" which preserves the " body " from corruption. Ido not—l dare not—minimise our unhappy divisions, but it is possible to undercount, underrate, and under- value the ' faithful and sober and loyal sons of the Church, and the future of the Church is with them. The Church of England is the most tolerant and THE MOST COMPREHENSIVE CHURCH IN CHRISTENDOM.</p>
        <p>Thereinlies her weakness, but, thank God, therein also lies her strength. And, after all, we, who are here to- day, are loyal to our mother Church, because she is a liberty-loving Church; and we would rather be of her, with all her controversies, than be members of a Church where individual opinion is suppressed with a strong hand, and where the scientific spirit is not only sus- pected, but silenced and its advocates cast forth as aliens. Great is our inheritance, great our trust, and magnificent our opportunity. We are the servants of a Church holding a unique position, holding fast by its great heritage from the past, reaching forth with its imperial spirit to the new and vigorgus nations that are springing up in the East and West. Let us not be blind to the changes of time; let us not be slow to seize every opportunity to advance the kingdom of our Lord and 'Saviour at home and abroad; let the Spirit of the Imperial Christ, "the Prince of the kings of the earth," be upon us and our children. Then shall "His way be known upon earth, His saving health among all nations." THE POSITION AND DUTIES OF • CHURCEIWAR DENS.</p>
        <p>The ARCHDEACON then spoke of the position and duties of churchwardens. He said the wardens were charged by the Bishop with the oversight of the parish in respect to the observance of, and obedience to the ecclesiastical laws. In pursuanceiof this authority, the wardens might present to the Bishop any person or persons, clerical or lay, who had committed a breach of such laws. Not to make such presentation to the Bishop when the churchwardens conscientiously believed the law of the Church was being deliberately broken, was a grave dereliction of duty. Wardens had also the right to make a formal complaint to the Bishop about any irregularity in the services of the Church or in the administration of the sacraments, or for any neglect of duty on the part of the vicar of the parish. It was the wardens' duty to observe and report, but not to take any further action. Churchwardens had no right of interference with the vicar or the services; their duty was to present their complaint in due form to the Bishop, upon whom rested the responsibility to act or not to act on the complaint. Strictly speaking, sides- men held office jointly with the churchwardens, and were entitled to an equal voice with them on all ques- tions; but in many parishes this rule was not adhered to, and the sidesmen took only a subordinate position. Of sidesmen, it was, perhaps, correct to say that they might do all that the churchwardens might do, but that they were not usually required to do all that church- wardens must do. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FAall4;'i'lES 44. ND "ORNAMENTS."</head>
        <p>FAall4;'i'lES 44. ND "ORNAMENTS."</p>
        <p>Alluding to faculties and" ornaments," Archdeacon Madden said he had gone carefully over the returns made by most of the rural deans during the past year, and he found that in many of the churches no table of degrees "had been" set up,"atathat in a few churches the Ten Commandments were absent from the east end of the church. Several of the rural deans reported that in their personal visitation of the churches they had found great laxity as regarded the obtaining of faculties for alterations or additions. Many important alterations —e.g., the replacing of a wooden pulpit by. a stone pulpit ;of a simple font by a more elaborate font; of plain choir seats by carved choir stalls—had been made without any faculty having been obtained. In Simmons v. Middleton, August 31, 1898, Chancellor Blofeld, of Norxvich,held that a cross, candlesticks, and flower vases could not be removed from a retable to another part of the church without a faculty. It ought to be known by all wardens that alterations by " removal " required a faculty. In respect of furniture, fittings, decorations, and " ornaments," introduced into a church after conse- cration,he thought there could be no doubt that a faculty was required. It was, therefore, the duty of vicar and wardees to see that none of these things were intro- duced into their church without due legal process.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOSIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOSIETY.</p>
        <p>This Year's Show.</p>
        <p>--- The fifteenth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society will be held at Walton Park on Thursday, August 11th. The arrangements are already in an advanced stage. There will be the usual classes, with increased and additional prizes for cheese, and there will again be the shoeing examination classes for prizes kindly presented by Lady Greenall. The famous band of the Yorkshire Hussars will play selections of music during the day. The catering for the public luncheon and the public bar will this year be in the hands of Mr. A. NV. Paul, of the Griffin Hotel, Warrington. A special marquee will be erected for the use of the public in the event of the weather proving unfavourable,while round a portion of the large ring there will be a raised platform so as to enable visitors to see easily and avoid the discomfort of having to stand on possibly wet grass.An important change has been effected in'regard to the management of the Society's affairs. The old) committee has been succeeded by a Council, one- third of the members of which will retire each year and will not be eligible for immediate re-election. The object is to infuse new blood into the Council each year, and so far as possible secure representation of the various districts within the Society's radius. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart, is again the president, while Col. Hall Walker, M.P., is the vice-president. Mr. H. Beresford Melville, Bold-street, is the secretary, from whom full particulars in regard to the forthcoming show may be obtained. The Society is doing a most useful work in Warrington and the district, and it is to be hoped it will receive at the hands of the public that generous support it so well merits. '</p>
        <p>The Municipal Bowling Green at Latchford. The laying of a municipal bowling green in Victoria Park has been successfully completed under the superintendence of Mr. Parker, the head gardener of the Corporation. There is every likelihood of the green being second to none in the town. It has been carefully laid and the crown of the green is perfect. It is probable that it will be thrown open to the public in June next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>IN AND AROUND WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>IN AND AROUND WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Licensing Bill.</p>
        <p>[BY TIIE FREE CRITIC.]</p>
        <p>The Licensing Bill before Parliament interests Warrington probably more than most towns in Eng- land with the exception perhaps of Burton-on-Trent. Brewing beer from mysterious compounds is quite an industry in Warrington and district and the inhabi- tants as shown by the police courts and by walks about the town in the evening,evidence that too many imbibe more than they can conveniently carry inside them. Even children of 8 and 12 can get drunk in Warrington and those who supply them can hold up their hands in"pious" horr or. I might say in passing that why beer is a mysterious compound is the fact that the brewers have extraordinary latitude allowed them in manufacturing it, as they are not troubled by the Adulteration Acts, and therefore they brew it to suit their own satisfaction. It sometimes suits the stomachs of the customers and sometimes doesn't, but that of mrse is a detail. This is a glorious free country, especially for brewers. Now this Licensing Bill is about the coolest piece of pretended legislation that has been brought before the notioe of the public for a long time. First of all, when a license is granted in Warrington it generally means putting at least £l,OOO into tbe pockets of the owner of the house—sometimes it has reached as high a figure as £5,000. In Liverpool Sir Thomas Hughes, the Chairman of the Licensing Committee, has just declared that the value of giving a licence there is as much as £15,000. Now, as I am not a teetotaler, I cannot be accused of being unduly biassed against the Brewing Trade. Yet I am prepared to say that if all the public-houses in the United Kingdom were closed to-morrow and, of course, for the next twenty years it would be one of the finest things that could happen for the welfare of all of us. If Parliament would set about trying to do something in the direction of converting the public- houses from drinking houses into refreshment houses, instead of bringing in a Bill to compensate the owners, they would be committing a wise act of legislation. Fancy the owners of public-houses asking for compen- sation if a licence is extinguished on the ground that it is not required, or on moral and other reasonable grounds. I put it to anyone. Is there any other trade which is so profitable to all those who are engaged in it, and yet is the most expensive and ruinous to the rate- payers and inhabitants in general? Should we require half our police force, prisons, workhouses, and lunatic asylums, if it were not for this licensed trade ? Would, therefore, in consequence of the expenses of these institueons being kept up, our rates be nearly so high as they are? Of course not. And yet the trade can cry out for compensation. Who is it that ought to be compensated? The license owners or the general public? What do reason and common- sense say4on these points? Carlyle once pithily said in speaking about the United Kingdom, that it con- tained 40,000,000 inhabitants, mostly fools. If this country is mad enough to agree to pass any Bill to compensate licenseholders some will consider Carlyle's description not far off the mark. Of course others will say. Oh! but it is intended that the trade shall compensate themselves. How beautiful is the guile and the simplicity thereof. Don't you think that the trade will take good care that you and I pay for it in the long run? The danger in the Bill becoming law is, that the "Trade " is so strong and the public so apathetic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON PHOTOGLZAPHIZ SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PHOTOGLZAPHIZ SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Exhibition of Pictorial Photographs.</p>
        <p>There is at present on view in the Old Academy an interesting exhibition of pictorial photographs, the work for the most part, of members of the Warrington Photographic Society. The exhibition was opened on Tuesday afternoon and will close this (Saturday) even- ing. Admission is free and we should advise those who have not yet availed themselves of the opportunity of seeing the photographs to do so to-day, as the work exhibited is highly meritorious and in every way credit- able to the members of the Warrington Photographic Society. The exhibition was opened on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. Alderman Bennett, in the unavoidable absence of Mr. John Fairhurst, the president of the societr Whilst not a photographer himself, Alderman Bennett said he could appreciate the beauty of the pictures which adorned the walls of that Old Academy and he must congratulate the members of the Photographic Society upon the great advance which they had made since their first exhibition, a year ago. (Hear, hear.) On behalf of the executive of the Warrington Society he wished to express the pleasure which the members felt in having associated with them a society of such an interesting and artistic character. He trusted that the progress which had been already made would be con- tinuous and that in due time the rooms would be found too small to accommodate the exhibits. (Hear, hear.) The services of Aldermanßennett were acknowledged on the proposition of Mr. E. J. HALL, seconded by Mr. F. W. PURSE. Mr. PURSE also expressed the thanks of the members to the gentlemen who had so kindly officiated as judges—Messrs. S. L. Coulthurst and C. J. Harrison of Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Pictures Reviewed.</head>
        <p>The Pictures Reviewed.</p>
        <p>No one could visit the Exhibition without being com- pelled to admit thrt it is a credit to the Society,forithe improvement in the quality of the work compared with the first Exhition i year ago s really remarkable. And this is not by any means due to the introduction of an open Competition class, for the members of the Society are themselves in the forefront with some excellent pictures, and have succeeded in annexing the bulk of the awards. The principal class is, of course, "land- scape or seascape," and udder this comprehensive section there are nearly a hundred frames, inTly of them con- taining very choice pictures. Premier honours in this class go so far away as Plymouth. Mr. William CLayden, of that town, has only one exhibit, but the judges have unhesitatingly placed it first, and most people will agree with their verdict. "A misty morn" is the title, and the scene a harbour with shipping in the distance, and one schooner boldly sailing in. One can, . in imagination, conjure up the breeze, the ripple on water, and the mist slowly dispersing as the sun rises higher in the sky, so realistic and natural is the photographer's treatment of his theme. The second award falls to Mr. H. Bond, whose" Royal "and "Northern "exhibition picture of Oakmere, Delamere, has caught the eye of the judges at Warrington. It is- a pleasing and restful little view suggesting eventide in summer. Mr. Bond has a number of excellent land scapes on the walls,but, for reasons that will be obvious to most of our readers, we shall not refer to them in detail. Mr. P. J. Frith's " Seashore at Nevin" presents this usually serene and bright little place in a gloomy atmosphere, but the picture, with its sombre sea and clouds, and" The rivals"looming up in the distance is an excellent composition. "The Langdales," by Mr. H. R. Lacy, St. Helens, is another fine picture with rich cloud effects and striking mountain slops, but a rather obstrusive and park-like pathway in the foreground detracts considerably. 'A number of winter scenes are hung, quite the best being one by Mr. J. E. Latham (Southport),who has rendered the gleam of a wintry sun and soft shadows on the snow in a tastdal manner. Mr. W. H. Podmore is well represented,his "Golden Harvest" being thebest of a numerous collection and, too,akead of several somewhat similar compositions on the walls." The Quarry Team" by Mr. J. Newton, is a bromide enlarge- ment full of vigour amd action, while one of the sweetest pictures.," Water Lilies," by F. R. Whitby, is not for competition. Mr. Birrel has a bright little print of the terrace steps, ,Haddon Hall, aad Mr. F. Gar- side one or two small pictures, the merit of which is undoubtedly detracted from by over-framing. In portraiture and figure studies, the members of the War- rington Photographic Society hardly distinguish them- selves, and this class falls far below the landscape sec- tion in the auality of the exhibits. There are, nevertheless, a few very good studies indeed. Mr. H. Bond secures first award for "An Artist's Model " and there qan be no question that this artistic head and ' shoulders is far and away the best in its class. Mr. Bond has been fortunate in his model, but the picture is hill of good technical work. Mr. K. F. Bishop has made great endeavours in figure studies, and is to be Warmly congratulated on his clever and original work. He has well earned an award for "And that's the way the money goes," a street scene very true to life, and with each figure admir- ably posed. Messrs. Nicholls and Kertland also show creditable work? Architecture is a representative section, embodying a quantity of very careful work. First awards go to Mr. A. H. Hughes (Llandudno) for a , view in Tewkesbury Abbey-_a difficult !subject, combining harmoniously the strongest illumination with the deepest shadow. Mr. J. V. Moore has the best "Val Crucis Abbey " front. in toned bromide we have yet seen, while 11r.r. Nicholls is to th fore with a similarly toned print of the Choir Stalls, Chester Ckhedrhl. There are two views of an old stairway in Haddon Hall, that of Mr. W. H. P.odmore's being the brighter and better, though Mr. Buret runs him close. The latter has also tin evenly-toned print of the organ pedestal in Chester Cathedral. The.judges were Messrs. Coulthurst and Harrison, of IVlanchester,and they are to be warmly congratulated on the general approbation they have themselves won in placing the awards.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES</p>
        <p>FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search Lights on Land, Labour j and Social Problems."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MANCHESTER LABOUR</head>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER LABOUR</p>
        <p>REGISTRY.</p>
        <p>"The misery and demoralisation caused by acute poverty and loss of employment can scarcely. be exaggerated. The working character of an efficient and self-respecting workman is a put of the social mechanism which disuse rapidly causes to deteriorate." —" Manchester Guardian."</p>
        <p>Franklin, in his "Poor Richard's Maxims," tells us that"it is .the used tool that is always sharp and bright," and "it is the tool that lies idle that is blunt and rusty." And what is true of men's tools is equally true of their brains and their talents. Ifa man ceases to think and work he will soon lose both the desire and the ability to think and work.</p>
        <p>To me the moral aspect of poverty is more disastrous to men than the physical. If a man has been out of employment for weeks and months, and has borrowed and pawned and sold all he can, you can give him food, and you may repair his physical losses. But you can- not bring back his love of work, and his ability to manage his own affairs, and the hope, and confidence,and efficiency he has lost,. I.—THE MANCHESTER LABOUR REGISTRY WAS ESTABLISHED TO DEAL WITH EXCEPTIONAL POVERTY, And the story of its origin and growth is a good object lesson to every student of our complicated labour problems. The aftermath of the war, and our reckless expenditure, and our heavy taxation, have produced their natural and inevitable results in commercial depression, and lack of enyloyment, during the last year or two; and pinching poverty and hard times are making some people think who never thought before. A merciful providence has wisely decreed that "what- soever a man soweth that shall he also reap," and it is only fair that those who shouted for war, and voted for war, should pay] for it in empty pockets and pinched bellies. The mischief of it is that the curse falls on everybody,and punishes us all round,whether we deserve it or not. _</p>
        <p>But Manchester has had worse luck than some other towns I could name. A gang of reckless cotton gamblers have cornered her raw material, and rigged the market, and played the mischief with her trade. Then she has been spending money with a lavish hand, in improving the condition of the city. She has re-laid all her tram lines, and supplied them with electricity. She has found a pile of money to extend and improve the Ship Canal, and to purchase and lay out new parks. and pleasure grounds. All these great public works brought into the city hundreds of workmen, and their families, from all parts of the kingdom. These people found re- mnnerattve employment as long as the jobs lasted, and when they came to an end these migratory labourers should have taken themselves off, like the swallows to another big job elsewhere. But they took no thought for the morrow. _ . _</p>
        <p>They saw these jobs finished one after another, and then fondly imagined that the Manchester Corporation would spend a few more millions of somebody's money, in finding them more work, or that employment would drop down from Fle.aven, like manna in the wilderness. But these things did not happen, so these thoughtless improvident ones joined the ranks of the unemployed, and left us to do their thinking for them. IL—MANCHESTER HAS NO MONEY TO THROW</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>AWAY.</head>
        <p>AWAY.</p>
        <p>She has come to the end of her most ambitious schemes, and she has a congested labour market, and she does not see her way out of her difficulties. The Lord Mayor in addressing 9, deputation of the unemployed said: "It is not possible to hold oat hope of employ- ment for any considerable number of men. It is with difficulty that we can find work for our regular men,and we have no large works in prospect. It is better that these facts should be known and stated, than that pros- pects of work should be held out, that are likely to prove delusive." It has been suggested that the Corporation should borrow some more money, and spend it in some other great undertaking, to find employment far the people at the ratepayers' expense. But I strongly object to any municipalisation of the labour market that would interfere with the commercial value of any man's labour. I believe, as strongly as Carlyle himself, in "a fair day's wages for a fair day's work," but I also believe in 'a fair day's work for a fair day's wages." I want justice all round, and I would pay every man neither more or less than he earns.</p>
        <p>elief works, and municipal employment schemes usually give remuneration below the standard rate of wages, and thus indirectly impoverish the men outside by lowering th..ir wages. Sometimes they put square men into round holes, and set men to tasks for which they are utterly unfitted, and where they cannot earn their salt. So the ratepayerslmoney is absolutely wasted and the rates are increased, or more money must be borrowed.</p>
        <p>What would be the effect of allowing all our unem- ployed to quarter themselves on the rates, and demand six shillings a day when they could not earn three? Bankruptcy for the municipality or the State would be the end of that game. Our friends in Australia, and New Zealand, are living after this fashion, on borrowed money, and when they get to the end of their credit, they will have to starve. Ido not believe in mortgag- ing the future, and leaving posterity to pay my debts. It is not honest, and nobody but a rogue, or a fool would advocate such a policy. _ _</p>
        <p>But how does State help for the unemployed affect the individual? It teaches him to lean on other people till he cannot stand alone. He pets his thinking out, till he loses the power to think and reason altogether. The very worst advice you can give to any man, is to teach him to rely upon other people, for the supply of his needs. You sap his manhood, and destroy all initia- tive, and enterprise, and you make him a blind, un- reasoning, helpless machine, or a slave to some stronger will. _ _ _</p>
        <p>And if he gets callous, and indifferent to his circum- stances, and surroundings, and loses all desire, or hope for better things, no power on earth can save him. You may throw him a life-line, but he will neither grasp it, nor hold it. You must either risk your own life in saving him, or you must leave him to perish. lII.—YOU MAY HELP MEN WHO TRY TO HELP THEMSELVES.</p>
        <p>If a man is able to work, and willing to work, the kindest thing you can do to him is to help him to find a suitable berth at a fair wage, and this is what the Manchester Labour Registry has been trying to do ever since it was opened last December. I have a copy of the report of this scheme for the first three months of its existence, and the facts and figures it gives are well worth yeur serious study. Any bona-fide resident in the city, who wanted employment was invited to go to the Town Hall,and enter his name and address, and qualific- ations, in a book kept for that purpose. The police would make enquiries about his character, habits, and necessities, and, if he proved honest and trustworthy, they would recommend him to employers who were in need of workmen.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor caused all exceptional cases of poverty and distress to be -reported to him, and the ProVident Society.and other charities, cave tickets for food, coals, medicine and blankets. More than three thousand five hundred people received relief in this way, and when employment was found for men in dis- tant towns their railway fares were paid by the same charities.</p>
        <p>In studying the report of this registry, I am struck with the small number of applications for employment/. The register for men and boys was opened about last Christmas, and in the following fifteen weeks, only 5,802 names were entered. The register for women and girls had only been opened three weeks when the report was issued, and it contained 353 names. But Manchester has a population of nearly six hun- dred thousand souls, and vet there were only 6,155 of them who took the trouble to register their names in three months. These applicants only represent about one per vent, of the entire population, and they do not include criminals, or bad characters, known to the p'iiice. Now let us see what kind of applicants entered their names among the ranks of the unemployed, and how they fared? In every labour crisis, as a rule, it is the weakest that goes to the -.volt, and the untrained, and unskilled, and unorganised workers, usually fare the Worst. There are over three thousand names on the books to-day, and there are hardly any skilled artisans among them. Of the three hundred and fifty three women and girls out of employment, two hundred and nineteen of them are "charwomen, and office cleaners, and attendants." More than half the men and boys on the books are "general labourers, and carters.and horse- keepers." These -are types of workpeople who are thrown on their beam ends by every labour crisis, and who have no resources in themselves. They never think for themselves, and they are all their lives in the habit of doing As they are told, and asking no questions. They are the first to go down, and if you pick them up, they can no more stand alone, than an empty sack can stand on end.</p>
        <p>ggestive. have These figures, to me, are very su spent many of the hest years of my life in floating human wrecks, end liftjn up the fallen, and putting crooked things straight, and I know that you can never get more out of arty man than you have put into him. If you give him no education, and teach hun no trade, he will be a "hewer of wood "and a drawer of water all his life. If he goes into the world's labour market without brains, and eyes, and hands that have been trained to useful work, he will have to take the job that nobody else will have. Anothersuggestive item in the report is the fact that one hundred and forty-nine ern ployers came to the</p>
