      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MEMORIAL TO FALLEN SOLDIERS</head>
        <p>THE MEMORIAL TO FALLEN SOLDIERS</p>
        <p>Another Appeal for Funds.</p>
        <p>The movement to raise funds on behalf of the pro-- posed memorial to the men of the South Lancashire Regiment who fell in the South African war is makinir progress, though a more general response to the appeal of the Workingmen's Committee, which represented, works in the town and neighbourhood, is hoped for. Arrangements have been made at many works whereby' the collections of workmen's donations, however small, may be spread over a series of weeks, and no' doubt this example will be followed by other local employees who are anxious to contribute their ".mite" towards a fitting memorial to the men who- died in their country's service. A further meeting of the Workingmen's Committee was held at the Town, Hall on Thursday evening under the presidency of Mr.: W. Haigh (the vice-chairman). There was a moderate- attendance, including Mr. C. J. Newton, hon. secretary, and Mr. H. Hope (hon. treasurer). The CHAIRMAN, whilst regretting that the assembly was not larger, said they were living in hopes that the cause would be taken more to heart and that. there would be a more ready response to the appeal that was. being made. It was intimated by the SECRETARY that the Mayor had expressed his willingness to become presidint of the Al r.Newton pointed out that th had been' present at the meetings held up-to-date 26 representa- tives of firms and 3 private individuals who were taking anjinterest in the work. Some firms had not been reported very often, and he would suggest that a circular be forwarded to them, describing the system of collec- tions which was being adopted at other works. Fifteen firms were not yet represented, but the was sure their support could be enlisted if a special effart were made. The CHAIRMAN said he had interviewed Mr. P. Win- skill, of Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, but he was told that times were so bad with the employees at the mill that there was very little hope of a donation being obtained from them. He had also brought the matter before. representatives of Messrs. W. D. Houghton and C 0.,. who had [promised to consult the employees with a view to the appointment of a delegate. It was not expected, that the employees of Messrs. W. Smith and Sons,. Dallam-lane, would be able to contribute, owing to the state of trade; the works being almost at a stand- still. It was probable that Messrs. Greenings would be represented on the committee. At the last meeting of the Co-operative Society he suggested that a grant. should be made to the fund. He believed that if the Secretary wrote to the Board of Management a dona- tion would be forthcoming. It was stated that the employees of Messrs. W. Smith. and Sans had held a meeting and decided not to take part in the movement. THE ADMINISTRATION OF THE TRANSVAAL RELIEF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-04-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FUND.</head>
        <p>FUND.</p>
        <p>i At a previous meeting t was stated that there was-- some dissatisfaction among the employees of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., as to the method of administration of this fund to the widows and orphans of soldiers who fell in the war. Mr. Hope now desired it to be known that a deputation from the works had waited upon the borough troasurer, and, instead of 'lading fault with - the system, they returned full of admiration of the way in which the money was being distributed. They believed the result of the deputation would be that the - workmen would subscribe more readily to the memorial fund.</p>
        <p>Mr. OSBORNE, a representative of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company, said groat credit was due to the • borough treasurer and his staff for working so hard to• ensure the proper administration of the fund. The workmen previously had the idea that £1,300 was lying idle at the Town Hall, but they were now thoroughly satisfied that the money was being properly distributed. Although trade was very bad, they would do all in their- power to help the fund, and he had brought a donation, that night. It was proposed to deduct a penny a week from the wages of men earning under 30s. and 3d. a week from employees earning over that amount. The SECRETARY said the Mayor's fund now totalledl, a little over £6OO. He had not yet received any inti- mation of a workingmen's effort being made in Widnes, and St. Helens.</p>
        <p>It was decided to make another appeal to the em- ployees at the works which are not yet represented or have not sent delegates regularly.</p>
