        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHTING IN WARRINGTON Last Year's Profit. _ The Local Government Board Inquiry held at the Town Hall on Tuesday morning passed off very quietly in marked contrast to many inquiries held in recent years. Mr. M. li—lslorth, M.I.C.E. conducted the in- quiry, and it had reference to an application by the Corporation for sanction to borrow £5,000 for electri- city purposes. There was only a small attendance. Amongst those who were present were: Alderman Smethurst, Mr. H. Roberts, J.P., Councillor Ellison, Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), F. V. L. Mathias (electrical engineer), J. Deas (water engineer). 1 The Town Clerk, in stating the case for the Corpora- tion, said the population of the Bormigh at the last census was 64,242,and the assessable value £224,575. The outstanding loans for sanitary purposes amounted to £266,158. The Inspector: What period do you want for the re- payment of the loan? The Borough Treasurer: Thelongest possible period because we want to make both ends meet. The Town Clerk said the total rates of the Borough were 7s. 10d. in the £, and there had been an abnormal increase owing to education, which was responsible for an additional rate of 10d. in the £. The amount sanctioned by the Board in a previous application for buildings in connection with the electricity de- partment was £13,800 and that sum had been exceeded by £836 10s. 11d, which was included in the present application. With regard to mains, the Corporation were now asking for sanction to the spending of £3,030 98.2 d. This amount had already been spent, and £l,OOO in addition were required to meet the expenditure on mains during the ensuing twelve months. All the mains had been laid in the compulsory area. In the present application an item was also included of £ll9 16s. id. for public lighting. This was in excess of the sum of 1900 sanctioned by the Board for public lighting and was caused chiefly by the removal of lamps, necessitated by the tramways. Mr. F. V. L. Mathias electrical engineer went into details respecting the expenditure. Most of the money, he said, was required to meet the cost of laying new cables. Mr. J. Fairhurst, borough treasurer, gave evidence a to the financial position of the undertaking. Last years he said, the gross profit on the electricity department, was £4,433 Os. 10d., and the net profit, after meeting sinking fund and interest on loans, was £154 15s. 3d. This sum had gone towards the reduction of the debit balance which had been carried forward from previous years, and which amounted to £495 Bs. 3d. After de- ducting this year's net profit, there was still a debit balance owing on the electricity department amounting to £340 13s. Asked by the Inspector as to the result of the working of the tramways, Mr. Fairhurst said there had been a small loss. The Inspector: The last time I was here there was an !overdraft at the Bank amounting to £60,000. What is it now? The Borough Treasurer: We have now reduced the hank overdraft to .£3,238. This concluded the inquiry. The Inspector then proceeded to inspect the Electric- ty Station at Howley.</p>
