        <p>PEEK, FREAN &amp; CO.'S " VANESSA " (Regd.) BISCUITS'</p>
        <p>registry, and looked through the list of applicants, and! could not find hands that would suit them. There was a time when any man with a full supply of limbs, and who was "strong in the arm, and weak in the head,'' could find a job of some sort. But that day is gone by, • and the workman who has nothing to sell in the labour • market, but his physical strength,will not get much for his wares. More than half the applicants were "on the rocks," and starving, and their families were in a very bad way. - These received tickets from the charities, that afforded, temporary relief. Nearly one third of them, helped themselves by finding work elsewhere, or going back to. the places from whence they came, and their Aames were. taken off the books. Six hundred and sixty-eight of them obtained situations through this agency, and as it becomes better known to both employers and Work- people, its ssefulness will be vastly extended. There are still more than three thousand names on the books, and two hundred and twenty three of these are "clerks, and warehousemen." This is a sure sign of acute commerical depressions and if these unfortunate clerks cannot earn their living in any other way, I fear their prospects are not very brilliant. The Manchester Registry is an experiment that ought-. to be tried in all our large centres of population, and if it were run on wise, sound, business lines, and not as a charity, it ought to do a vast amount of good. It is, well calculated to help the men and women who deserve to be helped, and who try to help themselves, and who" have a real desire to regain a decent position in life. But for those who are without hope, or effort, and who expect other people to do everything they need, it will be a failure. These frosted failures should be handed over to the Salvation Army, or to some lablur for discipline, and training. colony'</p>
